t three years, for \nproviding safe drinking water in rural areas under the National Rural drinking w ater \nProgramme (NRdwP) and the districts in which it is being implemented;\n(b) number of villages in rural areas of the state not having access to drinking \nwater facilities, district-wise;\n(c) whether there is any provision for keeping some water resources, as reserves, \nunder NRdwP to tackle a drought situation; and\n(d) details of other steps being taken to ensure adequate supply of safe drinking \nwater throughout the year and if so, details thereof?\nTHe MINIsTeR of dRINKINg wATeR ANd sANITATIoN (susHRI uMA \nBHARATI): (a) and (b) The amount allocated and released to Maharashtra under \nNational Rural drinking w ater Programme (NRdwP) during last three years for \nproviding safe drinking water in rural areas is as under:–\n(` in crore)\nyear Allocation Release\n2015-16 344.16 330.88\n2016-17 413.57 404.45\n2017-18 338.13 282.47\nunder National Rural drinking w ater Programme (NRdwP), coverage of drinking \nwater is maintained in terms of fully Covered habitations [i.e. getting more than  \n40 litre per capita per day (lpcd) safe drinking water)], Partially Covered habitations \n(i.e.\t getting\t less\t than\t 40\t lpcd\t safe\t drinking\t water)\t and\t Quality\t Affected\t habitations\t\n(i.e. likely to be getting drinking water from unsafe sources). NRdwP is being \nimplemented in entire s tate. As per the information furnished by the s tate of \nMaharashtra on Integrated Management Information system (IMIs) of the Ministry, \n9.95% of rural habitations are Partially Covered and 0.9% of rural habitations are \nQuality\t Affected\t in\t the\t State.\t The\t district-wise\t details\t of\t habitations\t covered\t under\t\nNRdwP in the state of Maharashtra are given in the statement (See below).\n(c) As reported by state government of Maharashtra, the state has the policy to \nreserve the water resources for drinking water purpose to tackle the drought condition. \n\nWritten Answers to [11 february, 2019]   Starred Questions 75\nfurther, under scarcity condition, the power has been delegated to district Collector \nfor requisition of private drinking water sources such as wells and bore wells. In \ncase of drought situation, following measures are taken to provide adequate drinking \nwater in rural areas:–\n(i) digging new bore wells\n(ii) Hand pumps repairs\n(iii) Requisition of wells and bore wells\n(iv) deepening of wells\n(v) sanction of new temporary piped water supply schemes\n(vi) Pws special repairs\n(vii) w ater supply through tanker\nIn addition to this, the states can use flexi funds to the extent of 25% of the \ntotal funds released to them under NRdwP for taking up works for mitigating the \ndrought situation in an area as per the instructions issued by the Ministry in this \nregard.\n(d) Rural drinking water supply is a state subject. It is the state governments \nwho plan, design, approve, execute and operate and maintain the schemes for providing \nsafe drinking water to rural population. However, this Ministry provides technical \nand\t financial\t assistance\t to\t States\t under\t NRDWP\t for\t improving\t the\t coverage\t of\t safe\t\ndrinking water in rural areas. further, this Ministry has restructured NRdwP to make \nit more competitive, outcome based and result oriented wherein focus of the Ministry \nis\t on\t providing\t Piped\t Water\t Supply\t to\t every\t rural\t household\t which\t aimed\t to\t fulfill\t\nthe commitment made by the country in the international forum while agreeing for \nuN sustainable development goal (sdg)-2030. This Ministry has launched ‘swajal’ \nprogramme, a community demand driven, single village, preferably solar Powered \npiped\t water\t supply\t schemes\t in\t Aspirational\t districts\t identified\t by\t National\t Institution\t\nfor Transforming India (NITI) Aayog which will help in increasing the outreach of \ndrinking water in underserved rural areas.\n\n76 Wr