t plans to connect 10,000 gPs in \nPhase-2, when only 1200 gPs have been connected in Phase-I; and\n(e) by when Ministry would ensure that all gPs are connected under BharatNet \nin Andhra Pradesh?\nTHe MINIsTeR of PANCHA yATI RAJ (sHRI NAReNdRA sINgH T oMAR): \n(a) BharatNet project is being implemented by department of Telecommunications in \na phased manner to create network to connect all the gram Panchayats (approximately \n2.5 lakh) with broadband in the country. The aims and objectives of BharatNet projects \nare (i) to provide high speed digital connectivity to Rural India at affordable price; \n(ii) to make available the Broadband Infrastructure created under the project in a \n\n78 Written Answers to [RAJyA sABHA] Starred Questions\nnon-discriminatory manner for provision of services; (iii) to facilitate proliferation of \nbroadband services in rural areas so as to foster socio-economic development in line \nwith the vision of ‘digital India’ programme which has been launched by government \nof India. The responsibility of utilization is primarily with state government/Telecom \nservice Providers/Internet service Providers.\nModified\t strategy\t for\t implementing\t BharatNet:\t On\t 19.07.2017,\t the\t Government\t\nof\t India\t approved\t a\t modified\t strategy\t for\t implementing\t BharatNet\t in\t three\t Phases:–\nPhase-I: 1,00,000 gram Panchayats (gPs) to be implemented by 3 CPses \nnamely Bharat sanchar Nigam limited (BsNl), RailTel and Power \ngrid Corporation of India limited (PgCIl). This Phase has been \ncompleted in december, 2017.\nPhase-II: The remaining 1,50,000 gPs (approximately) are targeted to be \nimplemented by March, 2019.\nPhase-III: To upgrade the network to meet the future requirements.\n(b) to (d) The department of Telecommunications has informed that in Andhra \nPradesh under BharatNet Phase-I, out of total 2288 (approximately) g Ps of 2 \ndistricts namely Chittoor and v isakhapatnam, about 1674 gPs (including 14 gPs \nof v isakhapatnam already made service ready in a separate pilot project) have been \ntaken up for providing broadband connectivity through PgCIl. As on 03.02.2019, a \ntotal of 1303 gPs have been made service ready and the remaining gPs are under \nimplementation.\nunder BharatNet Phase-II, broadband connectivity is being provided to the balance \n614 gPs of Chittoor and v isakhapatnam (which were not taken up in Phase-I for \nconnectivity) and all the gPs of other districts under state-led model through the \ngovernment of Andhra Pradesh. The state government has issued the letter of Intent \nfor implementation of Phase-II of the BharatNet project.\n(e) The department of Telecommunications has informed that the BharatNet \nproject is targeted to be completed by March, 2019.\nPradhan Mantri Gramodaya Yojana\n*88. sHRI P. BHATTACHAR yA: w ill the Minister of RuRAl  develoPMeNT \nbe pleased to state:\n(a) the district-wise and the constituency-wise amount allocated to various states \nunder the Pradhan Mantri gramodaya yojana and the Pradhan Mantri Rozgar yojana \nduring the last two years particularly in w est Bengal;\n\nWritten Answers to [11 february, 2019]   Starred Questions 79\n(b) the details of the work-wise amount allocated together with the works for \nwhich the same have been allocated out of the said amount; and\n(c) the details of amount approved and spent on roads, district-wise?\nTHe MINIsTeR of RuRAl  develoPMeNT (sHRI NAReNdRA sINgH \nToMAR): (a) to (c) Pradhan Mantri gramodaya yojana (PMgy) was launched by \nthe Ministry of Rural development during the year 2000-2001. The scheme was \ntransferred to the then Planning Commission in the year 2002-2003. As per the \ninformation received from the National Institution for Transforming India (NITI) \nAayog, the scheme has been discontinued from the year 2005-06.\nPradhan Mantri Rozgar yojana (PMR y) has ceased to exist. Prime Minister's \nemployment generation Programme (PMegP) scheme has been created by merging \nRural e mployment generation Programme (RegP) and Pradhan Mantri Rozgar yojana \n(PMRy) schemes and is being implemented by the Ministry of Micro, small and \nMedium enterprises.\nPrime Minister's employment generation Programme (PMegP) is a credit linked \nsubsidy scheme, for setting up of new micro-enterprises and to generate employment \nopportunities in rural as well as urban areas of the country through Khadi and v illage \nIndustries Commission (KvIC), state Khadi and v illage Industries Board (KvIB) and \nDistrict\t Industries\t Centre\t (DIC).\t General\t category\t beneficiaries\t can\t avail\t of\t margin\t\nmoney subsidy of 25% of the project cost in rural areas and 15% in urban areas. \nFor\t beneficiaries\tbelonging\t to\t special\t categories\t such\t as\t SC/ST/Women/PH/Minorities/\nex-servicemen/NeR, the margin money subsidy is 35% in rural areas and 25% in \nurban areas. The maximum cost of projects is ` 25 lakh in the manufacturing sector \nand ` 10 lakh in the service sector.\nThe state-wise position of PMegP scheme during the last two years and current \nyear is given in the statement.\n\n80 Written Answers to [RAJyA sABHA] Starred Questions\nStatement\nState-wise position of PMEGP scheme during the last two years \nand current year\n(A) state-wise details of Prime Minister's employment generation Programme (PMegP) \nscheme for fy  2016-17\nsl. \nNo.\nstate/uT Margin \nmoney \nannual \ntargets \n(` in lakh)\nMargin \nmoney \nsubsidy \nutilized#\n(` in lakh)\nNumber \nof projects \nassisted\nestimated \nemployment \ngenerated\n(No. of\npersons)\n1 2 3 4 5 6\n1. Jammu and Kashmir 3541.26 2621.40 1492 11691\n2. Himachal Pradesh 1970.1 2185.27 941 6916\n3. Punjab 3504.09 3181.60 1266 9858\n4. uT Chandigarh 100 82.84 47 376\n5. uttarakhand 2140.93 2122.33 1345 9890\n6. Haryana 3371.31 3383.53 1377 11016\n7. delhi 300 182.41 119 952\n8. Rajasthan 5500.99 4641.6 1749 13408\n9. uttar Pradesh 12981.52 14271.05 4074 36315\n10. Bihar 6909.77 8336.51 3234