46. Andhra Pradesh v isakhapatnam 9 17 73 -\nNA: no monitoring station in the city.\n@ monitoring station sanctioned but not yet operational.\n‘-‘\t data\t not\t received,\t National\t Ambient\t Air\t Quality\t Standard\t (NAAQS)\t for\t Residential,\t Industrial,\t Rural\t\nand others Areas (Annual average) for so\n2 = 50 µg/m 3, No 2 = 40 µg/m 3, PM 10 = 60 µg/m 3 and \nPM2.5 = 40 µg/m 3 and so 2 = 20 µg/m 3, No 2 = 30 µg/m 3, PM 10 = 60 µg/m 3 and PM 2.5 = 40 µg/m 3 \nfor ecologically sensitive area. The data furnished in the table for year 2017 is as available on date.\nWRITTEN ANSWERS TO UNSTARRED QUESTIONS\nProgress in production of Tejas Aircraft\n763. dR. R. lAKsHMANAN: w ill the Minister of defeNCe be pleased to state:\n(a) whether government is aware of the fact that there is inordinate delay in \nproduction and induction of Tejas light Combat Aircraft in Indian Air force (IAf);\n(b) if so, the details thereof;\n(c) whether government has taken any step to expedite the process of inducting \nTejas light Combat Aircraft into IAf as soon as possible; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of defeNCe (dR. suBHAsH \nRAMRAo BHAMRe): (a) and (b) Two contracts were signed between Indian Air \nforce (IAf) and Hindustan Aeronautics ltd., (HAl) for procurement of (i) 20 Initial \nOperational\t Clearance\t (IOC)\t standard\t aircraft\t (16\t IOC\t fighters\tand\t 4\t IOC\t trainers)\t on\t\n31st March, 2006 to be completed by december, 2011 and (ii) 20 final operational \nClearance\t (FOC)\t standard\t aircraft\t (16\t FOC\t fighters\t and\t 4\t FOC\t trainers)\t on\t 23rd\t\ndecember, 2010 to be completed by december, 2016.\n\nWritten Answers to [11 february, 2019]   Unstarred Questions 93\n At the time of placement of IoC order in 2006, it was expected that Aeronautical \ndevelopment Agency (AdA), who is the Programme Management Agency will obtain \nIoC in 2008 after which delivery was planned till december, 2011. However, IoC \nhas\t been\t achieved\t by\t ADA\t in\t December,\t 2013\t that\t too\t only\t for\t 16\t fighter\t version\t\nIOC\t aircraft,\t after\t which\t only,\t HAL\t could\t take\t up\t production\t and\t first\t IOC\t fighter\t\naircraft\t was\t delivered\t in\t 2016-17.\t Till\t date,\t out\t of\t total\t 16\t IOC\t fighter\t aircraft,\t \n12\t fighters\thave\tbeen\tdelivered\tto\t IAF.\tHAL\t plans\tto\t deliver\tthe\t balance\t4\t IOC\t fighter\t\naircrafts by March, 2019. AdA has handed over drawing Applicability list (dAl) \nand\t Equipment\t Standard\t of\t Preparation\t (SOP)\t documents,\t for\t FOC\t configuration\t to\t\nHAl  on 31st december, 2018 duly cleared by centre of Military Airworthiness and \nCertification\t (CEMILAC)\t to\t go\t ahead\t with\t the\t production\t of\t FOC\t fighter\t aircraft.\t\nAccordingly\t HAL\t has\t taken\t up\t the\t production\t of\t LCA,\t FOC\t fighter\t aircraft.\nThe production of 8 trainer aircraft (4 each in IoC and foC contracts) would \nbe taken up only after design clearance from AdA.\n(c) and (d) yes, sir. In order to fast track the delivery of lCA Tejas, the \nfollowing steps have been taken:–\n ● for ramping up production capacity from existing 8 aircraft to 16 aircraft \nper annum, government of India (goI) has sanctioned ` 1381.04 crore \nin March, 2017.\n ● establishment of second line for structural and equipping activities at \nAircraft division, HAl.\n ● Increased the number of jigs for front fuselage, Centre fuselage, Rear \nfuselage, w ings and various sub-assemblies and established contracts \nfor outsourcing to private partners.\n ● Reduction in manufacturing cycle time through improved supply chain \nmanagement, learning and augmentation of manpower.\nExpansion of NCC  activities to Schools and Colleges\n764. sHRI sAMBHAJI CHHATRAPATI: w ill the Minister of defeNCe be \npleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that the activities of National Cadet Corps (NCC) \nhas shrunk over the years, because of limited resources, despite the fact that it is \nrecognized as second line of defence in India;\n(b) if so, the details thereof including the number of schools and colleges where \nNCC has its visible presence;\n\n94 Written Answers to [RAJyA sABHA] Unstarred Questions\n(c) whether government has any plan to expand NCC activities to cover at \nleast all government controlled and aided schools and colleges; and\n(d) whether there is any proposal to make NCC compulsory for the students of \nschools and colleges and if not, the reasons therefor?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of defe NCe (dR. suBHAsH \nRAMRAo BHAMRe): (a) No, sir.\n(b) details of National Cadet Corps (NCC) in the country is as below:–\nInstitutions government Private Total\nschools 9340 3205 12545\nColleges 3906 923 4829\nToT al 13246 4128 17374\nThere\t has\t been\t a\t significant\t increase\t in\t the\t spectrum\t of\t activities\t undertaken\t\nby\t NCC\t especially\t in\t the\t field\t of\t social\t service\t and\t community\t development.\t NCC\t\nis actively participating in swachhta Abhiyan, AIds Awareness, digital l iteracy, \nImmunization, Tree Plantation, Anti-drug Campaign, w ater Conservation measures, etc.\n(c) and (d) The expansion of NCC has been planned from 13 lakh to 15 lakh \ncadets in a phased manner from 2014-15 to 2020-21 period. full NCC coverage of \nall the government schools and colleges will require substantial commitment in terms \nof\t manpower,\tfinance,\tinfrastructure,\t etc.\t There\t is\t therefore\t no\t proposal\t to\t make\t NCC\t\ncompulsory for the students of schools and colleges in the country.\nSubsidised houses to serving and retired army personnel\n765. dR. KANw AR deeP sINgH: w ill the Minister of defeNCe be pleased \nto state:\n(a) whether government proposes to provide houses at concessional prices to \nserving and retired army personnel and if so, the details thereof; and\n(b) whether government has recently conducted any survey to provide houses \nto the serving and retired army personnel who belong to Punjab and Haryana and \nif so, the details thereof?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of defe NCe (dR. suBHAsH  \nRAMRAo BHAMRe): (a) No, sir. There is no such proposal to provide houses at \nconcessional prices to serving and retired Army personnel.\n(b) does not arise.\n\nWritten Answers to [11 february, 2019]   Unstarred Questions 95\nRecommendations of the Hooda Panel\n766. sHRIMATI AMBIKA soNI: \n  dR. T. suBBARAMI Reddy :\nw ill the Minister of defeNCe be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Hooda Panel on optimisation of the Army submitted its \nrecommendations to government;\n(b) if so, the key recommendations thereof;\n(c) whether it recommended for cutting down the size of Indian Army by \ncreating a reserve, if so, the details thereof and the response of government; and\n(d) whether it is also recommended for setting up of a new directorate to \naccelerate technology induction, if so, the details thereof and government’s response \nthereto?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of defe NCe (dR. suBHAsH \nRAMRAo BHAMRe): (a) to (d) The information sought is about size and shape \nand operational aspects of Army. By its very nature it is sensitive and impinges on \nthe\t security\t of\t the\t country.\t Disclosure\t of\t details\t in\t this\t regard\t on\t the\t floor\t of\t the\t\nHouse will not be in the interest of national security.\nImprovement in the capability of HAL\n767. sHRI v. MuRAleedHARAN: w ill the Minister of defeNCe be pleased \nto state:\n(a) the details of efforts made by government to improve the capability of \nHindustan Aeronautics limited (HAl) in last three years;\n(b)\t the\t details\t of\t investment\t made\t and\t revenues\t or\t profits\t of\t HAL\t during\t the\t\nlast three years and the current year; and\n(c)\t whether\t it\t is\t a\t fact\t that\t despite\t challenges\t HAL\t has\t achieved\t significant\t\ngrowth\t revenue\t and\t profits\t in\t 2018-19,\t if\t so,\t the\t details\t thereof?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of defe NCe (dR. suBHAsH \nRAMRAo BHAMRe): (a) during the last three years, following efforts have been \nmade by the government to improve the capabilities of HAl:–\n ● for establishment of major servicing facility for Hawk-132 aircraft, \ngovernment of India (goI) has sanctioned `  450.59 crore in february, 2016.\n\n96 Written Answers to [RAJyA sABHA] Unstarred Questions\n ● for Repair and overhaul (RoH) facility for Adour Mk 871-07 engines, \ngoI has sanctioned ` 205.65 crore in March, 2016.\n ● for ramping up of manufacturing facility of l ight Combat Aircraft (l CA) \nTejas from existing 8 to 16 aircraft per annum, goI has sanctioned  \n` 1381.04 crore in March, 2017 with 50% funding by goI.\n ● for enhancement of RoH capacity of su-30 MKI from existing 15 to \n20 aircraft and aggregates from 15 to 35 aircraft sets per annum, goI \nhas sanctioned ` 1324.33 crore in April, 2017.\n ● for establishment of RoH facility for 3 line Replaceable units (lRus) \nof\t Adour\t Mk\t 871-07\t engines\t fitted\t on\t Hawk\t Mk\t 132\t aircraft\t GoI\t has\t\nsanctioned ` 60.72 crore in August, 2017.\n(b)\t and\t (c)\t The\t details\t of\t investment,\t Revenue\t and\t profit\t made\t by\t HAL\t during\t\nthe last 3 years and the current year are as below:–\n(` in crore)\nParticulars 2015-16 2016-17 2017-18 2018-19 \n(upto september, 2018)\nCapital Investment 1430 1514 1452 418\nRevenue from operations (Net) 16758 17950 18519 5424\nProfit\t After\t Tax 1998 2616 2070 650\n In the financial year 2018-19 upto second quarter i.e. september, 2018 compared \nwith corresponding quarter of financial year 2017-18, HAl  has registered 3% increase \nin\t revenue\t and\t 63%\t increase\t in\t profit.\nPost-retirement\t medical\t benefits\t to\t SSC\t officers\n768. sHRI deReK o’BRIeN: w ill the Minister of defeNCe be pleased to state:\n(a)\t whether\t Government\t has\t denied\t post-retirement\t medical\t benefits\t to\t Short-\nService\t Commission\t (SSC)\t officers\t of\t the\t armed\t forces;\t and\n(b) if so, the reasons therefor?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of defeNCe (dR. suBHAsH \nRAMRAo BHAMRe): (a) and (b) The government has recently formulated a special \nscheme\t to\t mitigate\t the\t hardships\t of\t Short\t Service\t Commissioned\t Officers\t (SSCOs)\t\nand\t Emergency\t Commissioned\t Officers\t (ECOs)\t in\t obtaining\t medical\t facilities\t after\t\ntheir release. The same is being processed for approval of the Cabinet.\n\nWritten Answers to [11 february, 2019]   Unstarred Questions 97\nModernisation of army equipments\n769. sHRI deReK o’BRIeN: w ill the Minister of defeNCe be pleased to state:\n(a) the percentage of the army’s equipment in the vintage category, current \ncategory and state-of-the art category respectively and the details thereof;\n(b)\t whether\t the\tallocation\tin\tthe\tDefence\tBudget\tis\t sufficient\tfor\tthe\tmodernisation\t\nof army equipments;\n(c) whether Make-in-India is being utilised to modernise army’s equipment; and\n(d) if so, the status of all defence projects undertaken under the Make-in-India-\nInitiative during the last three years and the details thereof?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of defe NCe (dR. suBHAsH \nRAMRAo BHAMRe): (a) and (b) The authorisation and holding of army’s equipments \nis as per operational requirement of the Indian Army. Modernisation of the Armed \nforces is a continuous process undertaken to keep the forces in a state of readiness \nto meet operational and security challenges. The modernisation projects are progressed \nas per approval of services Capital Acquisition Plans and Annual Acquisition Plans \nin terms of extant defence Procurement Procedure (dPP). Based on the allocation \nof\t funds\t under\t Budget\t Estimates/Supplementary/Revised\t Estimates,\t sufficient\t funds\t\nare made available to meet the critical requirements of services including Army. \nThe allocated funds are optimally and fully utilised towards operational activities.\n(c) and (d) dPP-2016 focuses on institutionalizing, streamlining and simplifying \ndefence procurement procedure to give boost to ‘Make in India’ initiative of the \ngovernment, by promoting indigenous design, development and manufacturing of \ndefence equipment, platforms, systems and sub-systems. during the last three years \nand current year (up to october, 2018), 121 contracts have been signed with the \nIndian vendors.\nInter-Governmental agreement between India \nand France on Rafale jets\n770. sHRI sANJA y  sINgH: w ill the Minister of defeNCe be pleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that an Inter-governmental agreement was made between \nIndia\t and\t France\t for\t the\t procurement\t of\t 36\t Rafale\t jets\t in\t fly-away\t condition\t from\t\na french company dassault Aviation;\n(b)\t if\t so,\t the\t details\t of\t the\t timeline/time-frame\t when\t the\t Services\t Qualitative\t\nRequirements\t (SQRs)\t for\t the\t procurement\t of\t 36\t Rafale\t jets\t in\t a\t fly-away\t condition\t\nwere prepared and issued; and\n\n98 Written Answers to [RAJyA sABHA] Unstarred Questions\n(c)\t if\t so,\t the\t details\t thereof\t and\t the\t timeline/time-frame\t when\t the\t SQRs\t for\t\nthe previously agreed upon 126 Medium Multi-role Combat Aircraft MMRCAs were \nprepared and issued?\nTHe MINIsTeR of defeNCe (sHRIMATI NIRMAlA sITHARAMAN):  \n(a) to (c) An Inter-governmental Agreement (IgA) was signed on 23rd september, \n2016 between the government of India and the government of french Republic \nfor\t the\t procurement\t of\t 36\t Rafale\t fighter\t aircraft\t in\t fly-away\t condition,\t along\t with\t\nan initial consignment of weapons, maintenance support through Performance Based \nLogistic\t (PBL),\t Simulator\t and\t associated\t equipment.\t The\t procurement\t of\t 36\t flyaway\t\nRafale\t aircraft\t have\t been\t processed\t on\t the\t same\t Services\t Qualitative\t Requirements\t\n(SQRs)\t as\t finalized\t for\t the\t 126\t Medium\t Multi-role\t Combat\t Aircraft\t (MMRCA).\t The\t\nASQRs\t for\t 126\t MMRCA\t case\t were\t finalized\t in\t June,\t 2006\t and\t the\t Request\t For\t\nProposal (RfP) was issued in August, 2007. \nDisability\t pension/benefits\t of\t soldiers/veterans\n771. sHRI RAJeev  CHANdRAseKHAR: w ill the Minister of defeNCe be \npleased to state:\n(a) whether government is considering putting an end to routinely challenging \nthe\t Armed\t Forces\t Tribunal\t rulings\t and\t filing\t appeals\t against\t cases\t of\t disability\t\npension/benefits\t of\t soldiers/veterans;\n(b) if so, the time-line for the same; and\n(c)\t any\t other\tsteps\ttaken\tby\t Government\tto\t end\t filing\tof\t appeals\tagainst\tdisability\t\npension/benefits\t to\t soldiers?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of defe NCe (dR. suBHAsH \nRAMRAO\t BHAMRE):\t (a)\t and\t (b)\t Ministry\t of\t Defence\t do\t not\t routinely\t file\t Civil\t\nAppeals in the Hon’ble supreme Court challenging the Hon’ble Armed forces Tribunals \n(AfTs) rulings. It is only in cases where rulings of Hon’ble AfTs are against the \nexisting\t Government\t policies/rules\t and\t involve\t huge\t financial\t implications,\t that\t Civil\t\nAppeals\t are\t filed\t in\t consultation\t with\t Legal\t Advisor\t (Defence)\t and\t Ministry\t of\t Law\t\nand Justice.\n(c) In cases where Hon’ble supreme Court has settled the law, no civil appeal \nis\t generally\t filed\t in\t the\t Hon’ble\t Supreme\t Court.\n\nWritten Answers to [11 february, 2019]   Unstarred Questions 99\nStatus of internal Committee to examine \nrecommendations of OMJC\n772. sHRI RAJeev  CHANdRAseKHAR: w ill the Minister of defeNCe be \npleased to state:\n(a) the status of the internal Committee constituted by government on 19.07.2017 \nto examine the recommendations of one Member Judicial Committee (oMJC) report \nwith\t respect\t to\t feasibility\t and\t financial\t aspects\t of\t One\t Rank\t One\t Pension\t (OROP);\t\nand\n(b) timeline for implementing recommendations made by the oMJC?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of defe NCe (dR. suBHAsH \nRAMRAo BHAMRe): (a) and (b) The recommendations of one Member Judicial \nCommittee (oMJC) are under examination by the Internal Committee.\nEncroachment of defence lands\n773.\t SHRI\t MD.\t NADIMUL\t HAQUE:\t Will\tthe\t Minister\t of\t DEFENCE\t be\t pleased\t\nto state:\n(a) whether there has been an increase in cases of illegal transfer of government \nland in defence estates across the country during last three years, if so, details thereof;\n(b) details of action taken against individuals/entities on the above cases during \nthe last three years;\n(c) measures being taken to prevent incidence of such cases in the future;\n(d) whether government has prepared digital database of the defence estates/ \nland/properties in the country, if so, the details thereof; and\n(e) total defence land under the control of directorate general of defence estates \ntill date in different states/union Territories of the country including w est Bengal?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of defe NCe (dR. suBHAsH \nRAMRAo BHAMRe): (a) No, sir.\n(b) In cases of illegal transfer based on the nature and circumstances of the case, \nFIRs/criminal\t cases\t have\t been\t filed\t against\t the\t sellers/purchasers,\t Courts\t have\t been\t\napproached to set aside such transactions. Competent Revenue authorities have also \nbeen approached for not effecting mutation of such transactions. such transactions \nare not recognised and no mutation in defence land records are effected.\n\n100 Written Answers to [RAJyA sABHA] Unstarred Questions\n(c) To prevent such incidences in future, the following steps are taken:–\n(i) The defence land across the country has been surveyed by defence \nEstates\t Officers\t and\t Chief\t Executive\t Officers\t so\t as\t to\t clearly\t demarcate\t\ndefence land to enable services/users to physically protect it. \n(ii) Matter has been taken up with the state governments vigorously at all \nlevels to mutate remaining defence land in the revenue records.\n(iii) suggestions have been made by the Ministry to the department of land \nResources for amending the Registration Act so as to make it mandatory \nfor the sub-Registrar to obtain ‘NoC’ from defence authorities prior to \neffecting registration of land located in Cantonments/Military stations.\n(iv) wherever such incidence is reported, immediate legal action is taken to \nprotect the interest of government.\n(v) Computerisation of defence l and records contained in general l and \nRegisters (glRs) and Military land Registers (MlRs) with the use of \nRaksha Bhoomi software.\n(vi) strengthening of defence land management by way of digitisation of \nland\t records,\t survey,\t demarcation\t and\t verification\t of\t defence\t lands\t and\t\nland Audit.\n(d) y es, s ir. The computerization of defence land records under the project \n‘Raksha Bhoomi’ was undertaken by the directorate general of defence estates in \nassociation with National Informatics Centre. defence land Records contained in \ngeneral land Registers (glRs) and Military land Registers (MlRs) were entered in \nthe\t Raksha\tBhoomi\tSoftware\tand\t made\t operational\tafter\tverification\tand\t authentication\t\nof database in July, 2011.\n(e) Total defence land under the management of defence estates organisation \nin the country is 66,103 acres. state and union Territories-wise details, including \nw est Bengal are given in the statement.\nStatement\nState/UT-wise details of total defence land under the control of Directorate \nGeneral of Defence Estates\nsl.No. state/union Territory Area (in acres)\n 1. Andhra Pradesh 1594.63\n 2. Andaman and Nicobar 0\n 3. Assam 2.31\n\nWritten Answers to [11 february, 2019]   Unstarred Questions 101\nsl.No. state/union Territory Area (in acres)\n 4. Arunachal Pradesh 0\n 5. Bihar 2182.68\n 6. Chandigarh 0\n 7. Chhattisgarh 0\n 8. delhi 1808.03\n 9. goa 0\n10. gujarat 480.24\n11. Haryana 1713.97\n12. Himachal Pradesh 2900.18\n13. Jammu and Kashmir 1255.91\n14. Jharkhand 1094.75\n15. Karnataka 757.9\n16. Kerala 98.43\n17. lakshadweep 0\n18. Maharashtra 8239.52\n19. Madhya Pradesh 6136.58\n20. Manipur 0\n21. Meghalaya 116.52\n22. Mizoram 0\n23. Nagaland 0\n24. odisha 1.8\n25. Puducherry 0\n26. Punjab 4242.35\n27. Rajasthan 3206.37\n28. sikkim 0\n29. Telangana 1065.8\n30. Tamil Nadu 1109.58\n31. Tripura 0\n32. uttar Pradesh 16553.34\n33. uttarakhand 10521.25\n\n102 Written Answers to [RAJyA sABHA] Unstarred Questions\nsl.No. state/union Territory Area (in acres)\n34. w est Bengal 1021.15\n35. dadra and Nagar Haveli 0\n36. daman and diu 0\n ToT al 66103.29\nEmploying ex-servicemen in services\n774. sHRI HIsHey  lACHuNgPA: w ill the Minister of defeNCe be pleased \nto state:\n(a) whether any steps are being taken by government to ensure that judicious \nuse of the services of ex-servicemen, in services such as patrolling ghats or acting \nas security personnel in domestic security purposes;\n(b) if so, the details thereof; and\n(c) if not, the reasons therefor?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of defeNCe (dR. suBHAsH \nRAMRAo BHAMRe): (a) No, sir.\n(b) Not applicable in view of reply to (a) above.\n(c)\t Directorate\t General\t Resettlement,\t an\t attached\t office\t of\t Ministry\t of\t Defence,\t\ndepartment of ex-servicemen w elfare empanels/sponsors ex-servicemen (esM) run \nPrivate security Agencies, and state esM Corporations for providing security guards \nto various Central Public sector undertakings (CPsus), Corporate Houses, Private \nsector undertakings only. The scheme offers self-employment opportunities to retired \nofficers\tand\t adequate\temployment\topportunity\tto\t retired\tJunior\tCommissioned\tOfficers/\t\nOther\t Ranks,\t in\t a\t field,\t where\t they\t have\t sufficient\t expertise.\nDefence innovation HUBS\n775. sHRI d. KuPeNdRA Reddy : w ill the Minister of defeNCe be pleased \nto state:\n(a) whether defence innovation hubs are being set up in the country;\n(b) if so, the details thereof and the salient features of the hub; and\n(c) the names of the states wherein the hubs are being set up and the reasons/ \ncriteria in selecting the states where these hubs are being set up?\n\nWritten Answers to [11 february, 2019]   Unstarred Questions 103\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of defe NCe (dR. suBHAsH \nRAMRAo BHAMRe): (a) to (c) 1. The Innovations for defence excellence (ideX) \nframework of the government envisages setting up and managing independent defence \nInnovation Hubs (dIHs). These dIHs will serve as platforms where innovators can \nget information about needs and feedback from the services directly and create \nsolutions for India’s major defence platforms. This structure is also geared towards \nattracting more innovators to work for the defence sector in India.\n2. The defence Innovation o rganisation set up under ideX has announced \nsetting up of two dIHs in Tamil Nadu (Coimbatore) and Maharashtra (Nashik).\n3. The framework to fund defence Innovation Hubs under ideX, approved \nby the Board of defence Innovation organisation (dIo) prescribes the following \nminimum criterion for setting up defence Innovation Hubs:\n3.1 Any Central g overnment recognized Incubator including but not limited \nto:\n3.1.1 department of science and Technology (dsT) recognized Incubators.\n3.1.2 Atal Innovation Mission, NITI Aayog created Atal Incubation Centres \n(AICs) and established Incubation Centres (eICs).\n3.1.3 Ministry of MsMe recognized incubators.\n3.2 Any other incubator recognized or funded through any Central \ngovernment scheme.\n3.3 The incubator located in districts mentioned in the list of sMe clusters \nhosted by the Ministry of MsMe in collaboration with uNIdo.\n3.4 Incubator/Hub promoted by local industry associations.\nRecommendations of Shekatkar Committee\n776. sHRIMATI wANsuK syIeM: w ill the Minister of defeNCe be pleased \nto state:\n(a) whether under the on-going army reform measures recommended by lt. gen. \n(Retd.)\t D.B.\t Shekatkar\t Committee,\t phased\t manpower\t cuts\t over\t two\t to\t five\t years\t\ninvolving 57,000 to 1,00,000 soldiers, will save ` 5,000 to ` 7,000 crore a year in \nrevenue expenditure;\n(b) whether under a new concept of manpower restructuring, by forming bulked-\nup brigades called Integrated Battle groups will replace division-sized forces; and\n(c) whether the w orld w ar-II era, Army Postal establishments and Military farms \nin peacetime will be shut down?\n\n104 Written Answers to [RAJyA sABHA] Unstarred Questions\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of defe NCe (dR. suBHAsH \nRAMRAo BHAMRe): (a) The implementation of certain recommendations made \nby the Committee of experts (Coe) constituted under the Chairmanship of lt. gen. \n(Retd.) d.B. shekatkar involves redeployment and restructuring of approximately \n57,000 posts.\n(b) Relevant steps are taken by the government from time to time to optimise the \nmilitary structures so as to make our forces lean, versatile and agile and effectively \nfaces the challenges posed to our national security.\n(c) As part of the recommendations of the shekatkar Committee, action has \nbeen initiated for closure of 39 Military farms and restructuring of Army Postal \nestablishments.\nProcedure followed for Rafale Jet contract\n777. dR. sANTANu seN: w ill the Minister of defeNCe be pleased to state:\n(a) the price of each Rafale jet; \n(b) the manner in which decision to buy 36 jets was arrived at; and\n(c) the manner in which Indian offset partner was selected?\nTHe MINIsTeR of defeNCe (sHRIMATI NIRMAlA sITHARAMAN): (a) \nBasic Aircraft price has already been disclosed to the Parliament. The government \nhas also shared all pricing details with CAg. \n(b) The Request for Proposal (RfP) for 126 Medium Multi-role Combat Aircraft \n(MMRCA) was withdrawn in June, 2015 due to stalemate in negotiations between \ndassault Aviation and HAl. In order to meet the Critical operational Necessity for \nfighter\t aircraft\t in\t the\t Indian\t Air\t Force\t (IAF),\t the\t Government\t decided\t to\t procure\t\n36 Rafale aircraft through government to government route to expedite induction \nof\t fighters.\t The\t procedures\t as\t per\t DPP\t provisions\t were\t followed.\n(c) As per d efence o ffset g uidelines, the o riginal equipment Manufacturer \n(oeM)/ vendor/Tier-I sub-vendor is free to select the Indian offset Partner (IoP) \nfor implementing the offset obligations provided the Indian offset Partner (IoP) has \nnot been barred from doing business by the Ministry of defence.\nDelay in building ships, cost escalation affecting Navy’s upgradation plans\n778. sHRI MAJeed MeMoN: w ill the Minister of defeNCe be pleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that severe cost overruns and delays have besieged \npublic sector defence shipyards, resulting in a cascading effect on the operational \n\nWritten Answers to [11 february, 2019]   Unstarred Questions 105\npreparedness and technological upgradation of the Navy, costing the exchequer several \ncrores;\n(b) whether it is also a fact that multi-billion-dollar effort to replace the Navy’s \nwarship\t fleet\t has\t got\t the\t Defence\t PSUs\t battling\t infrastructural\t constraints,\t as\t well\t\nas setbacks in the development of indigenised weapons and sensors and delays in \ntrial; and\n(c) steps taken by government to achieve the inspirational target of manufacturing \nseventy per cent of defence equipment indigenously?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of defe NCe (dR. suBHAsH \nRAMRAo BHAMRe): (a) and (b) There have been occasion where w arship \nConstruction Projects of Indian Navy have had time and/or cost overruns due to \ndesign\t finalization,\t delay\t in\t receipt\t of\t critical\t equipment/technology,\t infrastructural\t\nconstraints of the shipyard and delay in development of indigenized equipment/ \nweapon. Modernization of defence Public sector undertakings (dPsus) in terms of \ninfrastructure to enhance productivity from time to time on required basis is also \nundertaken by the Ministry.\n(c)\t Indigenisation\t of\t equipment/systems,\t sub-systems,\t components\t fitted\t onboard\t\nships and submarines, Indian Naval Indigenisation Pan-2015-30, are some of the steps \ntaken to indigenize manufacturing of defence equipment.\nProcedure followed for the Rafale Jets contract\n779. sHRI PARTAP sINgH BAJw A: w ill the Minister of defeNCe be pleased \nto state:\n(a) the exact date on which the Hon’ble Prime Minister announced the deal for \n36 Rafale jets in france;\n(b) the exact date on which the Cabinet Committee on security approved the \ndeal for 36 Rafale jets;\n(c)\t whether\t any\t concerns\t regarding\t pricing\t raised\t by\t Defence\t Ministry\t Officials\t\nin the Indian Negotiation Team; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof?\nTHe MINIsTeR of defeNCe (sHRIMATI NIRMAlA sITHARAMAN):  \n(a) and (b) A joint statement was issued on 10th April, 2015 during the visit of the \nHon’ble Prime Minister to france which indicated an intent to acquire 36 Rafale \njets\t in\t fly-away\t condition.\t After\t approval\t of\t the\t Cabinet\t Committee\t on\t Security\t\n\n106 Written Answers to [RAJyA sABHA] Unstarred Questions\n(CCs) on 24th August, 2016, an Inter-governmental Agreement was signed on  \n23rd september, 2016.\n(c) and (d) The Indian Negotiation Team (INT) followed the collegiate process \nand all views of various members of the INT were recorded and addressed suitably. \nAll the members of the INT had signed the Report.\nRequirement of Air Force and recommendation note \nby senior bureaucrats for Rafale\n780. sHRI BINoy  vIsw AM: w ill the Minister of defeNCe be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the present government has made any change or revised any contract \non Rafale deal that has been earlier signed with french government;\n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor;\n(c) whether Air force had made any submission to defence Ministry of their \nrequirement for the number of Rafale fighter Jets;\n(d) if so, the details thereof;\n(e)\t whether\t any\t senior\t bureaucrat\t or\t Air\t Force\t officers\t made\t any\t objection\t or\t\nnote of apprehension on the procedure or the way Rafale deal has been conducted \nwith france; and\n(f) if so, the details thereof?\nTHe  MINIsT eR of defe NCe (sHRIMATI NIRMAl A sITHARAMAN): (a) to \n(d) No deal for Rafale aircraft was entered into earlier by the government. due to \nthe impasse in negotiations the Request for Proposal (RfP) for 126 Medium Multi \nRole Combat Aircraft (MMRCA) was withdrawn in June, 2015. In order to meet the \nCritical\t Operational\t Necessity\t for\t fighter\t aircraft\t in\t the\t Indian\t Air\t Force\t (IAF),\t 36\t\nRafale aircraft are being procured through an Inter-governmental Agreement (IgA) \nbetween the government of India and the government of french Republic.\nThe IgA was signed on 23rd september, 2016 between the government of \nIndia and the government of french Republic for the procurement of 36 Rafale \nfighter\t aircraft\t in\t fly-away\t condition,\t along\t with\t an\t initial\t consignment\t of\t weapons,\t\nmaintenance support through Performance Based logistic (PBl), simulator and \nassociated equipment.\n(e) and (f) The Indian Negotiation Team (INT) followed the collegiate process \nand all views of various members of the INT were recorded and addressed suitably. \nAll the members of the INT had signed the Report.\n\nWritten Answers to [11 february, 2019]   Unstarred Questions 107\nDefence offset contracts\n781. sHRI RITABRATA BANeRJee: w ill the Minister of defeNCe be pleased \nto state:\n(a) details of offset contracts from defence sector by government in last three \nyears;\n(b) whether government has conducted or is aware of any study, on the ways in \nwhich\t the\t offset\t contracts\t have\t benefited\t Indian\t manufacturers\t and\t if\t so,\t the\t details\t\nthereof;\n(c) whether India has been able to develop a strong manufacturing industry to \ninvest foreign direct Investment (fdI) in the sector and if so, details thereof; and\n(d) the total fdI in defence sector during the last three years?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of defe NCe (dR. suBHAsH \nRAMRAo BHAMRe): (a) 21 defence offset contracts with cumulative value of 5.67 \nBillion us dollars approximately have been signed by Ministry of defence (Mod) \nin last three years.\n(b) Mod has assigned a study through Institute for defence studies and Analysis \n(IdsA) to ascertain the impact of offsets on Indian defence Industrial Base. The \ninterim report has been received.\n(c)\t and\t (d)\t Defence\t industry\t sector\t was\t first\t opened\t up\t in\t May,\t 2001\t for\t 100%\t\nmanufacturing by Indian Private sector including fdI upto admissible cap both subject \nto licensing. department of Industrial Policy and Promotion (dIPP) vide press note \n5(21016)\t notified\t revised\t FDI\t policy\t under\t which\t FDI\t is\t allowed\t under\t automatic\t\nroute upto 49% and beyond 49% through government route whenever it is likely to \nresult in access to modern technology or for other reasons to be recorded. so far, 41 \nfdI proposals/Joint ventures have been approved in defence sector for manufacture \nof various defence equipments, both in public and private sector. further, government \nhas so far issued 430 licenses till december, 2018 for manufacturing of various \nlicensable defence items.\nfdI of ` \t 1.16\tcrore\thas\tbeen\treceived\tunder\tthe\t3\t National\tIndustrial\tClassification\t\n(NIC) Codes of defence sector as informed by dIPP. In addition, 6 companies, for \nwhich fdI approval had been accorded, received ` 237.44 crores in defence and \naerospace sector since April, 2014 in other than 3 NIC codes.\n\n108 Written Answers to [RAJyA sABHA] Unstarred Questions\nStatus of Arjun Tank\n782. sHRI HARsHv ARdHAN sINgH duNgARPuR: w ill the Minister of \ndefeNCe be pleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that as Arjun battle tank of the Army was rejected due \nto failure to meet the minimum operational requirements of the Army;\n(b)\t whether\t certain\t changes\t were\t made\t for\t improvements\t and\t modifications\t on\t\nthe basis of observation during Accelerated usage cum Reliability Trial (AuCRT) \nand extended AuCRT of main battle tank, Arjun;\n(c) if so, the details thereof; and\n(d) if not, the reasons therefor and the status of main battle tank, Arjun as on \ndate?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of defe NCe (dR. suBHAsH \nRAMRAo BHAMRe): (a) to (d) Main Battle Tank (MBT) Arjun Mk-1 tank has met \nthe operational requirements of the Indian Army. At present MBT Arjun is in-service \nwith\t the\t Indian\t Army.\t Improvements/upgradation\t for\t enhancing\t operational\t efficiency\t\nof equipment is an on-going process with validation trials.\nClean drinking water supply\n783. sHRI AMAR sINgH: w ill the Minister of dRINKINg wATeR ANd  \nsANITATIoN be pleased to state:\n(a) whether government has launched pilot projects for the supply of clean \ndrinking water in villages and if so, the details thereof; \n(b)\t whether\t NITI\tAayog\t has\t identified\tsome\t districts\t for\t implementation\t of\t clean\t\ndrinking water supply in villages and if so, the details thereof; and\n(c) whether all villages in u.P., Haryana and Punjab are likely to be covered \nunder this scheme and if so, by when?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of dRINKINg wATeR \nANd sANITATIoN (sHRI RAMesH CHANdAPPA JIgAJINAgI): (a) to (c) This \nMinistry has launched ‘swajal’ programme, a community demand driven, single \nvillage, preferably solar Powered piped water supply schemes in Aspirational districts \nidentified\tbyNational\t Institution\t for\t Transforming\t India\t (NITI)\tAayog\t which\t will\t help\t\nin increasing the outreach of drinking water in underserved rural areas. \nThe funding pattern of the scheme is 81:09:10 (Centre; state; gram Panchayat) \n\nWritten Answers to [11 february, 2019]   Unstarred Questions 109\nfor Himalayan and Hilly states and 45:45:10 (Centre:state:gram Panchayat) for other \nstates. The funding is to be under the “flexi-fund” of National Rural drinking w ater \nProgramme and states can access 5% of funds both Central and state share put \ntogether. Individual swajal schemes are to be completed between six months to one \nyear. only villages without an existing piped water supply scheme in Aspirational \ndistricts can be taken for implementation under swajal.\nThe list of Aspirational districts in Haryana, Punjab and uttar Pradeshis as under:–\nstate district\nHaryana Mewat\nPunjab firozepur, Moga\nuttar Pradesh Bahraich, Balrampur, Chandauli, Chitrakoot, fatehpur, shrawasti, \nsiddharthnagar, sonbhadra\nUnutilised funds under SBM(G)\n784. sHRI vIve K K. TANKHA: w ill the Minister of dRINKINg wATe R \nANd sANITATIoN be pleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that the Central government allocation to swachh Bharat \nMission (gramin) for the period 2014-15 to 2018-19 was ` 1,00,447 crore and out \nof this, only ` 52,166 crore has been allocated to the scheme up to 2018-19; and\n(b) if so, the detailed reasons, as to why only 52 per cent of the funds have \nbeen allocated till date and proposals for utilisation of the 48 per cent unutilized \nfunds, that are yet to be released, before october, 2019?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of dRINKINg wATeR ANd \nsANITATIoN (sHRI RAMesH CHANdAPPA JIgAJINAgI): (a) and (b) details \nof Central government Budgetary allocations for swachh Bharat Mission (gramin) \n[sBM(g)] since 2014-15 are given below:–\nyear Budgetary allocation (` in crore)\n2014-15 2850.00\n2015-16 6525.00\n2016-17 10513.00\n2017-18 16948.27\n2018-19 (Re) 14478.03\nToT al 51314.30\n\n110 Written Answers to [RAJyA sABHA] Unstarred Questions\nduring 2018-19, in addition to the Budgetary allocation, funds up to ` 15,000 \ncrore has been provisioned to be raised through extra Budgetary Resources. for the \nyear 2019-20, Budget estimate for sBM(g) is ` 10,000 crore.\nBio-toilets in village panchayats\n785. sHRI sANJAy  RAuT: w ill the Minister of dRINKINg wATeR ANd \nsANITATIoN be pleased to state:\n(a) whether government has any proposal to set up bio-toilets in village panchayats \nin Maharashtra state;\n(b) if so, the details thereof, district-wise; and\n(c) the time-frame by which all the village panchayats are likely to be converted \nunder the scheme?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of dRINKINg wATeR ANd \nsANITATIoN (sHRI RAMesH CHANdAPPA JIgAJINAgI): (a) No, sir.\n(b) and (c) does not arise. \nAction plan for pure drinking water\n786. Ms. sARoJ PANdey : w ill the Minister of dRINKINg wATeR ANd \nsANITATIoN be pleased to state whether government has formulated any action plan \nto provide pure drinking water to every house in the rural areas of the country, if \nso, the roadmap thereof along with the number of villages which have been provided \npure drinking water under the action plan during last three years?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of dRINKINg wATeR ANd \nsANITATIoN (sHRI RAMesH CHANdAPPA JIgAJINAg I): Rural drinking water \nsupply is a state subject. It is the state governments which plan, design, approve, \nexecute and operate and maintain the schemes for providing safe drinking water to \nrural\t population.\t However,\t this\t Ministry\t provides\t technical\t and\t financial\t assistance\t to\t\nstates under National Rural drinking w ater Programme (NRdwP) for improving the \ncoverage of safe drinking water in rural areas. further, this Ministry has restructured \nNRdwP to make it more competitive, outcome based and result oriented wherein \nfocus of the Ministry is on providing Piped w ater supply to every rural household \nwhich\t aims\t to\t fulfill\t the\t commitment\t made\t by\t the\t country\t in\t the\t international\t forum\t\nwhile agreeing for uN sustainable development goal (sdg)-2030. w ithin NRdwP, \nthis Ministry has also launched ‘swajal’ programme, a community demand driven, \nsingle village, preferably solar Powered piped water supply schemes in aspirational \n\nWritten Answers to [11 february, 2019]   Unstarred Questions 111\ndistricts\t identified\t by\t National\t Institution\t for\t Transforming\t India\t (NITI)\t Aayog\t which\t\nwill help in increasing the outreach of drinking water in underserved rural areas.\nunder NRdwP, every year Annual Action Plan meetings with all states is held \nduring the month of february-March wherein detailed action plan is discussed to \nfurther improving the coverage of safe drinking water in rural areas in order to \nmeet the demand of rising population of the country. In addition to this, this year, \na\t National\t Workshop\t for\t finalizing\t of\t Annual\t Action\t Plan\t was\t held\t with\t the\t States\t\non 28-29th January, 2019.\nunder NRdwP, coverage of safe drinking water in rural areas is maintained in \nterms of fully Covered habitations (i.e. getting more than 40 litre per capita per \nday (lpcd) safe drinking water), Partially Covered habitations (i.e. getting less than \n40\t lpcd\t safe\t drinking\t water)\t and\t Quality\t Affected\t habitations\t (i.e. water containing \nchemical contaminant) and not in terms of villages. As reported by states on \nIntegrated Management Information system (IMIs), coverage of habitations under \nNRdwP during last three years are as under:–\nyear Coverage of habitations\n2015-16 85,888\n2016-17 61,042\n2017-18 53,411\nformat1 of IMIs.\nSwachh Bharat Mission-Gramin\n787. sHRI PARTAP sINgH BAJw A: w ill the Minister of dRINKINg wATeR \nANd sANITATIoN be pleased to state:\n(a) the salient features of the swachh Bharat Mission-gramin;\n(b) the details of the funds allocated every year since the inception of the \nscheme, along with the funds released to states/districts and all the activities on \nwhich these funds were spent; and\n(c) the minimum criteria for self-reporting of villages/towns/localities as open \ndetecation free, along with the total number of villages/towns/localities that have \ndone so?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of dRINKINg wATeR ANd \nsANITATIoN (sHRI RAMesH CHANdAPPA JIgAJINAg I): (a) The government \nhad launched swachh Bharat Mission (gramin) [sBM(g)] with effect from 2nd \n\n112 Written Answers to [RAJyA sABHA] Unstarred Questions\no ctober, 2014 with the aim to attain o pen d efecation f ree (odf ) India by  \n2nd october, 2019 by providing access to toilet facilities to all rural households in \nthe country. The salient features of sBM(g) are:–\n(i) emphasis on behaviour change including interpersonal communication; \nstrengthening implementation and delivery mechanisms down to gram \nPanchayat level.\n(ii) flexibility to states in designing delivery mechanisms taking into account \ntheir local culture, practices, sensibilities and demands.\n(iii) greater emphasis on capacity building, especially in community approaches \nand programme management - training of KRCs (Key resource Centres) \nand involvement of Collectors to provide leadership at the district level.\n(iv) Programme being run as a citizens’ movement with cooperation of all \nsections of society including Ngos, corporates, youth etc.\n(b) details of the funds allocated for sBM(g) and the funds released to the \nstate/uTs since 2014-15 are given below:–\n(` in crore)\nfinancial year funds allocated for sBM(g) funds released to states/uTs\n2014-15 2850.00 2730.30\n2015-16 6525.00 6362.96\n2016-17 10513.00 10271.96\n2017-18 16948.27 16610.88\n2018-19 (Re) 14478.03 11900.1\n(up to 06.02.2019)\nduring 2018-19, in addition to the general Budgetary support, funds up to  \n` 15,000 crore have been provisioned to be raised through extra Budgetary Resources, \nout of which an amount of ` 5425.40 crore has been raised and released to the states \nup to 06.02.2019. The funds under sBM(g) are spent under following components:\n(i) Construction of Individual Household latrines (IHHls)\n(ii) Construction of Community sanitary Complexes (CsCs)\n(iii) solid and liquid w aste Management (slwM) works\n(iv) Information, education and Communication (IeC) and Capacity Building \nactivities\n(v) Administrative charges\n\nWritten Answers to [11 february, 2019]   Unstarred Questions 113\n(c) As per sBM(g) guidelines, odf would mean the termination of faecal-oral \ntransmission,\t defined\t by,\t (a)\t no\t visible\t faeces\t found\t in\t the\t environment/village,\t and\t\n(b) every household as well as public/community institution(s) using safe technology \noption for disposal of faeces. v illages declare themselves odf through a rigorous \nprocess\t of\t self-declaration,\t followed\t by\t a\t multi-level\t decentralized\t verification\tprocess\t\nby the districts and states. 5,50,185 villages in the country have been declared odf \nas on 6.2.2019. \nsBM (urban) is being implemented by Ministry of Housing and urban Affairs. As \nper the information received from that Ministry, the urban local Bodies may declare \nthemselves\t ODF\t after\t fulfilling\t the\t infrastructure\t requirement\t of\t IHHL,\t Community\t\nToilets/Public Toilets and urinals and declaration of their elected representatives as \nper sBM (urban) guidelines. 4,125 urban local Bodies and Cantonment Boards \nhave been declared odf as on 31.12.2018. \nFunds under SBM(G)\n788. sHRI vIJA y  PAl  sINgH T oMAR: w ill the Minister of dRINKINg  \nwATeR ANd sANITATIoN be pleased to state:\n(a) the funds allocated under swachh Bharat Mission (gramin) (sBM-g) during \nthe last one year in the country, state/uT-wise;\n(b)\t whether\t Government\t has\t released\t first\t instalment\t of\t Central\t assistance\t for\t\nannual plan for the year 2018-19;\n(c) if not, the reasons therefor and the time by which the funds are likely to \nbe released;\n(d) the current progress of sBM-g; and\n(e) whether government has issued any guidelines to the states to bring \nimprovement therein and if so, the details thereof?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of dRINKINg wATeR \nANd sANITATIoN (sHRI RAMesH CHANdAPPA JIgAJINAgI): (a) swachh \nBharat\t Mission\t (Gramin)\t [SBM(G)]\t is\t a\t demand\t driven\t scheme,\t hence\t fixed\t State-\nwise allocation of funds is not made. However, s tate/u T-wise details of Centre \nshare funds released by the government of India during 2017-18 are given in the \nstatement (See below).\n(b) and (c) The 1st installment of Central assistance for annual plan for the \nyear 2018-19 under sBM(g) have been released to all the states/uTs except for \n\n114 Written Answers to [RAJyA sABHA] Unstarred Questions\nfew\t States/UTs\t to\t which\t 1st\t instalment\t could\t not\t be\t released\t due\t to\t non-fulfillment\t\nof\t some\t pre-requisite\t conditions.\t The\t same\t will\t be\t released\t on\t their\t fulfillment\t by\t\nthese states/uTs.\n(d) and (e) The government had launched sBM(g) with effect from 2nd october, \n2014 with the aim to attain open defecation free (odf) India by 2nd october, \n2019 by providing access to toilet facilities to all rural households in the country. \nunder sBM(g), 916.25 lakh individual household toilets have been constructed as \non 06.02.2019, and the sanitation coverage in the country, which was 38.7% as \non 2nd october, 2014, has increased to 98.85%. 5,50,185 villages, 2,46,126 gram \nPanchayats, 5937 Blocks, 602 districts, and 28 states/uTs, have been declared odf . \nStatement\nState/UT-wise Central share funds released by the Government of \nIndia under SBM(G) during 2017-18\nstate/uT Amount released (` in crore)\nAndaman and Nicobar Islands 30.72\nAndhra Pradesh 1219.88\nArunachal Pradesh 137.30\nAssam 1171.95\nBihar 875.92\nChhattisgarh 677.83\ndadra and Nagar Haveli 17.68\ndaman and diu 2.00\ngoa 0.52\ngujarat 466.04\nHaryana 39.66\nHimachal Pradesh 20.68\nJammu and Kashmir 202.38\nJharkhand 698.66\nKarnataka 983.39\nKerala 59.36\nMadhya Pradesh 1380.61\nMaharashtra 1235.34\nManipur 77.02\n\nWritten Answers to [11 february, 2019]   Unstarred Questions 115\nstate/uT Amount released (` in crore)\nMeghalaya 153.89\nMizoram 46.24\nNagaland 71.41\nodisha 457.02\nPuducherry 50.25\nPunjab 283.48\nRajasthan 981.51\nsikkim 12.98\nTamil Nadu 865.94\nTelangana 481.94\nTripura 24.00\nuttar Pradesh 3155.37\nuttarakhand 146.69\nw est Bengal 583.23\nToT al 16,610.88\nEffects of Global Warming\n789. dR. KIRodI lAl  MeeNA: w ill the Minister of eARTH sCIeNCes be \npleased to state:\n(a) whether the abnormal weather conditions like Tsunami, heavy rains and \ndrought are the outcome of global warming;\n(b) if so, the details thereof and the regions which have been worst affected \nby the climate change;\n(c)\t whether\t Government\t has\t conducted\t any\t scientific\t study\t regarding\t climate\t\nchange due to global warming and its adverse impact on the country;\n(d) if so, the outcomes thereof;\n(e) the details of the discussions held at various international fora and agreements \nentered into; and\n(f) the action plan of government to tackle the challenges of climate change?\nTHe  MINIsT e R of e ARTH sCI e NCes ( dR. HARsH vARdHAN): (a) \nAlthough not a direct cause and effect type relationship, events like heavy rainfall \nand drought in different parts of the country may have possible linkages with the \n\n116 Written Answers to [RAJyA sABHA] Unstarred Questions\nconcurrent\t global\t warming.\t Climate-model\t simulations\t predict\t an\t intensification\t of\t\nextreme precipitation in almost all areas of the world under global warming. local \nvariations\t in\t the\t magnitude\t of\t this\t intensification\t are\t evident\t in\t these\t simulations.\nTsunami is not an outcome of global warming, it is a geological hazard which \nis caused due to sub-marine earthquake, landslide and meteoritic impact. Though \ntsunami impact is not directly attributed to global warming, coastal inundation by \ntsunamis can exacerbate the impact on coastal zones due to future sea leve l rise. \n(b) Regions which are more prone to such events in the changing climate, include \nCentral India, northern Indian regions and w estern Himalayas (extreme rainfall) and \nnorth and northwest India and neighboring Central India (moderate droughts and \nexpansion in semiarid regions).\nglobal warming is also a driver for sea level rise around the globe. According \nto\t International\t Panel\t on\t Climate\t Change\t (IPCC)\t fifth\tassessment\t report\t (AR5)\t global\t\nsea level is rising at an average rate of 1.8 mm/yr over the last century. \nHowever, the sea levels are changing at different rates along the Indian coast as \nper the studies carried out at Indian National Centre for ocean Information services \n(INCoIs).The rate of change of sea level at 11 major ports as observed using the \nsea level gauges is shown in the table given below.\nsl. \nNo.\nlocation Rate of change of sea \nlevel (mm/year)\nduration of data used \n(years)\n1. Chennai 0.33 1916-2005\n2. diamond Harbour 5.6 1948-2005\n3. Haldia 2.89 1972-2005\n4. Kandla 3.18 1950-2005\n5. Kochi 1.30 1939-2005\n6. Mumbai 0.74 1878-2005\n7. Paradeep 1.03 1966-2005\n8. Port Blair 2.20 1916-1964\n9. v izag 0.97 1937-1988\n10. okha 1.50 1964-1991\n11. v izag 0.97 1937-2005\n\nWritten Answers to [11 february, 2019]   Unstarred Questions 117\nAnother study carried out at INCoIs based on satellite altimetry and model \nsimulations showed that during the last decade (2003-2013) the North Indian ocean \n(NIo) experienced an alarming sea level rise at a rate of 6. mm/year.\n(c)\t Y es\t there\t have\t been\t several\t scientific\t studies\t on\t these\t fronts\t and\t their\t\nadverse impacts on the country. sudden occurrences of rainfall and temperature \nextremes,\t shifts\t in\t semiarid\t regions\t etc.\t are\t some\t of\t the\t recent\t findings\t which\t may\t\nhave possible linkages with climate change.\n(d)\t Studies\t have\t reported\t significant\t rising\t trends\t in\t the\t frequency\t and\t the\t\nmagnitude of extreme rainfall over different regions of India against a backdrop of \nglobal warming. More intense droughts are mainly observed over north and northwest \nIndia and neighboring Central India. The warmer ssTs over the tropical oceans are \nfound\t to\t be\t significantly\t associated\t with\t the\t variability\t of\t meteorological\t droughts\t\nover\t India.\t Further\t it\t was\t found\t that\t significant\t increasing\t trend\t in\t the\t intensity\t\nand areal coverage of moderate droughts over India during the recent decades. Also \nexpansion in the area of the semiarid regions in the different parts of country during \nthe recent decades due to the changing regional precipitation patterns in association \nwith rising temperatures due to global warming. INCoIs has carried out some coastal \nvulnerability studies as part of Tsunami early w arning system. Coastal v ulnerability \nIndex (CvI) mapping (at 1:100000 scale) was carried out to assess the probable \nimplications of the sea level rise along the Indian coast. Besides, INCoIs has also \ngenerated the Multi-Hazard v ulnerability Maps (MH v M) for mainland of India  \n(at\t 1:25000\t scale).\t These\t MHVMs\t are\t indicative\t maps\t of\t probable\t coastal\t flooding\t\ndue to ocean related disasters that can cause coastal inundation.\n(e) Ministry of environment, forest and Climate Change (Moef&CC) government \nof India has been successful in establishing bilateral relations with several countries \non areas of environment, clean technology and climate change. some of the major \nagreements in this area include agreement on cooperation on addressing climate change \nbetween India and China signed in october, 2009, Joint statement between India \nand united Kingdom on energy and Climate Change to promote secure, affordable \nand sustainable supplies of energy signed in November, 2015.\nfurther, India is a party to number of Conventions, Protocols, International Treaties, \nMultilateral environment Agreements like united Nations framework Convention on \nClimate\t Change\t (UNFCCC),\t United\t Nations\t Convention\t to\t Combat\t Desertification\t\n(uNCCd), Convention on Biological diversity (CBd), and Montreal Protocol on \nsubstances that deplete ozone layer. India has signed the Paris Agreement on Climate \nChange on 22 April, 2016, which is a milestone in global climate cooperation.\n\n118 Written Answers to [RAJyA sABHA] Unstarred Questions\nIn the climate change negotiations, India has always been a strong advocate of \nan ambitious and durable agreement based on the principles of equity and common \nbut differentiated responsibilities. In the run up to the Paris Agreement, India engaged \nconstructively and proactively with developing countries, including BAsIC (Brazil, \nsouth America, India, China) countries, like Minded developing Countries (lMdC) \nand group of 77 and China for protecting the interest of developing countries in the \nnew agreement. India has been able to secure its interest in the Paris Agreement. \n(f) Recognizing the challenge of Climate Change, the government is implementing \nNational Action Plan on Climate Change (NAPCC) which has eight missions in \nspecific\t areas\t of\t solar\t energy,\t energy\t efficiency,\t water,\t agriculture,\t Himalayan\t eco-\nsystem, sustainable habitat, green India and strategic knowledge on climate change. \nThree of these Missions namely the Missions on solar energy, enhanced energy \nefficiency\t and\t sustainable\t habitats\t focus\t on\t mitigation,\t while\t the\t rest\t are\t focused\t\non adaptation. Thirty two states/union Territories have prepared state Action Plan \non Climate Change (sAPCC) consistent with objectives of NAPCC and taking in to \naccount\t State’s\t specific\t issues\t relating\t to\t climate\t change.\t The\t SAPCCs\t prepared\t by\t\nstates/uTs inter alia\t indicate\t sector\t specific\t and\t cross\t sectoral\t priority\t action\t and\t\nfocus on adaptation of vulnerable areas in their respective jurisdictions.\nFunds for increasing forest cover\n790. dR. KIRodI l Al  Mee NA: w ill the Minister of e NvIRoNMe NT, foResT \nANd ClIMATe CHANge be pleased to state:\n(a) the area of forest cover in hectares in the country and the per centage \nthereof, state-wise;\n(b) the national average of forest cover in terms of hectare and per centage;\n(c) if so, the details thereof;\n(d) details of the funds provided by government for conservation, development \nand enrichment of the forests in the states including Rajasthan during the last three \nyears and the current year, scheme-wise, state-wise and year-wise; and\n(e) the details of achievements made as a result thereof?\nTHe MINIsTe R of sTATe  IN THe  MINIsTR y  of eNvIRoNMe NT, foResT \nANd ClIMATe CHANge (dR. MAHesH sHARMA): (a) to (c) forest survey \nof India, dehradun, a subordinate organisation under the Ministry carries out the \nassessment\t of\t forest\t cover\t of\t the\t country\t biennially\t and\t the\t findings\t are\t published\t\nin India state of forest Report (IsfR). As per the latest report i.e. IsfR-2017, \n\nWritten Answers to [11 february, 2019]   Unstarred Questions 119\nthe total forest and tree cover in the country is 8,02,088,00 hectares (forest cover \n70827300 hectares, tree cover 9381500 hectares) which is 24.39% of the geographical \narea of the country. There is an increase of 802100 hectares (forest cover 677800 \nhectares, tree cover 124300 hectares) of total forest and tree cover compared to that \nof IsfR-2015. The state/uTs-wise details of forest cover along with percentage as \nper IsfR-2017 is given in the statement-I (See below).\n(d) for conservation, development and enrichment of forest cover in the country, \nafforestation programmes are being taken up under various Centrally sponsored \nschemes such as National Afforestation Programme (NAP) and green India Mission \n(gIM) being implemented by Ministry of environment, forest and Climate Change. \nAfforestation activities are also taken up under various programmes/funding sources \nsuch as Mahatma gandhi National Rural employment guarantee scheme (MgNRegs), \nPradhan Mantri Krishi sinchayee yojana (PMKsy ) and Compensatory Afforestation fund.\nNational Mission for a green India (gIM) is one of the eight Missions outlined \nunder the National Action Plan on Climate Change. It aims at protecting, restoring \nand enhancing India’s forest cover and responding to Climate Change. gIM activities \nwere started in the financial year 2015-16.\nNational Afforestation Programme (NAP) of the Ministry of environment and \nforests is a Centrally sponsored scheme for afforestation and eco-restoration of \ndegraded forests and adjoining areas through people’s participation. The state/uTs-\nwise details of funds released under Compensatory Afforestation funds, green India \nMission and National Afforestation Programme for the last three years and current \nyear are given in the statement-II, III and Iv  respectively (See below).\n(e) As a result of various afforestation programme by Central and state \ngovernment, the forest and tree cover in the country has increased by 802100 \nhectares as per IsfR-2017 compared to that of IsfR-2015.\nStatement-I\nState/UTs-wise details of Forest Cover as per ISFR 2017\n(area in hectare)\nstate geographic Area Total forest cover \nas per IsfR-2017\nPer cent of \ngeographic area\n1 2 3  4\nAndhra Pradesh 16296800 28,14,700 17.27\nArunachal Pradesh 8374300 66,96,400 79.96\n\n120 Written Answers to [RAJyA sABHA] Unstarred Questions\n1 2 3  4\nAssam 7843800 28,10,500 35.83\nBihar 9416300 7,29,900 7.75\nChhattisgarh 13519200 55,54,700 41.09\ndelhi 148300 19,241 12.97\ngoa 370200 2,22,900 60.21\ngujarat 1