f RoAd TRANsPoRT \nANd HIgHw Ays be pleased to state:\n(a) whether more than 1000 kms. of National Highways are single lane and \nnearly 2,600 kms. of NHs are two lane in Andhra Pradesh; \n(b) if so, details thereof, district-wise; \n(c) reasons that such huge length of NHs are still languishing with single or \ntwo lanes; \n(d) details of constraints that the Ministry is facing to convert these into four/\nsix lane roads; \n(e) efforts made by this Ministry to overcome those constraints; and \n(f) length in kilometres in A.P. that are going to be converted into four lane \nand six lane out of proposed 2,700 kms. in 2016-17?\nTHe  MINI sT e R of sTAT e  IN THe  MINI sTRy  of R oAd TRANsPoRT \nANd HIgHw Ays (sHRI MANsuKH MANdA vIy A): (a) to (e) The length of \nsingle lane NH is 560 km and length of two lane NH is 3459 km in Andhra \nPradesh. development of National Highway to two to six lane standard is taken up \nin\t a\t phased\t manner\t depending\t on\t traffic\t volume,\t availability\t of\t funds\t and\t inter se \npriority of works. \n(f) In Andhra Pradesh, 505 km length for preparation of detailed Project Report \n(dPR) and 670 km length for implementation to four/six laning has been taken up. \nRoad projects in NE region\n835. sHRI AJA y  PRATAP sINgH: w ill the Minister of RoAd TRANsPoRT \nANd HIgHw Ays be pleased to state:\n(a) the details of the steps taken by government for the development of the \nNorth-eastern region;\n\nWritten Answers to [11 february, 2019]   Unstarred Questions 207\n(b) whether it is a fact that government is planning to make a network of roads \nfor the development of the North-eastern region; and\n(c) if so, the details thereof?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of RoAd TRANsPoRT ANd  \nHIgHw Ays (sHRI MANsuKH MANdA vIy A): (a) to (c) Ministry is primarily \nresponsible for development and maintenance of National Highways in the country. \nMinistry has already taken up massive road development programme in North-eastern \nregion under special Accelerated Road development Programme-North east (sARdP-\nNe). under this programme 2910 km road has already been developed and 3508 km \nis\t under\t implementation.\t Besides\t SARDP-NE,\t 5507\t km\t road\t has\t been\t identified\t for\t\ndevelopment under Bharatmala Pariyojana in North-eastern Region.\nStatus of Highway Projects\n836. sHRI TIRuCHI sIv A: w ill the Minister of RoAd TRANsPoRT ANd \nHIgHw Ays be pleased to state:\n(a) whether government has taken steps to ensure that all land and environmental \nclearances are obtained before awarding highway projects to concessionaires;\n(b) if not, the reasons therefor;\n(c) whether government is taking any step to establish a dispute redressal \nmechanism for clearing stressed or delayed road projects; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of RoAd TRANsPoRT ANd  \nHIgHw Ays (sHRI MANsuKH MANdA vIy A): (a) and (b) The process of awarding \nthe contract involves various stages which includes appointment of consultant for dPR \npreparation,\tfinalization\tof\t DPRs,\tappraisal\tof\t the\tproject\tby\t the\tCompetent\tAuthorities,\t\ninvitation\t of\t bids\t and\t finalization\t of\t successful\t bidders.\t Further,\t the\t projects\t are\t\nawarded based on the preparedness in terms of pre-construction activities mainly in \nland acquisition. As per current stipulation, projects are appraised by the Competent \nAuthority\tafter\t 3D\t is\t notified\tfor\t a\t substantial\tportion\tof\t land\t and\t compensation\taward\t\nis declared for majority of the acquired land. Also, as per Concession Agreement,  \n80% of the land in respect of BoT projects and 90 per cent of the length for ePC \nprojects should be available for declaration of the appointed date.\nAs\t a\t consequence\t of\t the\t MoEF\t modified\t guidelines\t and\t also\t the\t modification\t\nof criteria for obtaining environment clearance, there is no longer a requirement of \nenvironment Clearance (eC) for a large number of projects. \n\n208 Written Answers to [RAJyA sABHA] Unstarred Questions\n(c) and (d) following the decisions of the Cabinet Committee on economic \nAffairs (CCeA) and the provisions contained in the Arbitration and Conciliation \nAct, 1996, with the approval of NHAI Board, two nos. of Conciliation Committee \nof Independent experts (CCIe-1 and CCIe-2) have been constituted. Till date 19 \ncases have been referred to and dealt by CCIe, out of which 6 conciliation failed, \n8 succeeded and 5 are ongoing.\nConversion of fuel driven vehicles into electric vehicles\n837. sHRI lAl  sINH vAdodIA: w ill the Minister of RoAd TRANsPoRT \nANd HIgHw Ays be pleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that government is seriously contemplating to convert \nthe petrol and diesel run government vehicles into electric run vehicles; \n(b) if so, whether government has taken any step in this regard, so far; and\n(c) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of RoAd TRANsPoRT ANd  \nHIgHw Ays (sHRI MANsuKH MANdA vIy A): (a) to (c) The Ministry of finance, \nin its advisory dated 22nd december, 2018, to all the Ministries/departments have \nwritten that they may aim to replace the petrol and diesel cars hired by Ministries/\nDepartments\t in\t their\t Secretariats\t and\t attached\t offices\t (located\t in\t Delhi)\t through\t\ncontractors by electric cars for mobility in delhi. In cases where existing contracts \nfor hiring of petrol/diesel vehicles have come to an end, Ministries/departments \nmay consider fresh contract for hiring electric vehicles. further, the Ministry of \nRoad\t Transport\t and\t Highways\t had\t notified\t GSR\t 629(E)\t dated\t 24th\t June,\t 2016\t for\t\nretro-fitment\t of\t hybrid\t electric\t system\t or\t electric\t kit\t to\t vehicles\t running\t on\t petrol\t\nand diesel and s.o. 1013(e) dated 15th April, 2015 specifying the type approval \nprocedure of electric hybrid vehicles. \nAverage length of constructed roads\n838. sHRI NARA y AN lAl  PANCHARIy A: w ill the Minister of RoAd  \nTRANsPoRT ANd HIgHw Ays be pleased to state:\n(a) whether government has maintained any data relating to average length of \nroads constructed per day, during the last three years;\n(b) if so, details thereof and if not, reasons therefor;\n(c) whether the rate of construction of roads per day has increased during the \nlast three years as compared to the period from 2009-2014; and\n(d) if so, details thereof and if not, reasons therefor?\n\nWritten Answers to [11 february, 2019]   Unstarred Questions 209\nTHe  MINI sT e R of sTAT e  IN THe  MINI sTRy  of R oAd TRANsPoRT \nANd HIgHw Ays (sHRI MANsuKH MANdA vIy A): (a) to (d) The average per \nday construction of National Highways during the last three years and increment in \ncomparison to the 2009-14 is as follows:–\nsl. \nNo.\nyear length \nConstructed \n(km)\nRate\n(km per \nday)\nAverage \nper day \nconstruction \nPer centage \nincrement\n1. 2009-10 5145 14.0\n13.47\n+ 52.34%\n2. 2010-11 4439 12.6\n3. 2011-12 5013 13.73\n4. 2012-13 5732 15.71\n5. 2013-14 4260 11.67\n6. 2014-15 4410 16.61\n20.52\n7. 2015-16 6061 12.08\n8. 2016-17 8231 22.55\n9. 2017-18 9829 26.93\n10. 2018-19 as on dec., 2018 6715 24.42\nMaintenance and modernisation of Rural Roads\n839. sHRI PARIMAl  NATHw ANI: w ill the Minister of RoAd TRANsPoRT \nANd HIgHw Ays be pleased to state:\n(a) whether any proposal from state governments of Jharkhand and gujarat \nregarding maintenance and modernisation of roads in the rural areas is lying pending \nwith Central government; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether government is considering the aforesaid proposals; \n(d) if so, the details along with the current status thereof; and \n(e) if not, the reasons therefor?\nTHe  MINI sT e R of sTAT e  IN THe  MINI sTRy  of R oAd TRANsPoRT \nANd HIgHw Ays (sHRI MANsuKH MANdA vIy A): (a) to (e) The Maintenance \nof Rural Roads is state’s subject and this Ministry does not provide any funds \nfor maintenance of roads in rural area. The information regarding maintenance \nand modernisation of roads in rural areas under jurisdiction of Ministry of Rural \n\n210 Written Answers to [RAJyA sABHA] Unstarred Questions\ndevelopment is as follows:–\n(i) gujarat state government has moved to PMgsy -II during 2013-14 after \ngetting all sanctions under PMgsy - I. A target length of 1,205 Km has been \nallocated to gujarat state under PMgsy -II for 12th five year Plan Period. \nout of 1,205 Km. Ministry had already sanctioned proposal to a length of \n1,180.31 Km under PMgsy -II. No proposal is pending with Ministry for \nsanction in respect of gujarat state under PMgsy -I and PMgsy -II. \n(ii) Jharkhand has sent a proposal for upgradation of 140 roads for a length \nof 1,443.35 Km at a cost of ` 1,314.51 crores vide letter dated 06.02.2019 \nunder PMgsy -II. Pre empowered Committee meeting has been scheduled \non 21.02.2019 to consider this proposal. \nStatus of NH development in Kerala\n840. sHRI K. K. RAgesH: w ill the Minister of RoAd TRANsPoRT ANd \nHIgHw Ays be pleased to state:\n(a) the detailed status report of various National Highway development projects \nin Kerala;\n(b)\t details\t of\t time-frame,\t if\t any,\t set\t for\t the\t tenders,\t notifications\t progress\t and\t\ncompletion of development projects in Kerala; and\n(c) the reasons for delay, if any, in the progress of the NH development projects \nin Kerala? \nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of RoAd TRANsPoRT ANd  \nHIg HwAys  (s HRI MANsu KH MANd Av IyA): (a) and (b) d etails of various ongoing \nNational Highway development projects in Kerala with their target for completion is \ngiven in the statement (See below). Tenders for various works are invited only after \nfinalisation\t of\t Detailed\t Project\t Report\t (DPR),\t completion\t of\t land\t acquisition\t process,\t\nstatutory clearances and complete assessment of utility shifting execution plan in \naccordance with this Ministry’s guidelines. further, various timelines are set out as \nper standard Request for Proposal (RfP) and Model Contract/Concession documents \nfor respective mode of delivery. \n(c) The delay in progress of projects is mainly on account of delay in acquisition \nof land, delay in permission for felling of trees, shifting of utilities, delays in permission \nfor extracting borrow earth and public protest demanding vehicle underpasses (vuP)/ \nPedestrian underpasses (PuP) which are technically not feasible. \n\nWritten Answers to [11 february, 2019]   Unstarred Questions 211\nStatement\nDetails of various ongoing National Highway development projects in Kerala\nsl. \nNo.\n     Name of Project length\n(km)\nTotal Cost\n(in crore)\nAppointed \ndate\nschedule/\nlikely date of \ncompletion\nPhysical \nprogress\n(in %)\n1        2 3 4 5 6 7\n1. 6 laning of vadakkanchery (Km. 240.000) to Thrissur (Km. \n270.000) on NH-544\n28.355 910.28 15.09.2012 14.03.2015/ \n31.03.2019\n82.87\n2. 4 laning of Thiruvananthapuram Bypass from Mukkola to Kerala/\nTamil Nadu Border (Km. 26.500 to Km. 43.000) on NH-66\n16.202 1435.43 15.06.2016 14.06.2018/ \n14.04.2019\n54.86\n3. 4 l aning of Thalassery-Mahe Bypass from Km. 170.600 to \nKm.89.200 on NH-66\n18.6 1181 04.2.2017 31.05.2020 12\n4. 6-laning of Kozhikode Bypass from Km. 230.400 to  \nKm. 258.800 on NH-66\n28.4 1710 w ork awarded on 26.02.2018. Agreement \nsigned on 18.04.2018. Appointed date yet \nto\t be\t fixed.\n5. RoB at Neleshwaram on NH-66 0.78 81.87 13.05.2018 21.02.2020 2\n6. elevated Highway between Kazhakkottam to Technopark Jn. \nNH-66\n2.721 317.31 w ork awarded on 25.09.2018. Agreement \nsigned on 09.10.2018. Appointed date yet \nto\t be\t fixed.\n7. Construction of service Road at Mulavukad area from Ch. \nKm. 12.300 to Ch. Km. 14.900 on lHs in the four lane NH \nConnectivity to ICTT vallarpadam on NH-966A\n2.6 21.47 11.08.2018 1