T \nANd HIgHw Ays be pleased to state:\n(a) whether any guidelines have been devised by government for the regulation \nof shared cab services in the country for their better monitoring and effective use; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and\n(c) if not, the reasons therefor?\nTHe  MINI sT e R of sTAT e  IN THe  MINI sTRy  of R oAd TRANsPoRT \nANd HIgHw Ays (sHRI MANsuKH MANdA vIy A): (a) to (c) ‘Transportation by \nRoad’ is a state subject under the seventh schedule of the Constitution of India. \nThe Ministry of Road Transport and Highways had constituted a Committee to \nformulate taxi policy guidelines. The Committee submitted the report proposing taxi \npolicy guidelines to promote urban mobility in the month of december, 2016. The \nreport has been accepted by the government of India and shared with the states. \nCopy of the report is available on the website of the Ministry of Road Transport \nand Highways (www.morth.nic.in).\nBengaluru Peripheral Ring Road Project\n848. sHRI K. C. RAMAMuRTHy : w ill the Minister of RoAd TRANsPoRT \nANd HIgHw Ays be pleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that the Ministry has agreed to assist the Bengaluru \nPeripheral Ring Road Project (BPRR); \n(b) if so, the reasons as to why the Ministry has changed its view and decided \nto fund the satellite Town Ring Road (sTRR) instead of BPRR; \n(c) whether it is a fact that the Chief Minister of Karnataka has written to \nCentral government for assistance to BPRR; and \n(d) if so, reasons as to why Ministry can not fund both BPRR and sTRR? \nTHe  MINI sT e R of sTAT e  IN THe  MINI sTRy  of R oAd TRANsPoRT \nANd HIgHw Ays (sHRI MANsuKH MANdA vIy A): (a) No sir. \n(b) The development of satellite Town Ring Road (sTRR) as part of newly \ndeclared\t National\t Highway\t (NH)-948A\t is\t taken\t up\t for\t improving\t the\t efficiency\t of\t\nlogistic support under Bharatmala programme.\n(c) y es, sir.\n\nWritten Answers to [11 february, 2019]   Unstarred Questions 223\n(d) This Ministry is primarily responsible for development and maintenance of \nNational Highways and development of state Roads are the responsibility of the \nconcerned state government.\nAirstrips on NH\n849. sHRI T. RATHINA vel: \n  sHRI susHIl  KuMAR guPTA: \nw ill the Minister of RoAd TRANsPoRT ANd HIgHw Ays be pleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that government plans to set up 29 airstrips on National \nHighways\t at\t strategic\t locations\t for\t emergency\t landings\t by\t fighter\t aircrafts;\n(b) if so, the details thereof along with names of highways proposed to be \nselected for the purpose;\n(c) whether it is also a fact that on some highways, stretches will be made in \na manner that allows combat aircraft to land in case of emergencies; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of RoAd TRANsPoRT ANd  \nHIgHw Ays (sHRI MANsuKH MANdA vIy A): (a) and (b) No sir. However, there \nare 11 emergency landing airstrips on NHs which are entrusted to NHAI. details \nare given in the statement (See below).\n(c) y es sir.\n(d) details at sl. No. (b) above.\nStatement\nDetails of Emergency Landing Strips on the National Highways\nsl. \nNo.\nProposed stretches by \nAir\t HQ\nRemarks estimated \nCost\n(INR in \ncrore)\nlikely \ndate of \ncompletion\n1 2 3 4 5\n1. Bijbehera and Chinar \nBagh\nBids are being issued yet to be \nfinalised\n-\n2. Banihal-srinagar Kept on hold after discussion with \nlocal IAf unit\nyet to be \nfinalised\n-\n3. Kharagpur and \nBalasore\nAward of the work in process. 109.34 -\n\n224 Written Answers to [RAJyA sABHA] Unstarred Questions\n1 2 3 4 5\n4. Phalodi and Jaisalmer Kept in abeyance due to wildlife \nissue \n48.6 -\n5. Barmer and Jaisalmer Kept in abeyance due to land \nissues\n36.87 -\n6. dwarka and Maliya Contract Agreement signed on \n28.1.2018\n65.25 \n(Contract \nAmount)\n27.1.2019\n7. Chennai and \nPuducherry (Closer to \nPuducherry)\nloA being issued 89.52 -\n8. Kodikkarai and Rama \nnathpuram (closer to \nRamanathpuram)\nstate govt. has expressed inability \nto handover highway till 2021\nyet to be \nfinalized\n-\n9. Nellore-ongole Contract Agreement signed on \n20.08.2018\n66.88 \n(Contract \nAmount)\n19.08.2019\n10. ongole-Chilakaluripet loA being issued 75.8 -\n11. Kharagpur-Keonjhar Kept on hold after discussion with \nlocal IAf unit \n- -\nConstruction, repair and maintenance of Highways in Maharashtra\n850. dR. vIKAs MAHATMe: w ill the Minister of RoAd TRANsPoRT ANd \nHIgHw Ays be pleased to state:\n(a) the target set by government, in kilometres, for construction of National \nHighways\t during\t the\t financial\t year\t 2018-19;\t\n(b) the average daily pace of construction of highways and whether this pace \nis in line with the average daily speed set to achieve the target; if not, the reasons \ntherefore; \n(c) the length in kilometres, of highways passing through Maharashtra; \n(d) the details of the funds allocated to Maharashtra in each of the last three \nyears for the maintenance and repair of these highways; and \n(e) whether this allocation has been adequate for the maintenance of the highways?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of RoAd TRANsPoRT ANd  \nHIgHw Ays (sHRI MANsuKH MANdA vIy A): (a) The target set by government, \n\nWritten Answers to [11 february, 2019]   Unstarred Questions 225\nin\t kilometres,\t for\t construction\t of\t National\t Highways\t during\t the\t financial\t year\t \n2018-19 is 10,000 Km.\n(b) The average daily pace of construction of highways is 24.42 km. per day \nas on 31.2.2018, which is in line with the average daily speed set to achieve the \ntarget.\n(c) The length of National Highways passing through Maharashtra is about 17756 \nkms. \n(d) The details of the funds allocated to Maharashtra in each of the last three \nyears for the maintenance and repair of these highways is as under:–\nsl. No. year fund Allocated for maintenance and repair\n(` in crore)\n1. 2017-18 234.21\n2. 2016-17 326.01\n3. 2015-16 225.55\n(e) The allocation made by the government for the maintenance of highways \nis not adequate keeping in view of nature and extent of damages, overall condition \nof\t National\t Highways\t stretches,\t traffic\t levels,\t desired\t service\t levels\t etc.\t However,\t\nefforts\t are\t being\t made\t to\t keep\t the\t highways\t in\t traffic\t worthy\t condition.\nRegistering and licensing authority under Motor Vehicles Act\n851. sHRI B.K. HARIPRAsAd : w ill the Minister of RoAd TRANsPoRT ANd \nHIgHw Ays be pleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that the registering and licensing authority presently lies \nwith\t the\t State\t Government\t and\t the\t power\t of\t State\t Government\t in\t fixing\t jurisdiction\t\nis being removed in Motor vehicles (Amendment) Bill, 2017; and\n(b) if so, the details thereof including the major issues raised against the bill \nby the stakeholders and state representatives?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of RoAd TRANsPoRT ANd  \nHIgHw Ays (sHRI MANsuKH MANdA vIy A): (a) and (b) The power of both \nregistering and licensing authority under the Motor vehicles Act, 1988 vest with the \nstate government. No such amendment of removal of powers of state government \nin\t fixing\t jurisdiction\t of\t registering\t and\t licensing\t authority\t is\t being\t proposed\t in\t \nthe Bill.\n\n226 Written Answers to [RAJyA sABHA] Unstarred Questions\nConstruction of Highways\n852. sHRI susHI l  KuMAR guPTA: w ill the Minister of R oAd TRANsPoRT \nANd HIgHw Ays be pleased to state:\n(a)\t whether\t it\t is\t a\t fact\t that\t the\t Ministry\t had\t fixed\t a\t target\t of\t constructing\t more\t\nthan 16,000 kilometres of roads in the year 2018-19;\n(b) if so, the target achieved so far; and \n(c) details of steps being taken to achieve this target?\nTHe  MINI sT e R of sTAT e  IN THe  MINI sTRy  of R oAd TRANsPoRT \nANd HIgHw Ays (sHRI MANsuKH MANdA vIy A): (a) to (c) Ministry has set the \ntarget\tof\t 10000\t km\t for\t construction\tof\t National\tHighways\tin\t the\t current\tfinancial\tyear\t\n2018-19 and achieved 6715 km as on 31st dec., 2018 in fy  2018-19. To expedite \ncompletion of NHs projects various steps are taken which include streamlining of \nland acquisition and environment clearances, premium re-scheduling, close coordination \nwith other Ministries, revamping of dispute resolution mechanism, frequent reviews \nat various levels etc. In order to ensure timely completion of the projects, regular \nmeetings are held with project developers, state governments and contractors at all \nlevels.\nReduction in road accidents\n853. sHRI P. l. PuNIA: w ill the Minister of RoAd TRANsPoRT ANd  \nHIgHw Ays be pleased to state:\n(a) the total number of road accidents and resultant deaths and injuries in 2018, \nstate-wise; \n(b)\t whether\t a\t fifty\t per\t cent\t reduction\t in\t road\t accidents\t can\t be\t achieved\t by\t\n2020; \n(c) if so, steps taken by government to ensure the same; and \n(d) if not, the reasons for not being able to meet the target? \nTHe  MINI sT e R of sTAT e  IN THe  MINI sTRy  of R oAd TRANsPoRT \nANd HIgHw Ays (sHRI MANsuKH MANdA vIy A): (a) The Ministry collects data/\ninformation on road accidents from the all state/uTs. They provide data/information \nto this Ministry in the prescribed format on road accidents on all roads including \nNational Highways. The latest compiled data of road accidents available from the \nstates/uTs is for the calendar year 2017.\n\nWritten Answers to [11 february, 2019]   Unstarred Questions 227\n(b) to (d) united Nationals organisation has set the target to reduce road accidents \nand road accidents fatalities by 50% by 2020. The government is also committed \nto achieve the target set by united Nationals organisation along with other member \ncountries. The Ministry of Road Transport and Highways has taken a number of \nsteps to prevent such road accidents as per details mentioned under:–\n(i) government has launched a mobile app for highway users i.e.  \n“sukhady atra 1033” which enables highways users to report potholes \nand other safety hazards on National Highways including accidents.\n(ii)\t Rectification\t of\t identified\t black\t spots.\n(iii) Road safety Audits are being carried out at all stages of development \nof the highway i.e. design/construction/operation stages.\n(iv) facilities such as foot over bridges and under passes are being \nprovided wherever required, for safe crossing of pedestrians and other \nroad users.\n(v) Road safety w eek is observed every calendar year for spreading \nawareness about safer behaviour of road users on National Highways.\n(vi) The government has approved a National Road safety Policy. This \nPolicy outlines various policy measures such as promoting awareness, \nestablishing road safety information data base, encouraging safer road \ninfrastructure including application of intelligent transport, enforcement \nof safety laws.\n(vii) The Ministry has constituted group of Ministers of state Transport \nMinisters to examine the best practices of Transport and suggest issues \nto improve road safety. Based on the recommendation of group of \nMinisters, the Ministry introduced Motor vehicle (Amendment) Bill \n2017 covering entire gamut of road safety. \n(viii) The Ministry has formulated a multi-pronged strategy to address the \nissue of road safety based on 4 ‘e’s viz. education, engineering (both \nof roads and vehicles), enforcement and emergency Care. \n(ix) Road safety has been made an integral part of road design at planning \nstage. \n(x) The threshold for four laning of National Highway has been reduced \nfrom 15,000 Passenger Car units (PCus) to 10,000 PCus. About \n52,000\t km.\t of\t stretches\t of\t State\t Highways\t has\t been\t identified\t for\t\nconversion to national highways.\n(xi) setting up of model driving training institutes in states and refresher \ntraining to drivers of Heavy Motor vehicle in the unorganised sector.\n\n228 Written Answers to [RAJyA sABHA] Unstarred Questions\n(xii) Advocacy/Publicity campaign on road safety through the electronic \nand print media. \n(xiii) Tightening of safety standards for vehicles like seat Belts, anti-lock \nbraking system etc.\n(xiv)\t High\t priority\t has\t been\t accorded\t to\t identification\t and\t rectification\t of\t\nblack spots (accident prone spots) on National Highways.\n(xv) As a measure of supplementing the efforts of states/uTs for minimizing \nthe\t accident\t potential\t at\t the\t identified\t locations/stretches\t through\t\nengineering improvement on state roads, Ministry of Road Transport \nand Highways had taken a decision to sanction road safety works on \nstate roads with an earmarked allocation of 10% of funds allocated \nto the state roads under Central Road fund. \n(xvi)\t Ministry\t has\t delegated\t powers\t to\t Regional\t Officers\t of\t MoRTH\t for\t\ntechnical\tapproval\tto\t the\t detailed\testimates\tfor\t rectification\tof\t identified\t\nRoad\t Accident\t black\t spots\t for\t expediting\t the\t rectification\t process\t to\t\nensure safety of road users.\n(xvii) Ministry had issued guidelines  vide o.M. dated 14.1.2016 for taking \nup of Road safety Audits on National Highways either as part of \nePC/BoT projects or as stand-alone Road safety Audits.\n(xviii) guidelines for pedestrian facilities on National Highways for persons \nwith disabilities have also been issued to all states/uTs. \n(xix) A\t Certification\t Course\t for\t Road\t Safety\t Auditors\t has\t been\t commenced\t\nin Indian Academy of Highway engineers (IAHe) and 42 Auditors \nare\t certified.\t\n(xx) Ministry of Road Transport and Highways has constituted a district \nRoad safety Committee in each district of the country to promote \nawareness amongst road users under the chairmanship of Hon’ble \nMember of Parliament (lok sabha) from the district.\n(xxi) free eye Check-up Camp and distribution of eye glasses is conducted \nfor truck/bus drivers operating on NH.\n(xxii)\t Capacity\t building\t through\t training\t of\t NHAI\t field\tstaff/Concessionaires/\nContractors/Consultants engaged in NHAI project works throughout \nIndia.\n(xxiii) facilities such as foot over bridges and underpasses are being provided \nwherever required, for safe crossing of pedestrians and other road \nusers.\n\nWritten Answers to [11 february, 2019]   Unstarred Questions 229\n(xxiv) Removal of liquor shops as per directions of Hon’ble supreme Court \nvide circular of f . No. R w/NH-33044/309/2016/s&R dated 06.04.2017 \nand 01.06.2017.\nComplaints against poor maintenance of National Highways\n854. sHRI sANTIuse KuJuR: w ill the Minister of RoAd TRANsPoRT ANd \nHIgHw Ays be pleased to state:\n(a) whether government has received many complaints against poor maintenance \nof National Highways (NHs) during the last three years and the current year, if so, \nthe details thereof, state-wise;\n(b) the remedial steps taken/being taken by government in this regard;\n(c) the details of the proposals approved by government received from various \nstate governments including Assam for the maintenance of National Highways (NHs); \nand\n(d) the details of pending proposals, if any, along with the reasons therefor and \nby when the same are likely to be cleared, state-wise?\nTHe  MINI sT e R of sTAT e  IN THe  MINI sTRy  of R oAd TRANsPoRT \nANd HIgHw Ays (sHRI MANsuKH MANdA vI yA): (a) and (b) Condition of \nthe\t highways\t is\t checked\t and\t maintained\t regularly\t by\t the\t field\t officers\t and\t regional\t\nofficers\t and\t Branch\t Officers\t of\t the\t Ministry/National\t Highway\t Authority\t of\t India/\t\nNational Highways and Infrastructure d evelopment Corporation. o n completed sections, \nrepair and maintenance is carried out by operation and Maintenance contractors/BoT \nconcessionaires as part of their obligations in the contract. stretches entrusted to state \nPwd/NHAI/NHIdCl  but where work is yet to start are maintained either directly \nby NHAI/NHId Cl  or through state Pwd. where concessionaire fails to repair roads \nas per concession agreement, state Pwd/NHAI/NHIdCl  undertakes repairing work \nat risk and cost of the concessionaire. \n(c) and (d) The state-wise details of the proposals received and approved for the \nmaintenance of National Highways (NHs) including Assam is given in the statement \n(See below).\t Balance\t proposals\t is\t likely\t to\t get\t approved\t by\t the\t end\t of\t this\t financial\t\nyear depending upon the availability of funds and inter-se priority. \n\n230 Written Answers to [RAJyA sABHA] Unstarred Questions\nStatement\nDetails of the proposals received and approved for the maintenance of \nNHs as on 31st January in 2018-19 \nsl. \nNo.\nstate/uT Received Approved\nNos. Cost (` crore) Nos. Cost (` crore)\n1. Andhra Pradesh 138 100.84 138 100.78\n2. Arunachal Pradesh 3 47.5 0 0\n3. Assam 9 97.24 0 0\n4. Chhattisgarh 6 206.72 5 158.21\n5. gujarat 19 164.58 10 28.24\n6. Himachal Pradesh 20 70.83 20 70.25\n7. Jharkhand 7 36.7 7 2.25\n8. Karnataka 9 92.5 0 0\n9. Kerala 6 74.05 6 74.05\n10. Madhya Pradesh 4 14.2 0 0\n11. Manipur 9 32.68 0 0\n12. Meghalaya 15 188.2 10 141.58\n13. Nagaland 7 30.5 7 29.96\n14. odisha 5 14.2 0 0\n15. Punjab 6 17.56 6 17.56\n16. Rajasthan 9 39.9 9 39.9\n17. sikkim 5 68.25 3 2.25\n18. Telangana 26 102.79 7 2.8\n19. Tripura 20 33.66 11 3.55\n20. uttar Pradesh 11 125 0 0\n21. uttarakhand 10 35.8 9 26.57\n22. w est Bengal 14 153.62 6 111.6\nToT al 358 1746.9 254 809.1\nProjects in West Bengal\n855. dR. ABHIsHeK MANu sINgHvI: w ill the Minister of RoAd TRANsPoRT \nANd HIgHw Ays be pleased to state:\n(a) the list of the all the projects sanctioned by the Ministry in past three years \nin the state of w est Bengal; \n\nWritten Answers to [11 february, 2019]   Unstarred Questions 231\n(b) details of the status of those projects, along with deadlines; \n(c) details of the projects that have failed to meet the 