td. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 509\nShortage of coal in Raichur Thermal Power Station\n652. dR. l. HANuMANTHAIAH: w ill the Minister of CoAl  be pleased to \nstate:\n(a) whether it is a fact that the Raichur Thermal Power station (RTPs) has \nshortage of coal supply since two years;\n(b) if so, the reasons therefor and if not, the details thereof;\n(c) whether government has taken any steps for shortage of coal in RTPs;\n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and\n(e) the details of total quantity of coal required in power stations across the \ncountry per year, state-wise?\nTHe MINIsTeR of CoAl  (sHRI PIyusH goy Al): (a) to (d) As per Central \nelectricity Authority (CeA), Raichur Thermal Power station (RTPs) has received \ncoal more than its consumption during 2017-18 and in 2018-19 (up to 31.01.2019). \nAs per CeA report of 05.02.2019, RTPs has coal stock of 22 days. The details of \ncoal receipt and consumption in the RTPs (1,720 Mw) during 2017-18 and 2018-19 \nare as under:–\n(figures in MT)\nsl.No. year Receipt Consumption\n1. 2017-18 7.363 6.932\n2. 2018-19 (up to 31.01.2019) 5.091 4.539\nCoal supplies to power sector is monitored regularly by an Inter-Ministerial sub-\ngroup comprising representatives of Ministry of Power, Ministry of Coal, Ministry of \nRailways, Ministry of shipping, CeA, NITI Aayog, Coal India limited etc. Priority \nis given by coal companies to power sector in respect of coal dispatch.\nAs per the CeA energy Power supply position report of december, 2018 \n(Provisional) the energy power supply to the state of Karnataka during the period \nApril-december 2018 has been 51418 Mu against the energy requirement of 51483 \nMU\t which\t is\t 99.9\t %.\t Further,\t no\t notified\t power\t cuts\t have\t been\t reported\t in\t respect\t\nof Karnataka during this period.\n(e) As per CeA, the details of total quantity of coal required per year in power \nstations, state-wise are given below:–\n\n