 \nTiruvallur\nConsumer Products 19.02.2018 11.46 3.59 1.80\n17. M/s Hangrow f oods India \nPvt. ltd.\ndistrict erode Consumer Products 19.02.2018 5.17 1.00 0\n Karnataka\n1. M/s. Hain future Natural \nProducts Pvt. ltd.\nM/s Integrated Mega food Park, Tumkur f&v  Processing 19.02.2018 36.76 5.00 0\n2. M/s Petunt food Processors \nPvt. ltd.\nM/s Integrated Mega food Park, Tumkur Consumer Products 19.02.2018 20.85 5.00 0\n3. M/s delect spices and Herbs \nPvt. ltd.\nM/s Integrated Mega food Park, Tumkur Consumer Products 19.02.2018 29.15 5.00 0\n4. M/s Banashankari Chemicals \nPvt. ltd.\nM/s favorich Mega food Park, Mandya f&v  Processing 19.02.2018 14.99 4.97 0\n5. M/s ella foods M/s Innova Agri Bio Park, Malur, Kolar Consumer Products 13.04.2018 16.72 4.06 0\n6. M/s Ramkrishna Industries Navanagar Agrotech Park, Industrial \nArea, gaddanakeri Hobli Kaladgi Taluk\ngrain Milling 14.09.2018 6.76 1.95 0\n7. M/s sankraman organic \nfoods Pvt. ltd.\nM/s favorich Infra Pvt. l td., Bannenahalli, \nBookanakere Hobli, K.R. Pet Taluk\nConsumer Products 26.09.2018 7.84 1.64 0\n8. M/s R g Kasat Industries \nPvt. ltd.\nAgro Tech. Park Industrial Area, \nNavanagar\nConsumer Products 22.10.2018 9.06 2.55 0\n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 599\nUnsold food products discarded by supermarkets\n698. sHRI MAHesH Podd AR: w ill the Minister of food PRoCessINg \nINdusTRIes be pleased to state:\n(a) whether any data is available on the unsold food products discarded by \nsupermarket chains of big corporates, if so, the details thereof;\n(b) whether there is any mechanism for the donation of unsold food products of \nsupermarkets which are still suitable for consumption to charities, if so, the details \nthereof; and\n(c) if not, the mechanism for the donation of unsold food products of \nsupermarkets?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of food PRoCessINg \nINdusTRIes (sAdHvI NIRANJAN JyoTI): (a) to (c) The Ministry of food \nProcessing Industries (MofPI) is mandated to promote food processing industries \nin the country with the objectives, inter alia, to reduce wastage of harvest and \npost-harvest losses of different agricultural produce and therefore, MofPI monitors \nthe extent of losses of agricultural produce by getting studies/surveys conducted. A \nstudy conducted by Central Institute of Post-Harvest engineering and Technology \n(CIPHeT), ludhiana, Punjab has estimated the annual harvest and post-harvest losses \nof agricultural produce at ` 92,651 crore. The study was based on production data of \n2012-13 and wholesale prices of 2014. The Ministry has not collated any information \non the food products being discarded by super markets nor any proposal/mechanism \nis under consideration for the donation of these unsold food products.\nDecline in earning of foreign exchange in Food Processing Sector\n699. sHRI v. vIJA yAsAI Reddy : w ill the Minister of food PRoCessINg \nINdusTRIes be pleased to state:\n(a) the details of proposal for setting up of resource centre by CfTRI at Kurnool, \nAndhra\t Pradesh\t for\t the\t benefit\t of\t farmers\t of\t Rayalaseema\t area;\n(b) the reasons for low food processing in the country and how the country \ncompares to other countries like us, uK, China, france, etc. in this regard;\n(c) whether it is a fact that foreign exchange earned through food processing \nsector is gradually coming down from us $ 36 billion in 2012-13 to us $ 29 \nbillion in 2015-16; and\n(d) if so, the reasons behind this downtrend and in what manner Ministry is \nplanning to turn this around?\n\n600 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of food PRoCessINg \nINdusTRIes (sAdHvI NIRANJAN JyoTI): (a) CsIR-CfTRI has intimated that \nas of now, there is no proposal to establish a Resource Centre at Kurnool, Andhra \nPradesh.\n(b) The reasons for low food processing in the country are inadequate link \nbetween production and processing (lack of processable varieties), lack of product \ndevelopment and innovation, seasonality of operations and low capacity utilization, \nsupply chain institutional gaps, gaps in infrastructure of supply chain, etc. The level \nof food processing in India is around 10% as compared to nearly 20% in China \nand more than 50% in us and uK.\n(c) and (d) The value of export of food products during was us $35.89 billion \nin 2012-13 and us $ 29.67 billion in 2015-16 (Source: Ministry of Commerce and \nIndustry).\nChanges in exchange rate between Indian rupee and us d ollar, changes in \nunit price of exported item, changes in composition of exported item and changes \nin volume in export are the reasons for variation in value of export resulting in \nfluctuation\t of\t the\t foreign\t exchanges\t earnings\t during\t the\t period.\nThe department of Commerce (doC), Ministry of Commerce and Industry has the \nmandate to promote export of various products from the country including processed \nfood products. Promoting exports of processed food products is a continuous process. \nThe newly introduced Agriculture e xport Policy provides an assurance that the \nexport of processed agricultural products will not be brought under the ambit of any \nkind of export restrictions. The doC has also several schemes to promote exports, \nincluding exports of processed food products, viz. Trade Infrastructure for export \nscheme (TIes), Market Access Initiatives (MAI) scheme etc. Incentive on export of \nvarious value added agricultural/processed food items is provided to exporters under \nthe Merchandise exports from India scheme (MeIs). In addition, assistance to the \nexporters of value added agricultural/processed food items is also available under \nthe export Promotion schemes of Agricultural and Processed food Products export \ndevelopment Authority (APedA), Tea Board, Coffee Board and spices Board. These \norganisations also seek to promote exports through participation in international fairs \nand exhibitions, taking initiatives to gain market access for different products in \ndifferent markets, dissemination of market intelligence, taking steps to ensure quality \nof exported products etc.\ndue to efforts taken by the government the value of export of food products \nhas increased to us $30.87 billion in the year 2016-2017 and us $35.47 billion in \nthe year 2017-18 (Source: Ministry of Commerce and Industry).\n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 601\nResearch and Development in FPIs\n700. sHRI K.C. RAMAMuRTHy : w ill the Minister of food PRoCessINg \nINdusTRIes be pleased to state:\n(a) the reason why funds allocated for Research and development in food \nprocessing sector has come down form ` 10.50 crore in 2012-13 to ` 5.80 crore \nin 2016-17:\n(b) the status of implementation of Research and development since April, 2017 \nby the Ministry: and\n(c) the details of targets set and achieved since 2017?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of food PRoCessINg \nINdusTRIes (sAdHvI NIRANJAN JyoTI): (a) The scheme for Research and \ndevelopment (R&d) in food processing sector was implemented through science \nand engineering Research Board (seRB) of department of science and Technology \nfrom 2012-13 to 2016-17. Before transferring the implementation of the scheme to \nseRB, the R&d scheme was directly implemented by the Ministry. In the beginning \nof the year 2012-13, the Ministry had also some outstanding liabilities of already \napproved projects before 2012-13. The seRB had also started implementation of \nthe scheme and sanctioned new R&d projects during 2012-13. Keeping in view the \noutstanding liabilities of already approved projects by the Ministry and to meet the \nrequirement of funds of seRB projects, the allocation of the budget for 2012-13 was \nkept at ` 10.50 crore. The budget allocation of subsequent years was kept as per the \nrequirement of funds for seRB approved projects and to meet remaining outstanding \nliabilities of the approved projects of the Ministry. The outstanding liabilities of the \nprojects approved by Ministry before 2012-13 declined subsequently. Accordingly, the \nallocation of budget during the subsequent years under R&d scheme was reduced.\n(b) and (c) The Ministry has taken a fresh look at the implementation of the \nscheme in accordance with deliberations in the Parliamentary Consultative Committee \nfor Ministry of food Processing Industries in its meeting held on 04.08.2016. It was \ndecided to implement the R&d scheme directly by the Ministry of food Processing \nIndustries and from 1st April, 2017 onwards, the implementation of R&d scheme was \ntaken back from seRB to MofPI. Accordingly, the scheme guidelines were revised \nand issued on 10.08.2017. As per revised scheme guidelines, expert Committee \n(eC) for scrutinizing and reviewing of new R&d proposals had been constituted on \n13.09.2017. The scheme is open ended. for receipt of proposals under the scheme \na notice was put up on website of Ministry for receipt of fresh R&d proposals for \nsanction. After revision of scheme guidelines, so far three meetings of eC have been \nconvened to considered new R&d proposals received in the Ministry.\n\n602 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\nThe scheme for Research and development in food processing sector is now a \ncomponent of scheme for Human Resource and Institutions under the umbrella scheme \nof the Ministry i.e. Kisan sAMPAdA yojana. The scheme is being implemented \nwith an allocation of ` 50 crore to meet the committed liability of existing on-\ngoing projects and taking up of 100 new projects till 2020. After starting of Kisan \nsAMPAdA yojana, the Ministry has approved 32 new R&d projects as on 04.02.2019 \nwith approved grant-in-aid of ` 12.65 crore.\nSharing of infrastructure between food retail and e-commerce\n701. sHRI HIsHey  lACHuNgPA: w ill the Minister of food PRoCessINg \nINdusTRIes be pleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that the government is planning to relax riders in food \nretail with regard to sharing of infrastructure between food retail and e-commerce; and\n(b) if so, the details thereof;\n(c) whether government has done any assessment of likely effect of this move \non the small food growers and sellers in the market;\n(d) if so, the details thereof; and\n(e) if not, the reasons therefor?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of food PRoCessINg \nINdusTRIes (sAdHvI NIRANJAN JyoTI): (a) and (b) department for Promotion of \nIndustry and Internal Trade (dPIIT) has the mandate to promote retail trade including \nretail trade in food products. According to dPIIT, there is no such proposal presently \nunder consideration. However, as per extant fdI policy, 100% fdI under government \napproval route has been permitted for trading, including through e-commerce, in respect \nof food products manufactured and/or produced in India subject to sectoral guidelines.\n(c) to (e) do not arise.\nSuggestion of EC on NOTA  votes\n702. sHRI PRABHAT JHA: w ill the Minister of lA w  ANd JusTICe be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether the election Commission has given any suggestion to government \nunder which there should be such a provision that if there are more votes for NoTA \nas\t compared\t to\t the\t winning\t candidate,\t then\t that\t poll\t would\t be\t nullified\t and\t fresh\t\npolls would be conducted;\n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 603\n(c) whether government has taken/is taking initiatives in this regard; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of lA w  ANd JusTICe \n(sHRI P. P. CHAudHAR y): (a) No, sir.\n(b) to (d) do not arise. \nState funding of elections\n703. dR. sANTANu seN: w ill the Minister of lA w  ANd JusTICe be pleased \nto state: \n(a) the manner in which the election Commission proposes to crack down \nagainst black money;\n(b) whether there is any plan for state funding of elections; and\n(c) if so, by when it would happen, if not, the reasons therefor?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of lA w  ANd JusTICe \n(sHRI P. P. CHAudHAR y): (a) The election Commission has informed that it has \ndeveloped a robust mechanism on election expenditure Monitoring and has issued \ncomprehensive instructions for control of money power during elections which, inter \nalia, include appointment of e xpenditure observers, constitution of surveillance Teams \nto keep vigil over movement of cash linked with candidates or political parties or \nitems\t of\t bribe\t meant\t for\t inducement\t of\t electors,\t formation\t of\t Media\t Certification\t\nand Monitoring Committees to keep a watch on media advertisements and suspected \ncases of paid news and maintenance of shadow observation registers and folders of \nevidence in respect of the contesting candidates to monitor day to day expenditure \nincurred by them. deployment of v ideo surveillance Teams to oversee major items \nof expenditure, opening of separate bank account by the candidates for the purpose \nof election expenses, involvement of the Income Tax department to keep vigil over \nmovement\t of\t cash\t through\t the\t airports,\t hotels,\t financial\t brokers\t etc.\t during\t the\t entire\t\nelection process, are some of the other steps taken in this regard. As per standard \noperating Procedures (soP) laid down by the Commission, flying squads and static \nsurveillance Teams deployed in elections are authorized to seize cash exceeding  \n` 50,000, including other inducements like drugs, liquor, arms and other illicit articles \nor gift items valued at more than ` 10,000, if found in vehicles carrying candidate, \nhis agent, party worker, posters and election materials.\n\n604 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\n(b) and (c) The law Commission which examined the issue of electoral reforms \nin its entirety has not recommended state funding of elections.\nFraming of rules and regulations after passing of Acts\n704. sHRI. C. M. RAMesH: w ill the Minister of lA w  ANd JusTICe be \npleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that many Ministries/departments of the Central \ngovernment have not framed rules/regulations pursuant to passing of those Acts by \nParliament, if so, the details thereof, date and year-wise; and \n(b) whether government proposes to issue guidelines in this regard, making it \nmandatory for all the Ministries/ departments of the Central government by giving \nthem three months’ time-limit to frame the rules/ regulations, if so, the details thereof \nand if not, the reasons therefor?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of lA w  ANd JusTICe \n(sHRI P. P. CHAudHAR y): (a) The information is being collected and will be laid \non the Table of the House.\n(b) As per Para 11.3.1 of the Manual of Parliamentary Procedures in the \ngovernment of India, the statutory rules, regulations and bye-laws have to be framed \nby Ministries/departments within a period of six months from the date on which \nthe relevant statute came into force. There is no proposal to reduce this time limit \nto three months. \nSalary and perquisites of Judges\n705. sHRI PARIMAl  NATHw ANI: w ill the Minister of lA w  ANd JusTICe \nbe pleased to state:\n(a) the number of vacancies of Judges in various High Courts, state-wise, \nincluding gujarat and Jharkhand, as well as the supreme Court of country; \n(b) the number of pending cases, both criminal and civil, in High Courts,  \nstate-wise and the supreme Court of the country; \n(c) the steps taken by government in last four years to ease stress and burden \nof higher judiciary in the country; and\n(d) whether government increased salaries and perquisites of Judges in the \ncountry to raise their living standard to bring it at par with judiciary in the developed \ncountries, the details thereof?\n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 605\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of lA w  ANd JusTICe  \n(sHRI P. P. CHAudHAR y):  (a) to (d) The details of showing the number of \nvacancies of Judges in various High Courts are given in statement-I (See below) \nThe details showing number of pending cases, (both Criminal and Civil) in the High \nCourts and supreme Court are given in statement-II (See below). The details of the \nsalaries and perquisites of Judges are given in statement-III (See below).\nfilling up of vacancies in the High Courts is a continuous and collaborative \nprocess of the Judiciary and executive involving various Constitutional Authorities. \nduring the year 2015-2018, 18 Judges have been appointed in the supreme Court \nand 384 Judges have been appointed in various High Courts. The government has \nalso increased the Judges strength of the High Courts from 906 in May, 2014 to \n1079 at present. \nTo address speedy disposal of cases and reduction in pendency of cases to improve \naccess to justice, the following interventions have been made by the government \nduring the last 4 years:–\n(i) Pursuant to the decisions taken in the Joint Conference of the Chief Ministers \nof the states and Chief Justices of the High Courts in 2015, Arrears Committee \nhas been established in the supreme Court, High Courts and district Courts \nto assign top most priority for disposal of cases which are pending for more \nthan\t five\t years.\n(ii) In April, 2015, the government has taken up with all states to improve the \nworking of the judicial system and provide speedy justice in the country.\n(iii) In september, 2016, May, 2017 and August, 2018, Minister of law and \nJustice wrote to the Chief Ministers of states and the Chief Justices of \nHigh Courts to enhance the cadre strength of the district and subordinate \njudiciary and to monitor the status of the vacancies regularly.\ngovernment has taken the following initiatives to provide an ecosystem for faster \ndisposal of cases by the judiciary. \n1. The National Mission for Justice d elivery and legal Reforms has been \nestablished.\n2.\t Improving\t infrastructure\t[court\t halls\t and\t residential\tunits]\t for\t Judicial\tOfficers\t\nof district and subordinate Courts.\n3. leveraging Information and Communication Technology (ICT) for better \njustice delivery.\n\n606 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\n4. emphasis on Alternate dispute Resolution (AdR) to fast track special type \nof cases.\nStatement-I\nDetails of showing the number of vacancies of Judges in various High Courts\nsl. \nNo. \nName of the Court sanctioned \nstrength of Judges \nw orking strength \nof Judges\nvacancies\n1 2 3 4 5\nA supreme Court of India 31 28 03\nB High Court\n1. Allahabad 160 108 52\n2. Andhra Pradesh 37 13 24\n3. Bombay 94 70 24\n4. Calcutta 72 36 36\n5. Chhattisgarh 22 15 07\n6. delhi 60 38 22\n7. gauhati 24 20 04\n8. gujarat 52 28 24\n9. Himachal Pradesh 13 08 05\n10. Jammu and Kashmir 17 09 08\n11. Jharkhand 25 18 07\n12. Karnataka 62 31 31\n13. Kerala 47 37 10\n14. Madhya Pradesh  53 34 19\n15. Madras 75 60 15\n16. Manipur 05 03 02\n17. Meghalaya 04 03 01\n18. odisha 27 15 12\n19. Patna 53 27 26\n20. Punjab and Haryana 85 53 32\n21. Rajasthan 50 25 25\n22. sikkim 03 03 0\n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 607\n1 2 3 4 5\n23. Telangana 24 13 11\n24. Tripura 04 03 01\n25. uttarakhand 11 09 02\nToT al 1079 679 400\nStatement-II\nDetails of showing number of pending cases, (both Criminal and Civil)  \nin the High Courts and Supreme Court of India\nsl. \nNo.\nName of Court Total Pending Cases–\nCivil, Criminal, w rit\n1     2 3\n(A) supreme Court of India 58029 (As on 01.02.2019)\n(B) High Courts As on 05.02.2019\n1. Allahabad High Court 727145\n2. Calcutta High Court 20677\n3. gauhati High Court 41039\n4. Bombay High Court 267809\n5. Chhattisgarh High