y is also not centrally \nmaintained as the subject matter falls within the domain of the High Courts and the \nstate governments.\n(d) Appointment of Judges of the High Court is made under Article 217 and \n224 of the Constitution of India. These Articles do not provide for reservation for \nany caste or class of persons including women.\nStatement\nDetails of working strength of Judges and number of women Judges in the \nSupreme Court and High Courts of India\nsl. \nNo. \nName of the High Courts w orking strength \nof Judges \nNumber of working \nw omen Judges\n1   2 3 4\nA Supreme Court 28 03\nB High Court\n1. Allahabad 108 07\n2. Andhra Pradesh 13 03\n3. Bombay 70 09\n4. Calcutta 36 07\n5. Chhattisgarh 15 02\n6. delhi 38 07\n7. gauhati 20 01\n8. gujarat 28 03\n9. Himachal Pradesh 08 00\n10. Jammu and Kashmir 09 02\n11. Jharkhand 18 01\n12. Karnataka 31 03\n13. Kerala 37 05\n14. Madhya Pradesh 34 03\n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 611\n1   2 3 4\n15. Madras 60 11\n16. Manipur 03 00\n17. Meghalaya 03 00\n18. odisha 15 01\n19. Patna 27 02\n20. Punjab and Haryana 53 07\n21. Rajasthan 25 01\n22. sikkim 03 01\n23. Telangana 13 00\n24. Tripura 03 00\n25. uttarakhand 09 00\nHigh expense of Government litigations\n707. sHRIMATI vANdANA CHAvAN: w ill the Minister of lA w  ANd JusTICe  \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) the percentage of litigations ongoing in courts that has government as a \nparty; \n(b) the percentage of the above-mentioned litigations that has been initiated by \ngovernment; \n(c)\t the\t expense\t on\t Government\t litigations\t in\t the\t last\t five\t years,\t year-wise;\t\n(d) whether there is any proposal to create a separate department in the Ministry \nto handle government litigation; and \n(e) whether the new version of the National litigation Policy has been formulated \nand if so, the details thereof, and if not, the progress till date including stakeholder \nconsultations and proposed changes from the 'National litigation Policy, 2010'?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of lA w  ANd JusTICe \n(sHRI P. P. CHAudHAR y): (a) and (b) No such data is maintained. \n(c) Consolidated data in this regard is not available. However, the Budgetary \nProvision\t in\t Ministry\t of\t Law\t and\t Justice\t for\t payment\t of\t fees\t to\t Law\t Officers/\ngovernment Advocate/Panel Counsel in supreme Court, various High Courts and \nsubordinate Courts for the past 5 year period is as follows:–\n\n612 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\nfinancial year Amount (in Rupees)\n2013-2014 26,52,01,000\n2014-2015 28,45,76,000\n2015-2016 42,77,86,000\n2016-2017 51,23,11,000\n2017-2018 68,32,39,000\n(d) No, sir. There is no such proposal.\n(e) A National litigation Policy was formulated by the government in the year \n2010. since it could not be placed before the competent authority it could not be \nimplemented. formulation of fresh National litigation Policy is under consideration \nof the government.\nCooling off period for Judges after retirement\n708. sHRI AMAR sINgH: w ill the Minister of lA w  ANd JusTICe be pleased \nto state:\n(a) whether government proposes to prescribe a cooling off period of 5 years \nafter the retirement of Judges of High Courts and the supreme Court with a view \nto secure the independence of institutions recognised in the Constitution through \nspecial protection of sC/HC Judges and election Commissioners; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and by when; and \n(c) if not, the other reforms government proposes to bring forward in this \ndirection as government is a major litigant in Courts but holds the power to offer \npost-retirement position to Judges?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of lA w  ANd JusTICe  \n(sHRI P. P. CHAudHAR y): (a) to (c) There is no proposal to prescribe a cooling \noff period of 5 years after the retirement of Judges of High Courts and supreme \nCourt. various legislations enacted by the Parliament in the country have provisions \nfor appointment of sitting/retired Judges of the supreme Court or High Courts as \nChairpersons and Members of the Commissions, Tribunals and Authorities.\nE-Courts Mission Mode Project Phase-II \n709. sHRI PARIMAl  NATHw ANI: w ill the Minister of lA w  ANd JusTICe \nbe pleased to state:\n(a) the details of the e-Courts Mission Mode Project Phase-II;\n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 613\n(b) whether government recently launched mobile application based e-services \nfor courts;\n(c) if so, the details of the services shared through this application; and \n(d) the number of applicants using this applications, state-wise and court-wise?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of lA w  ANd JusTICe \n(sHRI P. P. CHAudHAR y): (a) The government is implementing the e-Courts \nMission Mode Project with the aim of computerisation and connecting district and \nsubordinate courts across the country, in association with the e-Committee of the, \nHon'ble supreme Court of India. The details are as follows: (i) The e-Courts Mission \nMode Project Phase-II is being implemented for a period of four years (2015-19) \nor until the project is completed, whichever is later. (ii) The target set out under \nthe\t project\t is\t 20,000\t courts.\t Against\t the\t financial\t outlay\t of\t ` 1,670 crores in the \nsecond phase, ` 1,087 crores have been released, including ` 931.3 crores to High \nCourts. ` 632.3 crore have been utilised by the High Courts. \n(iii) As per information received from e-Committee of supreme Court of India, \n16845 courts have been computerized till date. The High Court-wise details of such \ndistrict and subordinate courts are given in statement (See below).\n(b) to (d) yes, sir. The government launched mobile application based e-services \nfor district and subordinate courts. e-Courts services mobile app with the facility \nof\t QR\t Code\t was\t launched\t on\t 22.07.2017\t for\t use\t of\t litigants\t and\t lawyers.\t Services\t\nunder different captions viz. search by CNR, Case status, Cause list and My Cases \nare available on this application. All the court case information services which are \navailable on e-Courts services for district Courts and High Courts are made available \nthrough mobile app. Additionally portfolio management tools are provided in mobile \napp. Portfolio Management tools include saving cases of one’s interest and generating \ncase list for selected date. It also provides search facility on different parameters \nwithin the data of saved cases. for age-wise segregation different background colours \nare used. Cases can be sorted on the strength of location or date of listing. Calendar \ntool gives ready assistance showing number of saved cases listed on selected date. \nMobile\t app\t has\t feature\t of\t scan\t QR\t code\t which\t can\t be\t used\t for\t searching\t case\t\nhistory and current update. The number of downloads of the eCourts services mobile \napp have crossed 14.9 lakhs.\nsecond Mobile application called JusTIs has been created for use of Judicial \nOfficers.\t The\t Judicial\t Officer\t can\t see\t the\t cases\t of\t his\t court\t only.\t It\t can\t be\t used\t for\t\ncase\tmanagement\tby\t the\tJudicial\tOfficers.\tThis\t app\thas\tbeen\tcreated\twith\tcompatibility\t\nfor\t Android.\t The\t Judicial\t Officer\t can\t login\t the\t application\t after\t providing\t their\t code\t\n\n614 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\nand\t Mobile\t Number.\t The\t app\t will\t display\t the\t case\t information\t after\t verification\t of\t\nthe\t OTP\t sent\t to\t their\t respective\tmobile\t number.\tIf\t a\t Judicial\t Officer\tis\t transferred\tthe\t\napplication automatically will show the cases of the court, where he has been transferred.\nStatement\nHigh Court-wise details of computerised districts and subordinate courts\nsl. \nNo.\nName of the High Court Number of computerised district and \nsubordinate courts\n1. Allahabad 2,072\n2. Andhra Pradesh and Telangana 1,078\n3. Bombay 2,079\n4. Calcutta 811\n5. Chhattisgarh 357\n6. delhi 427\n7. gauhati 496\n8. gujarat 1,108\n9. Himachal Pradesh 119\n10. Jammu and Kashmir 218\n11. Jharkhand 351\n12. Karnataka 897\n13. Kerala 486\n14. Madras 1,032\n15. Madhya Pradesh 1,293\n16. Manipur 37\n17. Meghalaya 39\n18. odisha 534\n19. Patna 1,025\n20. Punjab and Haryana 1,018\n21. Rajasthan 1,094\n 22. sikkim 19\n23. uttarakhand 186\n24. Tripura 69\n ToT al 16,845\n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 615\nPendency in various High Courts\n710. sHRI vIJA y  PAl  sINgH T oMAR: w ill the Minister of lA w  ANd  \nJusTICe be pleased to state:\n(a) whether more than ten lakh cases have been lying pending in various High \nCourts for more than ten years, as on date;\n(b) if so, the details thereof, state-wise;\n(c) the reasons for the said cases lying pending for more than ten years;\n(d) the reaction of government thereto; and\n(e) whether there is any proposal of government to establish Benches of High \nCourts, particularly, Allahabad High Court, in w estern uP so that large number of \ncases pending in that Court may be solved in a time-bound manner, if so, the details \nthereof?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of lA w  ANd JusTICe \n(sHRI P. P. CHAudHAR y): (a) and (b) data on pendency of cases in the High \nCourts is maintained by the respective High Courts. As per information available on \nthe web-portal of National Judicial data grid (NJdg) as on date, 8.48 lakh cases \nare pending in various High Courts for more than ten years. High Court-wise details \nof cases pending for more than ten years are given in statement (See below). \n(c) and (d) disposal of cases in courts is within the domain of the judiciary. \nThe time taken for disposal of a case depends on several factors such as category \nof the case (civil or criminal), complexity of the facts involved, nature of evidence, \nco-operation of stake-holders  viz. bar, investigation agencies, witnesses and litigants \nbesides the availability of physical infrastructure, supporting court staff and applicable \nrules of procedure. No time-frame has been prescribed for disposal of various kinds \nof cases by the respective courts. government has no role in disposal of cases in \ncourts. The Central government is, however, fully committed to speedy disposal of \ncases in accordance with Article 21 of the Constitution.\n(e) High Court Benches are established in accordance with the recommendations \nmade by the Jaswant singh Commission and judgment pronounced by the Apex Court \nin w .P.(C) No. 379 of 2000 and after due consideration of a complete proposal from \nthe state government and the Chief Justice of the concerned High Court alongwith \nthe consent of the governor of the concerned state. The state government has to \nprovide necessary infrastructural facilities to establish a Bench of the High Court \naway from its principal seat and to bear the entire expenditure of the High Court \n\n616 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\nand its Bench. The Chief Justice of the High Court is required to look after the \nday-to-day administration of the High Court and its Bench and depute Judges from \nthe Principal seat to Bench from time-to-time. It is, therefore, necessary that both \nthe state government and the High Court consider the matter from all angles and \narrive at a consensus. At present, no proposal complete in all aspects, is pending for \nconsideration by the Central government including that from the Allahabad High Court.\nStatement\n Details of cases pending in High Courts for more than 10 years\nsl. \nNo.\nName of the High Court Cases pending for more \nthan ten years\n1. Allahabad High Court 273035\n2. Calcutta High Court 8569\n3. gauhati High Court 346\n4. High Court of Bombay 71346\n5. High Court of Chhattisgarh 6273\n6. High Court of delhi 7909\n7. High Court of gujarat 13539\n8. High Court of Himachal Pradesh 1355\n9. High Court of Jammu and Kashmir 7479\n10. High Court of Jharkhand 15207\n11. High Court of Judicature at Hyderabad 46821\n12. High Court of Karnataka 5850\n13. High Court of Kerala 17259\n14. High Court of Madhya Pradesh 63760\n15. High Court of Manipur 554\n16. High Court of Punjab and Haryana 58841\n17. High Court of Rajasthan 76409\n18. High Court of uttarakhand 22381\n19. Madras High Court 99867\n20. orissa High Court 30430\n21. Patna High Court 21525\nToT al  cases pending for more than 10 years 848755\nSource: NJdg data.\n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 617\nBench of the Orissa High Court in western part of Odisha\n711. sHRI PRAsANNA ACHAR yA: w ill the Minister of lA w  ANd JusTICe \nbe pleased to state:\n(a) whether the state government of odisha has written to the Central government \nseveral times proposing for establishment of a Bench of the orissa High Court in \nwestern part of odisha, pledging to provide all infrastructural requirements as would \nbe necessary for a High Court Bench; and\n(b) whether concurrence of the orissa High Court is mandatory for establishment \nof High Court Bench, if so, whether the Central government has sought approval \nof the orissa High Court and the response thereof?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of lA w  ANd JusTICe \n(sHRI P. P. CHAudHAR y): (a) and (b): High Court Benches are established in \naccordance with the recommendations made by the Jaswant singh Commission and \njudgment pronounced by the Apex Court in w .P.(C) No.379 of 2000 and after due \nconsideration of a complete proposal from the s tate g overnment and the Chief Justice \nof the concerned High Court along with the consent of the governor of the concerned \nstate. The state government has to provide necessary infrastructural facilities to \nestablish a Bench of the High Court away from its principal seat and to bear the \nentire expenditure of the High Court and its Bench. The Chief Justice of the High \nCourt is required to look after the day-to-day administration of the High Court and \nits Bench and depute Judges from the Principal seat to Bench from time-to-time. It \nis, therefore, necessary that both the state government and the High Court consider \nthe matter from all angles and arrive at a consensus. \nThe state government of odisha has requested for setting up of orissa High \nCourt Bench in w estern and southern region of odisha. In this regard, the Central \ngovernment has requested the High Court of orissa to provide its views which \nhave not been received.\nThe Central government has also apprised the state government of odisha in \nthe matter on various occasions, the last being vide letter dated 12.01.2019. \nProvision of court proceedings and judgements in \nregional languages\n712. sHRI RAKesH sINHA: w ill the Minister of lA w  ANd JusTICe be \npleased to state:\n(a) whether proceedings and judgments of High Courts and lower courts are \nprovided in regional languages (Indian languages) to the concerned parties;\n\n618 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\n(b) if so, the details of courts providing this facility, state-wise, if not, the steps \nbeing taken in this regard by government in the public interest; and \n(c) whether any fee is charged for providing judgments and other proceedings \nin regional Indian languages like Malayalam, Kannada, Telugu, odiya, etc.?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of lA w  ANd JusTICe \n(sHRI P.P. CHAudHAR y): (a) use of Hindi has been authorized in the proceedings \nas well as in the decree, Judgments in the High Courts of the states of Rajasthan, \nMadhya Pradesh, uttar Pradesh and Bihar. \n(b) and (c) Proposals were received for use of Bengali, Tamil, Hindi, gujarati and \nKannada in the High Courts of Calcutta, Madras, Chhattisgarh, gujarat and Karnataka \nrespectively. The Cabinet Committee in its decision dated 21.05.1965 required that \nconsent of the Hon’ble Chief Justice of India be obtained on any proposal relating \nto use of a language other than english in the High Court. Therefore the aforesaid \nrequests were forwarded to the Hon’ble Chief Justice of India. The Hon’ble Chief \nJustice of India on 18.01.2016 has conveyed that the full Court, after extensive \ndeliberation, disapproved the proposals, reiterating the previous Resolutions which \nhad unanimously resolved that the proposals could not be accepted.\nunder Article 235 of the Constitution of India, the administrative control over \nthe district and subordinate judiciary in the states vest with the concerned High \nCourt. use of regional language in lower courts is a subject matter of concerned \nstate/High Court. \nIT-enabled plan for expeditious disposal of Pending Cases\n713. sHRI RAM NATH THAKuR: w ill the Minister of lA w  ANd JusTICe \nbe pleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that government has contemplated to implement any \nIT-enabled plan for expeditious disposal of pending cases in lower and higher courts \nof the country;\n(b) whether any study has been conducted on the merit of this plan after \nimplementing it on pilot basis in any part of country; and\n(c) the number of pending cases disposed of during last three years along \nwith the number of cases pending at present and by when it is planned to dispose  \nthem of?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of lA w  ANd JusTICe \n(sHRI P. P. CHAudHAR y): (a) The government is implementing the e-Courts \n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 619\nMission Mode Project for Information and Communication Technology enablement \nof district and subordinate courts, across the country in association with the Hon'ble \nsupreme Court of India. The steps being taken by the government to promote the \nuse of technology in the working of the courts are as under:–\n(i) The e-Courts Mission Mode Project Phase-I was implemented during 2011- \n2015. At the end of Phase-I, out of the total target of computerisation \nof 14,249 district and subordinate courts, sites for all 14,249 courts \n(100%) were made ready for computerisation, out of which lAN was \ninstalled at 13,643 courts, hardware was provided in 13,436 courts and \nsoftware was installed in 13,672 courts. laptops were provided to 14,309 \njudicial\t officers\t and\t change\t management\t exercise\t was\t completed\t in\t all\t\nthe\t High\t Courts.\t Over\t 14,000\t Judicial\t Officers\t were\t trained\t in\t the\t use\t\nof uBuNTu-linux operating system and more than 4,000 court staff \nhave been trained as system Administrators in Case Information system \n(CIs). v ideo Conferencing facility was operationalised between 488 court \ncomplexes and 342 corresponding jails.\n(ii) The e-Courts Mission Mode Project Phase-II is being implemented for \na period of four years (2015-19) or until the project is completed, \nwhichever\t is\t later.\tAgainst\t the\t financial\t outlay\t of\t ` 1,670 crores in the \nsecond phase, ` 931.31 crores have been released to High Courts and \n` 632.30 crore have been utilised by the High Courts.\n(iii) Computerisation of 16,845 district and subordinate courts has been \ncompleted, through provisioning of computer hardware, local Area \nNetwork (lAN), and installation of standard application software in \ndistrict and subordinate courts. The details of computerised district and \nsubordinate courts under various High Courts are given in statement \n[Refer\t to\t the\t Statement\t appended\t to\t answer\t to\t USQ\t No.\t 709\t Part\t (a)].\n(iv) during the Phase II, till date, 172 crore electronic transactions have been \nrecorded for eCourts through eTaal portal. The eCourts transactions have \nthus emerged as one of the topmost accessed services of the government \nof India.\n(b) The evaluation of e-Courts Project Phase I (2010-15) to assess ICT deployment \nwas completed.\n(c) As per the web-portal of National Judicial data grid (NJdg), state/uT-wise \ndetails of cases disposed of in district and subordinate Courts during three years \n\n620 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\n(2014, 2015 and 2016) are given in statement-I (See below). data for the years \n2017 and 2018 is not available on the web-portal of National Judicial data grid.\nAs per the web-portal of National Judicial data grid (NJdg), state/uT-wise \ndetails of cases pending in district and subordinate Courts as on 05.02.2019 are \ngiven statement-II (See below).\nTimely disposal of cases in courts depends on several factors which, inter alia, \ninclude availability of adequate number of judges, supporting court staff and physical \ninfrastructure, complexity of facts involved, nature of evidence, co-operation of stake \nholders viz. bar, investigation agencies, witnesses and litigants and proper application \nof rules and procedures. No time frame has been prescribed for disposal of various \nkinds of cases by the respective courts.\nThe government is, however, fully committed to speedy disposal of cases. \nThe government has taken several initiatives to provide an ecosystem for faster \ndisposal of cases by the judiciary. The National Mission for Justice delivery and \nlegal Reforms established by the government has adopted a coordinated approach \nfor phased liquidation of arrears and pendency in judicial administration through \nvarious strategic initiatives, including improving infrastructure for courts, leveraging \nInformation and Communication Technology (ICT) for better justice delivery, and \nfilling\t up\t of\t vacant\t positions\t of\t Judges\t in\t High\t Courts\t and\t Supreme\t Court.\t The\t\nmajor achievements during the last four years under various initiatives to make the \nfunctioning\t of\t judiciary\t more\t efficient\t are\t as\t follows:–\n(i) Improving\tinfrastructure\tfor\t Judicial\t Officers\tof\t District\t and\t Subordinate\t\nCourts: As on date, ` 6,670.12 crores have been released since the inception \nof the Centrally sponsored scheme (Css) for development of Infrastructure \nfacilities for Judiciary in 1993-94. out of this, ` 3,225.82 crores (which \nare 48.36% of the total amount released till date) have been released to the \nstates and uTs since April, 2014. The number of court halls has increased \nfrom 15,818 as on 30.06.2014 to 18,796 as on date and number of residential \nunits has increased from 10,211 as on 30.06.2014 to 16,652 as on date under \nthis scheme. In addition, 2,925 court halls and 1,756 residential units are \nunder construction. The Central government has approved continuation of \nthe scheme beyond the 12th five year Plan period i.e. from 01.04.2017 to \n31.03.2020 with an estimated additional outlay of ` 3,320 crore.\n(ii) Leveraging Information and Communication Technology (ICT) for \nimproved justice delivery: government has been implementing the \n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 621\ne-Courts Mission Mode Project throughout the country for Information and \nCommunication Technology enablement of district and subordinate courts. \nNumber of computerised district and subordinate courts has increased from \n13,672 to 16,845 registering an increase of 3,173 during 2014 to 2018. New \nand user-friendly version of Case Information software has been developed \nand\t deployed\t at\t all\t the\t computerized\t District\t and\t Subordinate\t Courts.\t QR\t\nCode facility has been made operational in the software, which enables \nchecking of current status of the case. National Judicial data grid (NJdg) \nprovides\t citizens\t with\t online\t information\t about\t case\t filings,\t case\t status\t and\t\nelectronic copies of orders and judgments from district and subordinate courts \nthat have already been computerized. Information regarding 10.97 crore cases \nincluding more than three crore pending cases and more than 8.24 crore \norders/judgments are available on this portal. e-Courts services such as details \nof\t case\t registration,\t cause\t list,\t case\t status,\t daily\t orders\t and\t final\t judgments\t\nare available to litigants and advocates through e-Courts web portal, Judicial \nservice Centres (JsC) in all computerised courts, e-Courts Mobile App, email \nservice, sMs push and pull services. Information Kiosks have been set up \nat all computerized court complexes for disseminating judicial information \nrelated to cause lists and other case related information to the lawyers and \nlitigants. eCourts Project has been consistently amongst the top 5 Mission \nMode Projects of the country.\n(iii) filling up of vacant positions in supreme Court, High Courts and district \nand subordinate Courts:\nfrom 01.01.2014 to 31.12.2018, 27 Judges were appointed in supreme Court. \n446 new Judges were appointed and 379 Additional Judges were made permanent \nin the High Courts. sanctioned strength of Judges of High Courts has also been \nincreased from 906 in May, 2014 to 1079 currently. sanctioned and working strength \nof\t Judicial\t Officers\tin\t District\t and\t Subordinate\t Courts\t has\t been\t increased\t as\t follows:–\nAs on sanctioned strength w orking strength\n31.12.2013 19,518 15,115\n31.12.2018 22,833 17,701\n(iv) Reduction in Pendency through/follow up by Arrears Committees:  \nfurther, in pursuance of resolution passed in Chief Justices’ Conference held \nin April, 2015, Arrears Committees have been set up in 24 High Courts to \nclear\t cases\t pending\t for\t more\t than\t five\t years.\t Arrears\t Committees\t have\t been\t\n\n622 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\nset up under district Judges too. Arrears Committee has been constituted in \nthe supreme Court to formulate steps to reduce pendency of cases in High \nCourts and district Courts.\n(v) Nyaya Mitra Scheme: In order to reduce cases pending in courts for over \n10 years, the government launched the Nyaya Mitra scheme in April, 2017. \nUnder\t the\t Scheme,\t retired\t judicial\t officers\t are\t engaged\t and\t designated\t as\t\n‘Nyaya Mitra’ to facilitate expeditious disposal of the cases pending over 10 \nyears.\t In\t the\t first\t Phase,\t 15\t Nyaya\t Mitra\t have\t been\t engaged\t in\t 15\t Districts\t\nof Rajasthan, w est Bengal, Bihar, uttar Pradesh and Tripura.\n(vi) Emphasis on Alternate Dispute Resolution (ADR): Commercial Courts, \nCommercial division and Commercial Appellate division of High Courts \n(Amendment) Act, 2018 has been enacted on 20th August, 2018 whereby \nmandatory pre-Institution mediation mechanism has been introduced for \nsettlement of commercial disputes. Amendment to the Arbitration and \nConciliation Act, 1996 by the Arbitration and Conciliation (Amendment) \nAct 2015 has been made to expedite the speedy resolution of disputes by \nprescribing timelines. The Arbitration and Conciliation (Amendment) Bill, \n2018 passed by the lok sabha on 10.08.2018 seeks to set up Arbitration \nCouncil of India (ACI) to inter alia grade arbitral institutions, accredit \narbitrators\t and\t impart\t training\t and\t award\t certificate\t in\t the\t ADR\t field.\n(vii) Initiatives to Fast Track Special Type of Cases: The fourteenth finance \nCommission endorsed the proposal of the government to strengthen the \njudicial system in states which included, inter alia, establishing fast Track \nCourts for cases of heinous crimes; cases involving senior citizens, women, \nchildren\t etc.,\t and\t urged\t the\t State\t Governments\t to\t use\t the\t additional\t fiscal\t\nspace provided in the form of enhanced tax devolution form 32% to 42% \nto meet such requirements. At present, 731 such fast Track Courts are \nfunctioning across the country. To fast track criminal cases involving elected \nMPs/MlAs, twelve (12) special Courts have been set up in eleven (11) states \n(Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, Kerala, Karnataka, Tamil Nadu, Maharashtra, \nMadhya Pradesh, uttar Pradesh, Bihar, w est Bengal and NCT of delhi) and \nproportionate funds have been released to these states by the government. \nThe Criminal law (Amendment) Act, 2018 has been enacted on 11.08.2018 \nto amend the Indian Penal Code, Indian evidence Act, 1872, the Code of \nCriminal\t Procedure,\t 1973\t and\t the\t Protection\t of\t Children\t from\t Sexual\t Offices\t\nAct, 2012.\n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 623\nStatement-I\nDetails of number of cases disposed in District and Subordinate Courts \nduring 3 years (2014, 2015 and 2016)\nsl. \nNo\nName of the state/uT disposal of \ncases\ndisposal of \ncases\ndisposal of \ncases\n2014 2015 2016\n1 2 3 4 5\n1. Andhra Pradesh and Telangana 6,47,130 6,58,713 6,03,017\n2. Arunachal Pradesh 7,615 3,588 4,384\n3. Assam 2,76,138 2,72,538 2,51,119\n4. Bihar 3,05,583 2,92,678 3,44,683\n5. Chhattisgarh 1,76,144 1,95,174 1,95,495\n6. goa 30,625 34,765 34,130\n7. gujarat 11,32,433 10,93,664 15,86,926\n8. Haryana 5,87,385 5,42,440 5,93,132\n9. Himachal Pradesh 4,09,732 1,94,803 2,17,568\n10. Jammu and Kashmir 2,97,507 87,687 98,638\n11. Jharkhand 1,10,068 1,18,845 1,04,284\n12. Karnataka 13,67,041 12,09,127 10,79,586\n13. Kerala 13,55,926 13,38,443 11,93,996\n14. Madhya Pradesh 11,13,382 10,73,584 7,84,077\n15. Maharashtra 15,36,322 16,49,187 22,81,027\n16. Manipur 14,257 7,395 6,588\n17. Meghalaya 11,691 9,215 11,396\n18. Mizoram 10,747 10,355 10,906\n19. Nagaland 3,047 4,826 4,215\n20. odisha 4,70,085 4,08,261 4,69,571\n21. Punjab 5,49,300 5,78,681 6,05,324\n22. Rajasthan 11,32,028 13,71,762 8,16,129\n23. sikkim 2,008 1,725 2,142\n24. Tamil Nadu 16,45,329 11,51,349 @10,43,172\n25. Tripura 1,93,003 1,92,081 67,385\n\n624 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\n1 2 3 4 5\n26. uttar Pradesh 31,82,318 33,13,424 36,18,460\n27. uttarakhand 2,20,660 2,00,947 1,75,464\n28. w est Bengal and Andaman \nand Nicobar Island\n10,89,309 10,99,743 10,59,641\n29. Chandigarh 1,80,616 1,45,990 1,43,520\n30. dadra and Nagar Haveli and \ndaman and diu\n2,771 3,323 3,810\n31. delhi 9,30,732 6,12,553 6,18,618\n32. lakshadweep 95 280 269\n33. Puducherry 28,631 20,409 @\nToT al 1,90,19,658 1,78,97,555 1,80,28,672\n@figures of Tamil Nadu and Puducherry are combined in respect of the year 2016.\nStatement-II\nCases pending in District and Subordinate Courts as on 05.02.2019\nsl.No. state Total\n1. Andhra Pradesh 537926\n2. Assam 293822\n3. Bihar 2599079\n4. Chandigarh 44574\n5. Chhattisgarh 266468\n6. delhi 735176\n7. diu and daman 2145\n8. dadra and Nagar Haveli 3417\n9. goa 50564\n10. gujarat 1668347\n11. Haryana 748455\n12. Himachal Pradesh 263166\n13. Jammu and Kashmir 166268\n14. Jharkhand 363556\n15. Karnataka 1293002\n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 625\nsl.No. state Total\n16. Kerala 1172371\n17. Madhya Pradesh 1407753\n18. Maharashtra 3601493\n19. Manipur 9966\n20. Meghalaya 7102\n21. Mizoram 3774\n22. odisha 1156687\n23. Punjab 607063\n24. Rajasthan 1523301\n25. sikkim 1302\n26. Tamil Nadu 1119914\n27. Telangana 523202\n28. Tripura 22973\n29. uttar Pradesh 7151897\n30. uttarakhand 237666\n31. w est Bengal 2224014\nToT al  P endIng  c aSeS 29806443\nSource: National Judicial data grid.\nCollegium for the appointment of Judges\n714. dR. BANdA PRAKAsH: w ill the Minister of lA w  ANd JusTICe be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether the Bar Council of delhi underlined the need for a transparent, \naccountable and objective collegiums system for the appointment of Judges to maintain \ncredibility;\n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) the steps the Ministry is taking in this direction?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of lA w  ANd JusTICe \n(sHRI P. P. CHAudHAR y): (a) to (c) The information is being collected and will \nbe laid on the Table of the House. \n\n626 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\nReinforcement of credibility of EVMs\n715. sHRIMATI CHHA yA veRMA: \n  sHRI vIsHAMBHAR PRAsAd NIsHAd:\nw ill the Minister of lA w  ANd JusTICe be pleased to state:\n(a) the steps taken by the Ministry to examine the new facts that have come \nup regarding the tampering of the chip manufactured by electronics Corporation of \nIndia limited (eCIl), which is used in electronic voting machines (evMs) in Indian \nelections, so that evM's credibility could be retained; and\n(b) the details of number of technological aspects that have been examined \nin\t the\t last\t five\t years\t to\t clear\t the\t constant\t doubts\t raised\t over\t EVMs,\t which\t have\t\nreinforced the credibility of the evMs?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of lA w  ANd JusTICe \n(sHRI P.P. CHAudHAR y): (a) and (b) The election Commission of India (eCI) has \ninformed that no facts or evidence of any kind has come to the notice of Commission \nregarding any tampering of electronic voting Machine (evM) chip. The credibility \nof eCI-evMs has remained absolutely intact and robust since its introduction. The \nissue of possible tampering of evM has been examined by the various courts in the \ncountry including the supreme Court and after going through different aspects of the \ntechnological soundness and the administrative measures involved in the use of evMs, \nthe Courts have held that the evMs are credible, reliable and totally tamperproof. \nIn\t view\t of\t emerging\t scientific\t and\t technological\t advancements,\t the\t Technical\t\nexpert Committee of the eCI periodically undertakes improvement in the design \nof evMs to incorporate the latest technological features in addition to the existing \nsecurity\t features\t of\t the\t older\t EVMs.\t Further,\t V oter\tV erifiable\t Paper\t Audit\t Trail\t\n(vvPAT) unit has also been introduced as a further transparency measure. The \nCommission has stated that it is committed to 100% vvPAT unit coverage in all \nfuture elections to lok sabha and state legislative Assemblies.\nRequirement of voting machines for Lok Sabha elections of 2019\n716. dR. T. suBBARAMI Reddy : \n  sHRIMATI AMBIKA soNI:\nw ill the Minister of lA w  ANd JusTICe be pleased to state: \n(a)\t the\t number\t of\t EVMs\t and\t V oter\t V erifiable\t Paper\t Audit\t Trail\t (VVPA T)\t\nmachines estimated to be required for the 2019 lok sabha elections;\n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 627\n(b) whether essential design improvements were incorporated in evMs to make \nthem foolproof and tamper-proof;\n(c) if so, the details thereof, and\n(d) by when all the machines would be in place for the forthcoming lok sabha \ngeneral elections along with the details thereof ?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of lA w  ANd JusTICe  \n(sHRI P. P. CHAudHAR y): (a) The election Commission of India (e CI) has informed \nthat a total of 13,25,200 Control units; 18,12,600 Ballot units and 14,31,000 voter \nV erifiable\tPaper\t Audit\t Trail\t (VVPA T)\tUnits\t will\t be\t required\t for\t conducting\t the\t Lok\t\nsabha elections-2019.\n(b) and (c) The eCI has informed that all electronic voting Machines (evMs) \nthat the Commission uses are absolutely tamper proof as per the administrative \nstandard operating Procedures under which these machines are stored and used. A \nnumber of technological changes have been carried out in the evMs periodically \nfrom 2001, with the next generation ‘M3’ machines being produced since 2013. The \nlatest addition to the evMs is the vvPAT unit which was introduced in the year \n2013 as an additional measure of transparency in the evM-based voting system.\n(d) The Commission has stated that all the machines will be in place for the \nforthcoming lok sabha general elections.\nCases pending before Tribunals\n717. sHRI K.C. RAMAMuRTHy : w ill the Minister of lA w  ANd JusTICe \nbe pleased to state:\n(a) the details of each of the different kinds of Tribunals functioning in the \ncountry, state-wise; \n(b) the details of cases pending before each of the above-mentioned Tribunal \nand details of cases that are pending for more than one year, two years, three years \nand\t five\t years\t and\t reasons\t for\t such\t pendency;\t\n(c) whether it is a fact that delay is due to huge vacancies in Tribunals; \n(d) whether inordinate delay has defeated the very purpose of establishing \nTribunals; and\n(e) if so, the steps taken/proposed to be taken to dispose the pendency in a \ntimebound manner?\n\n628 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of lA w  ANd JusTICe \n(sHRI P. P. CHAudHAR y): (a) to (e) Information is being collected and will be \nlaid on the Table of the House.\nVacancies in Courts\n718. sHRI RIPuN BoRA: w ill the Minister of lA w  ANd JusTICe be pleased \nto state:\n(a) w hether over 10,000 posts ldA, Copyist, Typist, l aw Clerks, Private \nsecretary, stenographer, Judicial Assistant, Research Assistant are vacant in different \nHigh Courts of the country; \n(b) whether nearly 450 posts of Judges are still vacated cumulatively in the \nsupreme Court and all 24 High Courts of the country thereto; \n(c) whether in four High Courts, there are acting Chief Justice; \n(d)\t if\t so,\t the\t details\t of\t category-wise\t vacancies\t and\t proposal\t to\t fill\t up\t all\t\nvacancies in time-bound mechanism; and \n(e) the details of referral of supreme Court Collegium and state's recommendations \ntherein and action taken thereon?\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTATe  IN THe  MINIsTRy  of l Aw  ANd JusTIC e  (sHRI \nP. P. CHAudHAR y): (a) Information relating to vacancies in non-judicial staff in \nHigh Courts and subordinate judiciary is not maintained in the union government \nas the matter comes under the purview of the High Courts and respective state \ngovernments.\n(b) to (e) A statement showing Approved strength, w orking strength and vacancies \nof Judges in the supreme Court of India as on 01.02.2019 is attached. The High \nCourts of Andhra Pradesh, Calcutta, gujarat and Karnataka are presently having \nActing Chief Justices.\nThe proposals received from the various High Courts for appointment as Judges \nof the High Court are examined and acted upon in accordance with the Memorandum \nof Procedure. filling up of vacancies in the supreme Court and High Courts is a \ncontinuous and collaborative process, between the executive and the Judiciary. It \nrequires consultation and approval from various Constitutional Authorities. Hence, the \ntime\t frame\t for\t filling\t up\t of\t the\t vacancies\t cannot\t be\t indicated\t precisely.\t While\t every\t\neffort\tis\t made\t to\t fill\tup\t the\t existing\t vacancies\t expeditiously,\tvacancies\t keep\t on\t arising\t\non account of retirement, resignation or elevation of Judges (to the supreme Court).\n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 629\nApplications for Public Notary Licenses\n719. sHRI ANIl  desAI: w ill the Minister of lA w  ANd JusTICe be pleased \nto state:\n(a) whether a number of legal aspirants have applied for Public Notary licenses; \nand\n(b) if so, the number of applications received and by when these applications \nwould be cleared?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of lA w  ANd JusTICe \n(sHRI P. P. CHAudHAR y): (a) and (b) yes, sir. The receiving of applications for \nappointment as Public Notary is an ongoing process and the interviews are conducted \nfor appointment of Notaries as per the provisions of the Notaries Act, 1952 and \nNotaries Rules, 1956 framed thereunder from time to time for different states/uTs. \nTherefore no precise time frame can be indicated.\nElectrification\t of\t railway\t lines\t in\t the\t State\t of\t Andhra\t Pradesh\n720. sHRI T. g. veNKATesH: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be pleased \nto state:\n(a)\t whether\t it\t is\t a\t fact\t that\t Government\t is\t planning\t to\t take\t up\t electrification\t\nof railway lines in the country with funds worth ` 35,000 crore;\n(b) if so, the details thereof;\n(c)\t whether\t any\t railway\t lines\t have\t been\t identified\tin\t the\t State\t of\tAndhra\t Pradesh\t\nto be taken up under this programme, if so, the details thereof;\n(d)\t the\t details\t of\t electrification\t of\t railway\t lines\t particularly\t passing\t through\t\nKurnool, Andhra Pradesh; and\n(e)\t the\t quantum\t of\t funds\t estimated\t to\t be\t spent\t on\t this\t electrification\t of\t railway\t\nlines and the details thereof?\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) to (c) yes, sir. Indian Railways have prepared an Action Plan to \nelectrify balance Broad gauge (Bg) routes of Indian Railways including state of \nAndhra Pradesh.\n\n630 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\nAction Plan to electrify balance Broad gauge routes is as under:–\nyear Route\t Kilometer\t planned\t for\t electrification\n2018-19 6000\n2019-20 7000\n2020-21 10500\n2021-22 10500\nToT al 34000\nThe expenditure of `\t 29486\t crore\t is\t expected\t for\t electrification\t of\t 34000\t Route\t\nKilometers of sections. \nfollowing rail routes falling fully/partially in the state of Andhra Pradesh have \nbeen\t sanctioned\t for\t electrification:–\nName of The Project Total Route\nKilometers\nPagidipalli-Nallapadu 285\nguntakal-Bellary-Hospet incl. Tornagallu-Ranjitpura Branch line 138\nManmad-Mudkhed-dhone (excl. umdanagar-Mehboobnagar) 783\nguntakal-Kalluru 40\nChikjajur-Bellary 184\ndharmavaram-Pakala 228\nNandyal-yerraguntla 123\nNaupada-gunupur 90\nTenali-Repale 34\n(d) and (e) gadwal-dhone (1000 Route Kilometers) railway line passing through \nKurnool is part of Manmad-Mudkhed-dhone (excluding umdanagar-Mehboobnagar) 783 \nRoute\t kilometers\t electrification\t project\t sanctioned\t at\t ` 664.50 crore. The tender for \nthe\t work\t has\t been\t opened.\t Execution\t of\t the\t work\t will\t be\t taken\t up\t after\t finalization\t\nof tender.\nProposal for a special train from Gwalior to Bhopal\n721. sHRI PRABHAT JHA: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Ministry has received any proposal to run a daily special train \nfrom gwalior to Bhopal; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 631\n(c) whether any decision has been taken/ would be taken soon on the above \nmentioned proposal; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJeN \ngoHAIN): (a) to (d) Request from the Hon’ble Member of Parliament has been \nreceived for introduction of a train leaving gwalior at 05.00AM and arriving Bhopal at \n10.00 AM and in return direction leaving Bhopal at 05.00 PM and arriving gwalior at \n10.00 PM. The same has been examined but not found feasible, at present. However, \nat present, 12486 shri ganganagar-Hazur sahib Nanded express (Tri-weekly) leaves \ngwalior at 04.20 AM arrives Bhopal at 10.55 AM and in the return direction 12721 \nHyderabad-Nizamuddin dakshin express (daily) (leaves Bhopal at 04.50 PM and \narrives gwalior at around 10.40 PM are already available.\nAnnual Capital Expenditure by Railways\n722. dR. vINA y  P. sAHAsRABuddHe: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be \npleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that the Average Annual Capital expenditure incurred \nby Railways for the period 2014-19 is more than twice the average for the period \n2009-2014; and\n(b) if so, the impact of such drastic increase in the spending?\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) yes, sir. The Average Annual Capital expenditure incurred by Railways \nduring the period 2014-19 is more than twice the average for the period 2009-2014. \nThe Average Annual Capital expenditure incurred by Railways during the period \n2014-19 is ` 100274.37 crore as compared to ` 45979.66 crore during the period \n2009-2014 (figures for 2018-19 are as per Revised estimates).\n(b) Increase in Average Annual Capital e xpenditure has enabled speedy \nimplementation\t of\t the\t projects\t resulting\t in\t significant\t augmentation\t in\t capacity\t\nof\t the\t Railway\t system\t including\t electrification,\t manufacturing\t of\t upgraded\t rolling\t\nstock, improvement in passenger amenities and facilitated introduction of new trains \nand increased throughput. Indian Railways’ average annual freight loading increased \nfrom 967.66 Million Tonne during 2009-14 to 1135.69 Million Tonne during 2014-\n19. Average annual number of Passengers also increased from 7987.80 Million to \n8217.35 Million during the above periods.\n\n632 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\nTrain operation by private companies\n723. sHRI vIsHAMBHAR PRAsAd NIsHAd:  \n  sHRIMATI CHHA yA veRMA: \n  CH. suKHRAM sINgH yAdAv :\nw ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be pleased to state:\n(a) whether government is contemplating to hand over responsibilities of train \noperations to private companies;\n(b) if so, whether there is a proposal to hand over the operations of goods \ntrains to private companies along with passenger train; and\n(c) the details of work of Railways which have been delegated to private \ncompanies at present, the number of complaints received regarding poor quality of \nwork and the details of mechanism adopted for their redressal?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJeN \ngoHAIN): (a) to (c) There is no proposal to privatise any area of operation of \nRailways. Participation of Private sector has been allowed in construction and \nmaintenance of Railway lines under various models of Participative Policy such \nas Non government Railway, Joint venture, Build operate and Transfer, Customer \nfunding and Annuity. In all these schemes, responsibility of trains operation and \nsafety\t certification\t rests\t with\t Indian\t Railways.\t However,\t outsourcing\t of\t certain\t\nfacilities like cleaning, pay and use toilets, retiring rooms, parking etc. is done on \nneed\t based\t manner\t to\t improve\t efficiency.\t In\t case\t of\t any\t deficiency,\t the\t mechanism\t\nof redressal as per the contract is invoked.\nWater Vending Machines at Railway Stations\n724. sHRI MAHesH PoddAR: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be pleased \nto state:\n(a) the number of water vending machines installed by the Indian Railway \nCatering and Tourism Corporation (IRCTC);\n(b) whether IRCTC plans to install a water vending machine at every station; \n(c) whether it is a fact that the installation of water vending machines has \nconsiderably reduced the use of plastic bottles, if so, the details thereof; and\n(d) whether the plastic cups at the functioning vending machines have been \nfully replaced by paper cups?\n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 633\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) and (b) Indian Railway Catering and Tourism Corporation (IRCTC) \nhas installed a total number of 1995 w ater vending Machines (wvM) at around \n700 major Railway stations across the country consisting primarily of A-1, A, B and \nC category of stations. Installing w ater vending Machines (wvM) at every station \nis\t not\t financially\t viable.\n(c)\t Y es,\t Sir.\t Most\t of\t the\t passengers\t are\t bringing\t their\t own\t containers\t to\t refill\t\nwater dispensed from w ater vending Machines. The use of disposable cups for \ndispensing 300ml water from w ater vending Machines has further reduced the use \nof plastic bottles, resulting in saving of an estimated 2150 MT of plastic used for \nplastic bottles during the year 2018-19. \n(d) y es, sir. The use of plastic cups at the functional vending machines has \nbeen fully replaced with paper cups.\nPending railway projects in Chhattisgarh\n725. sHRI RAM vICHAR NeTAM: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be pleased \nto state:\n(a) whether due to the shortage of funds and other technical reasons Chirmiri-\nBarwadih rail line in Chhattisgarh along with other projects are pending since long \ntime;\n(b) if so, the details of the last three years, project-wise; \n(c)\t whether\t Government\t has\t not\t allocated\t sufficient\t funds\t for\t the\t pending\t rail\t\nprojects; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof and steps taken by government in this regard?\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) to (d) No sir, completion of projects depends upon several factors \nincluding, complete handing over of encumbrance free land by the state govt., \nforestry clearances, shifting of utilities etc. \nsurvey for new line between Barwadih-Chirmiri (Ambikapur) (182 Km) was \nconducted in year 2010-2011 at a cost of ` 1104.50 crore with Rate of Return \n(Ro R) of 8.61%. f urther, the then Planning Commission was requested (March \n2012) to accord ‘In Principle’ approval to the work of new line between Barwadih \nand Ambikapur (182 Km) at a cost of 1104.50 crore. out of total project length of \n182 Km, 77 Km falls in the state of Jharkhand and remaining 105 Km falls in the \n\n634 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\nstate of Chhattisgarh. The then Planning Commission has accorded (June, 2012) ‘In \nPrinciple’ approval to the aforesaid project with the condition that the Railway gets \nland free of cost from concerned state govt. and approach Coal India, which has \nhuge investible surplus, with the request to jointly develop this project as a joint \nventure. Accordingly, state government of Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand and Coal India l td. \nwere requested in May, 2013 to provide land free of cost and develop this project \njointly. At that time, neither state government of Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand nor Coal \nIndia ltd. responded. As such project could not be taken forward. \nNow Chattisgarh Railway Corporation l td. a Joint v enture Company of \nGovernment\t of\t Chattisgarh\t and\t Ministry\t of\t Railways\t has\t identified\t this\t project\t as\t a\t\npotential project for further development and has awarded a consultancy contract in \nNovember, 2017 for assessment of economic viability and feasibility of Barwadih-\nAmbikapur (182 Km) new line. Report once completed will be considered by the \nBoard of CRCl  for taking the project forward or not. \nProject-wise details including expenditure incurred thereon and budget allocations \nmade for the projects over the years are available in budget documents presented to \nthe Parliament and also available on Indian Railways website www.indianrailways.gov.in.\nfunds for projects are given based on status of land acquisition, progress of \nproject, relative priority and last mile connectivity projects. \nExpansion of passenger rail services to \nMadhya Pradesh\n726. dR. sAT yANARAyAN JATIyA: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be pleased \nto state the details of the updated status regarding announcement and implementation \nof passenger service expansion during the last four years for every train starting from \nand reaching Madhya Pradesh and information regarding proposals of forthcoming \nnew rail passenger services?\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): Indian Railways do not run train services on state-wise basis as railway \nnetwork straddles across state boundaries. However, during the last 4 years, 35 train \nservices (in single) originating and terminating in the state of Madhya Pradesh have \nbeen introduced. \nfurther, introduction of train services is an ongoing process on Indian  \nRailways\t keeping\t in\t view\t operational\t feasibility,\t traffic\t demand\t and\t availability\t of\t\nresources.\n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 635\nLocomotive-less trains\n727. sHRI NARA yAN lAl  PANCHARIy A: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays \nbe pleased to state:\n(a) whether government has taken any decision to run locomotive-less trains;\n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;\n(c)\t whether\t Government\t has\t assessed\t the\t financial\t requirement\t of\t running\t such\t\ntrains and if so, the details thereof; and\n(d) whether government has made any assessment of security aspects in running \nsuch trains, if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \nGOHAIN):\t (a)\t to\t (c)\t Y es,\tSir.\t The\t first\t locomotive-less\t semi\t high\t speed\t train,\t code\t\nnamed Train-18, was manufactured at Integral Coach factory/Chennai. The cost of \nvarious types of coaches used in Train-18 is as follows:–\nsl.No. Coach type Transfer price (In ` 000s)\n1. Train 18 Motor Coach 7,72,89\n2. Train 18 driving Trailer Coach 5,65,90\n3. Train 18 Non-driving Trailer Coach 4,89,28\n4. Train 18 Trailer Coach 3,99,91\nIn\t addition\t to\t this,\t Indian\t Railways\t (IR)\t has\t a\t fleet\t of\t 596\t Electric\t Multiple\t\nunits, 220 Mainline electric Multiple unit rakes and 225 diesel electric Multiple \nunit rakes, which are locomotive-less trains and are already running over IR. These \ntrains are being regularly manufactured as per requirement of zonal Railways.\n(d) No, sir. Policing on Railways being a state subject, prevention of crime, \nregistration of cases, their investigation and maintenance of law and order in Railways \nare the statutory responsibility of state governments, which they discharge through \ngovernment Railway Police (gRP)/district Police. Railway Protection force (RPf) \nsupplements the efforts of gRP to provide better protection and security of passenger \narea and passengers.\nwhenever a new train is introduced on any route, security is provided to the train \nand passengers by the concerned state governments and it is further supplemented \nby the RPf .\n\n636 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\nLaying of new railway lines\n728. sHRIMATI KANTA KARd AM: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be \npleased to state:\n(a) the details of total number of railway projects announced during last four \nyears for laying of new railway lines in uttar Pradesh;\n(b) whether work has been initiated on these projects;\n(c) if so, details thereof; and\n(d) if not, reason therefor?\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) to (d) Railway projects are not sanctioned state-wise. However, \nfollowing 7 new Rail line projects falling fully/partly in the state of uttar Pradesh \nannounced in the last 4 years and current year are in different stages of planning/ \nsanction/execution:–\nsl. \nNo.\nProject year of \ninclusion in \nBudget\nstatus of Project\n1 2 3 4\n1 Mau-ghazipur-Tarighat 51 km 2015-16 land acquisition taken up. \nConstruction of Rail-cum-Road \nbridge has been taken up.\n2 sahjanwa-dohrighat 81 km 2016-17 Project has been included in \nthe Budget.\n3 Bahraich and Khalilabad via \nBhinga, shravasti, Balrampur, \nutraula, domariyaganj, \nMehdawal, Bansi (240.26 km) \n2016-17 Project sanctioned in october \n2018.\n4 Meerut-Panipat 104 km 2016-17 Project has been included in \nthe Budget subject to requisite \napproval.\n5 Robertsganj-Mugalsarai via \nMadhupur, sukrit, Ahrora 70 \nkm \n2017-18 Project has been included in \nthe Budget subject to requisite \napproval.\n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 637\n1 2 3 4\n6 Padrauna-Kushinagar-gorakhpur \n(sardar Nagar) 64 km \n2017-18 Project has been included in \nthe Budget subject to requisite \napproval.\n7 etah-Kasganj 29 km 2017-18 Project has been included in \nthe Budget subject to requisite \napproval.\nCompletion of Railway project requires number of clearances from various \nMinistries and departments of state/Central governments. These, inter alia, include \nclearances inherent to land acquisition, statutory clearance and shifting of utilities etc, \nwhich are part of project execution. As many factors responsible for completion of \na\t project\t are\t beyond\t the\t control\t of\t Ministry\t of\t Railways,\t it\t is\t not\t feasible\t to\t fix\t\ntime frame for completion of all projects.\nEmpowering Railway Protection Force\n729. sHRI lAl  sINH vAdodIA: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be pleased \nto state:\n(a) whether government is seriously contemplating to further empower Railway \nProtection force;\n(b) if so, whether government has taken any steps in this direction; and\n(c) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\nTHe MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) to (c) A proposal has been initiated for amendment in the Railways \nAct, 1989 which inter alia envisages empowerment of the Railway Protection force \nto deal with cases of theft of passengers’ belongings and to initiate legal action \nagainst accused persons apprehended for outraging modesty of women passengers. \nAfter completion of inter-Ministerial consultations, proposal has been forwarded to \nthe Cabinet secretariat for approval.\nVacancies in Maintenance and Inspection Wings of Railways\n730. dR. K.v . P. RAMACHANdRA RAo: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that there are more than 60 per cent vacancies in the \nMaintenance and Inspection w ings of Railways;\n(b) if so, the details thereof;\n\n638 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\n(c) the number of sanctioned posts, zone/state-wise and the number of existing \nvacancies; and \n(d)\t whether\t Government\t has\t any\t plan\t to\t fill\t up\t these\t vacancies\t at\t the\t earliest?\t\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) No, sir.\n(b) does not arise.\n(c) The sanctioned strength, on-roll strength and vacancy in group ‘C’ and \nerstwhile group ‘d’ posts on Indian Railways as on 01.12.2018 is as under:–\nzonal Railways sanctioned strength on-roll strength vacancy\nCentral 124800 100642 24158\neast Coast 55038 46114 8924\neast Central 96678 78516 18162\neastern 129930 109057 20873\nMetro 4836 4027 809\nNorth Central 82868 64012 18856\nNorth eastern 62850 48949 13901\nNortheast frontier 73555 57831 15724\nNorthern 171667 135660 36007\nNorth w estern 61492 45523 15969\nsouth Central 96615 78700 17915\nsouth east Central 54178 45491 8687\nsouth eastern 98436 82566 15870\nsouthern 103184 84276 18908\nsouth w estern 41540 35297 6243\nw est Central 65080 53516 11564\nw estern 114348 92664 21684\nTotal (zonal Railways) 1437095 1162841 274254\nProduction units 48069 41477 6592\nothers 21274 16390 4879\nToT al 1506438 1220708 285725\n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 639\n(d)\t Y es,\t Sir.\t Three\t Centralized\t Employment\t Notifications\t (CENs)\t for\t filling\t up\t\nvacancies were issued in 2018:–\n(i) CeN No.01/2018 for Asst. loco Pilots (AlPs) and Technicians (Total \n64,371 vacancies). The 2nd stage Computer Based Test has been conducted \nfrom 21.01.2019 to 23.01.2019. \n(ii) CeN No.02/2018 for 62,907 vacancies in level-1 (erstwhile group ‘d’) \nposts. Computer Based Test has been conducted from 17.09.2018 to \n17.12.2018 in multiple shifts. \n(iii)\tCEN\t No.03/2018\t has\t been\t published\t on\t 29.12.2018\t for\t filling\t up\t of\t\n13,487 vacancies for the posts of Junior engineers and others. The last \ndate for online registration was 31.01.2019.\nRedressal\t of\t Cases\t and\t online\t filing\t of\t FIRs\n731. dR. K.v .P. RAMACHAN dRA RAo: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be \npleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that government is considering to allow railway passengers \nto\t file\t online,\t FIRs\t on\t railway\t crimes,\t if\t so,\t the\t details\t thereof;\t\n(b) whether it is also a fact that redressal rate of crimes registered with Railway \nPolice is very low, if so, the details thereof; and \n(c)\t the\t number\t of\t cases\t filed\t in\t Railway\t Police\t Stations,\t Zone/State-wise\t and\t\nnumber\t of\t cases\t resolved\t as\t well\t as\t number\t of\t cases\t pending,\t during\t last\t five\t years?\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) to (c) Policing on Railways being a state subject, prevention of crime, \nregistration of cases (fIRs), their investigation and maintenance of law and order in \nRailway premises as well as on running trains are the statutory responsibility of the \nstate governments, which they discharge through government Railway Police (gRP)/\ndistrict Police. Railway Protection force (RPf) supplements the efforts of gRP to \nprovide better protection and security of passenger area and passengers. \nThe\t issue\t of\t filing\t of\t FIRs\t online\t by\t Railway\t passengers\t was\t deliberated\t during\t\nthe recent All India Conference on Railway security held on 16.01.2019 at v igyan \nBhawan, New delhi. However, Policing on Railways being a state subject, the issue \nto\t allow\t Railway\t passengers\t to\t file\t online\t FIRs\t on\t railway\t crimes,\t comes\t under\t the\t\npurview of concerned state governments. \nfurther, cases of Indian Penal Code (IPC) crimes in Railways are registered and \ninvestigated by the concerned government Railway Police. Railways does not maintain \n\n640 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\nany data on Indian Penal Code crimes. whenever any information about status of \ncrime on Railways is sought, state gRP is requested to provide the information. \neach and every case registered by the concerned government Railway Police is \ninvestigated and redressed as per extant legal provisions. As per data provided by the \nGovernment\t Railway\t Police\t Stations,\t the\t zone-wise\t number\t of\t cases\t filed\t in\t Railway\t\nPolice stations and number of cases resolved as well as number of cases pending \nduring the last 05 years over Indian Railways are given in statement. \n Statement\nZone-wise number of cases filed in Railway Police Stations and number of cases \nresolved as well as number of cases pending during the last \n05 years over Indian Railways\nzonal Railway year No. of cases \nregistered in Railway \nPolice stations\nNo. of \ncases \nresolved\nNo. of \ncases \npending\n1 2 3 4 5\nCentral 2014 4807 3289 1011\n2015 5573 3589 1470\n2016 5614 3926 1016\n2017 21578 7071 12784\n2018 32494 6794 23740\neast Central 2014 2626 2338 288\n2015 2422 2266 156\n2016 3229 3009 220\n2017 3915 3465 450\n2018 3122 2390 732\neast Coast 2014 646 610 36\n2015 737 684 53\n2016 583 757 96\n2017 878 721 157\n2018 971 469 502\nNorthern 2014 5385 3780 538\n2015 7580 4589 869\n2016 8847 4810 1203\n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 641\n1 2 3 4 5\n2017 8834 4265 2640\n2018 7787 3943 3443\nNorth Central 2014 4054 1994 0\n2015 6186 3530 0\n2016 6923 3656 2\n2017 7801 4978 7\n2018 8108 4702 824\nNorth eastern 2014 1983 1983 0\n2015 2429 2428 0\n2016 2694 2692 2\n2017 2855 2841 14\n2018 2644 2300 344\nNortheast frontier 2014 554 126 0\n2015 636 151 3\n2016 721 199 11\n2017 640 188 13\n2018 818 216 137\nNorth w estern 2014 1281 1262 19\n2015 1334 1318 16\n2016 1463 1456 7\n2017 1519 1499 20\n2018 1486 1406 80\nsouthern 2014 4739 4738 1\n2015 6587 6581 6\n2016 8378 8344 7\n2017 4003 3888 107\n2018 3248 2551 687\nsouth Central 2014 1743 391 1352\n2015 2015 644 1371\n2016 2149 681 1468\n\n642 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\n1 2 3 4 5\n2017 2986 783 2203\n2018 3177 524 2653\nsouth eastern 2014 396 87 0\n2015 392 77 0\n2016 578 110 0\n2017 595 126 0\n2018 717 192 0\nsouth east Central 2014 437 215 222\n2015 634 163 471\n2016 759 204 555\n2017 1416 178 1238\n2018 1228 182 1046\nsouth w estern 2014 1466 1416 9\n2015 1409 1355 19\n2016 1833 1262 529\n2017 2270 1113 1104\n2018 1520 478 1036\nw estern 2014 2348 1639 312\n2015 3325 2184 502\n2016 3329 2247 482\n2017 12575 3253 8315\n2018 17353 3301 13615\nw est Central 2014 3332 2924 14\n2015 3522 3234 6\n2016 4076 3677 9\n2017 4649 4206 104\n2018 5067 4183 618\nNote:\t The\t above\t figures\texcludes\t the\t figures\tof\t Eastern\t Railway\t as\t figures\tnot\t received\t from\t State\t of\tWest\t\nBengal. The number of cases pending in south eastern Railway have also not received from concerned \ngRP authorities.\n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 643\nDeterioration of food quality and quantity in Rajdhani Express\n732. sHRIMATI sHANTA CHHe TRI: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be \npleased to state:\n(a) whether the Ministry is aware that reduction of food items served in Rajdhani \nexpress plying to eastern India and the sharp increase in railway fare since 2014 \nhave resulted in dissatisfaction amongst the passengers;\n(b) whether government plans to take pocket-friendly measures for passengers; \nand\n(c) if so, the details thereof?\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) No, sir. In terms of Catering Policy 2017, Indian Railway Catering \nand Tourism Corporation (IRCTC) has been rationalizing the menu of pre-paid trains \nincluding Rajdhani trains to eastern India where catering charges are inbuilt in the \npassenger fare. under rationalization of menu, IRCTC has changed the nature of \nmeals as per the needs of passengers. The menu is, however, commensurate with \nthe existing tariff.\n(b)\t and\t (c)\t It\t is\t the\t endeavour\t of\t Indian\t Railways\t to\t fix\t and\t notify\t tariff\t of\t\ncatering items from time to time, taking into consideration its affordability as per the \nneeds of travelling passengers. The tariff of standard items for Mail/express trains \nand static units was last revised in 2012 and for all pre-paid Rajdhani/shatabdi/\nduronto trains in 2013.\nIndian Railways have also taken the following pocket-friendly measures for \ncatering:–\n(i) Introduction of Janta Meal and standard Meal on trains and at platforms.\n(ii)\t Opening\t of\t Jan\t Aahars\t at\t railway\t stations\t (where\t food\t price\t is\t fixed\t from\t\n` 5 to ` 50).\n(iii) Provision of e-Catering services at railway stations.\n(iv) Provision of variety of regional cuisines at railway stations.\nVacancies in Railways\n733. sHRI susHI l  KuMAR guPTA: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be \npleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that there is a large number of vacancies in Railways;\n\n644 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\n(b) if so, the total number of employees in Railways at present as compared \nto 2014-15; and\n(c)\t the\t steps\t that\t have\t been\t taken\t to\t fill\t up\t these\t vacant\t posts\t so\t that\t efficiency\t\nof Railways does not suffer?\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) and (b) The vacancies of non-gazetted staff against sanctioned strength \nin Railways as on 01.04.2018 were 2,61,270. Total number of employees in Railways \nwere 12,48,101 as on 01.04.2018 as compared to 13,05,449 as on 01.04.2015.\n(c) Arising and filling up of vacancies is a continuous process. Railway \nRecruitment Boards (RRBs) are regularly providing panels of successful candidates to \nRailways/Production units concerned. during the last three years, a total of 66,682 \ncandidates (including 57,851 for safety category posts) have been empanelled for \nvarious group ‘C’ posts on Indian Railways through RRBs. \nThree\t Centralized\t Employment\t Notifications\t (CENs)\t for\t filling\t up\t vacancies\t\nmainly in safety categories were issued in 2018:–\n(i) CeN No.01/2018 for Asst. loco Pilots (AlPs) and Technicians. (Total 64,371 \nvacancies). The 1st stage Computer Based Test has been conducted from \n09.08.2018 to 04.09.2018 in multiple shifts.\n(ii) CeN No.02/2018 for 62,907 vacancies in level-1 (erstwhile group ‘d’) posts. \nComputer Based Test has been conducted from 17.09.2018 to 17.12.2018 in \nmultiple shifts. \n(iii)\tCEN\t No.03/2018\t has\t been\t published\t on\t 29.12.2018\t for\t filling\t up\t of\t 13,487\t\nvacancies for the posts of Junior engineers and others. The last date for \nonline registration was 31.01.2019.\nAccidents at Unmanned Level Crossings\n734. sHRI dHIRAJ PRAsAd sAHu: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be \npleased to state:\n(a) whether government is taken any steps to avert accidents at unmanned level \ncrossings; and \n(b) whether government is contemplating to install any equipment/device to \navoid such incidents in the near future? \nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) and (b) Indian Railways (IR) have been taken proactive measures to \n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 645\nensure safety and prevent accidents at unmanned level Crossings (uMlCs) in the \nform of elimination through closure/merger/grade-separator/manning etc. on Broad \ngauge (Bg) network in a mission mode. As on date, all the unmanned level crossings \non Broad gauge (Bg) have been eliminated on IR.\nE-catering service of IRCTC\n735.\t SHRI\t MD.\t NADIMUL\t HAQUE:\t Will\t the\t Minister\t of\t RAILW AYS\tbe\t\npleased to state:\n(a) the details of the expenditure incurred by IRCTC on the e-catering service \nvis-a-vis the revenue in the last three years;\n(b) the details of the passengers who ordered food from the IRCTC e-catering \nservice vis-a-vis the target in the last three years; and \n(c) the steps taken by government to improve the IRCTC e-catering service?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJeN \ngoHAIN): (a) and (b) The details of the expenditure incurred by IRCTC on e-catering \nservices vis-a-vis the revenue generated through e-catering and number of passengers \nopting for e-catering services in the last three years vis-a-vis the target are given \nin statement (See below).\n(c) The steps taken by the government to improve e-catering services are as \nunder:–\n(i) e-catering services have been extended to all 408 A1 and A category \nstations over Indian Railways.\n(ii) Provision of a user-friendly mobile version of website for booking of \nmeals through e-catering has been made.\n(iii) facility to book meals through Mobile App. (food-on-Track) or by \ncalling number 1323 is now available.\n\n646 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\nStatement\nDetails of the expenditure incurred by IRCTC on e-catering and the number of \npassengers opting for e-catering services in the last three years\n(A) details of expenditure incurred by IRCTC on e-catering vis-a-vis the revenue \nduring the period 2015-16, 2016-17, 2017-18 and 2018-19 (upto 31.12.2018)\nHead 2015-16 2016-17 2017-18 2018-19\n(upto \n31.12.2018)\nexpenditure incurred by \nIRCTC on e-catering \n(in Rupees)\n1,29,62,930.00 2,89,16,273.00 3,90,91,703.00 3,07,44,129.00\nRevenue received by \nIRCTC through e-catering \n(in Rupees)\n7,02,976.00 1,22,42,666.00 3,47,21,856.00 2,32,93,650.00\n(B) details of targets set by IRCTC and the number of orders booked (average \nper day) through e-catering during the period 2015-16, 2016-17, 2017-18 and 2018-19 \n(upto 31.12.2018).\nTarget 2015-16 2016-17 2017-18 2018-19 \n(upto 31.12.2018)\nTarget set for number of order \nbookings through e-catering \nNil 3,500 15,000 10,376\nTarget achieved in number of \norder bookings through e-catering \n790 4,273 5,188 10,632\nInstances\t of\t fire\t in\t Kolkata\t Metro\n736. dR. sANTANu seN: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be pleased to state:\n(a)\t whether\t it\t is\t a\t fact\t that\t there\t have\t been\t several\t instances\t of\t fires\t in\t Kolkata\t\nMetro in the last few months;\n(b) if so, the details of causes revealed in inquiries conducted; and\n(c) whether there is any plan for modernisation of Kolkata Metro?\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \nGOHAIN):\t (a)\t and\t (b)\t There\t is\t one\t instance\t of\t fire\tin\t Metro\t Railway,\tKolkata\t in\t the\t\nrear bogie of the driving coach of uP train No. Kd 191 (AC 1) between Rabindra \n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 647\nsadan and Maidan Railway stations on the up line in the underground section at \nabout\t 17.02\t hrs.\t on\t 27.12.2018.\t As\t per\t provisional\t findings\t of\t Commissioner\t of\t\nRailway\t Safety\t (CRS)/Eastern\t Circle,\t Kolkata,\t the\t fire\t was\t caused\t by\t short\t circuit\t\nbetween\t ‘Third\t Rail\t Current\t Collector’\t (TRCC)\t with\tAxle\t box\t bracket\t resulting\t in\t fire\t\ndue\t to\t which\t rubber\t air\t spring\t also\t got\t fire.\t Error\t of\t design\t of\t TRCC,\t inadequate\t\nprotection\t of\t TRCC\t and\t cables\t up\t to\t junction\t box\t and\t placement\t of\t flammable\t part\t\n(air spring) close to TRCC are the cause of the short circuit.\n(c) New rolling stocks are being added replacing over aged rolling stock. New \nrolling stock shall have TRCC away from the rubber air spring. fuse unit shall be \nplaced on the TRCC to enhance protected zone between high speed circuit breaker \n(HsCB) and TRCC. v ideo monitoring and surveillance system shall be provided in \nthe existing AC rake of dC propulsion and all upcoming new rakes of 03 phase \npropulsion shall have similar provision. w ork shall be carried out for detection of \nfire\t in\t the\t 15\t kilometer\t tunnel\t and\t detection\t of\t fire\t and\t smoke\t at\t the\t 24\t stations\t\nwith integration to the central control.\nRailway line from Hajipur to Mahua\n737. sHRI RAM NATH THAKuR: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be pleased \nto state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that government has recently approved samastipur \nrailway line from Hajipur to Mahua;\n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the work of Mahua railway line has been started; and\n(d) the progress made so far in Mahua railway block and by when this railway \nline would be started, along with the details thereof?\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) No, sir. At present, Hajipur-Mahua is not a sanctioned railway project. \nHowever,\t a\t Reconnaissance\t Engg-cum-Traffic\t Survey\t (RETS)\t for\t new\t line\t between\t\nBhagwanpur and samastipur via Mahua, Karpurigram, Tajpur (60 km.) has been \ntaken\t up.\t Sanction/materialisation\t of\t this\t project\t depends\t upon\t its\t financial\t viability\t\nprojected in survey report.\n(b) to (d) do not arise.\n\n648 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\nUnmanned Level Crossings \n738. sHRI RIPuN BoRA: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be pleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that government has failed to stop accidents in the \nunmanned level crossings;\n(b) if so, the total number of unmanned level crossings in the country and \nnumber of accidents that have occurred at unmanned level crossings since 2014, \nyear-wise;\n(c) the action that has been taken to remove unmanned level crossings during \nthe\t last\t five\t years,\t State-wise;\t\n(d) the reasons why there is vacancy of staff in the unmanned railway crossings \ntill date; and\n(e)\t the\t details\t of\t action\t taken\t along\t with\t flyovers\t constructed\t and\t budget\t\nsanctioned\t for\t flyovers\t since\t 2014\t till\t date?\t\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) and (b) Indian Railways (IR) have taken proactive measures to ensure \nsafety and prevent accidents at unmanned level Crossings (uMlCs) in the form \nof elimination through closure/merger/grade separator/manning etc. on Broad gauge \n(Bg) network in a mission mode. As on date, there are no uMlCs on Bg network \non IR. Railway zone-wise details of accidents occurred at uMlCs since 2014-15 \nto 2018-19 (upto 31st January, 2019) are as below which shows that accidents at \nUMLCs\t have\t reduced\t significantly:–\nNumber of Accidents\nzonal Railway 2014-15 2015-16 2016-17 2017-18 2018-19\n(upto \n31.01.2019)\n1 2 3 4 5 6\nCentral 0 0 0 0 0\neast Coast 2 0 0 0 0\neast Central 5 0 4 2 1\neastern 0 0 1 0 0\nNorth Central 1 1 1 2 0\nNorth eastern 6 5 2 1 1\nNortheast frontier 0 2 3 0 0\n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 649\n1 2 3 4 5 6\nNorthern 9 9 4 3 0\nNorth w estern 8 7 2 1 0\nsouth Central 2 0 0 0 0\nsouth east Central 1 0 0 0 0\nsouth eastern 2 2 1 0 0\nsouthern 2 0 0 0 0\nsouth w estern 5 1 2 0 0\nw est Central 1 0 0 0 0\nw estern 6 2 0 1 0\nKonkan Railway \nCorporation \n0 0 0 0 0\nMetro 0 0 0 0 0\nToT al 50 29 20 10 3\n(c) state-wise details of elimination of unmanned level crossings during the last \nfive\t years\t is\t as\t given\t below:–\nstate 2015-16 2016-17 2017-18 2018-19\n(upto 31.01.2019)\n  1 2 3 4 5\nAndhra Pradesh 70 119 103 171\nAssam 35 84 69 72\nBihar 44 76 66 535\nChhattisgarh 28 17 12 3\ndelhi 0 0 0 1\ngujarat 67 80 154 635\nHaryana 43 68 44 48\nHimachal Pradesh 0 0 2 0\nJammu and Kashmir 0 0 0 1\nJharkhand 40 66 82 34\nKarnataka 88 46 75 179\nKerala 2 1 7 0\nMadhya Pradesh 63 40 43 2\n\n650 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\n  1 2 3 4 5\nMaharashtra 75 66 37 39\nodisha 134 151 95 125\nPuducherry 0 0 0 2\nPunjab 41 60 107 228\nRajasthan 75 90 160 312\nTamil Nadu 96 123 152 184\nTelangana 35 34 29 9\nTripura 8 0 0 0\nuttar Pradesh 170 214 200 729\nuttarakhand 6 9 13 23\nw est Bengal 133 159 115 147\nToT al 1253 1503 1565 3479\nAs on date, there are no unmanned level crossings on Bg network on IR. \n(d) does not arise. \n(e) Road over Bridges (RoBs) and Road under Bridges (RuBs) are constructed \nunder Plan Head 30. The detail of RoBs and RuBs constructed and fund allotted \nfor them since 2014 are as follows:–\nyear RoBs/RuBs \nconstructed\nBudget allocated  \n(` in crore)\n2014-15 1108 1740\n2015-16 1024 2144\n2016-17 1354 3066\n2017-18 1395 4000\n2018-19 (upto december, 2018) 904 4637\nEscalation of project cost due to delay\n739. sHRI NARANBHAI J. RATHw A: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be \npleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that due to delay in timely completion of railway projects, \ncost of projects is increasing; \n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 651\n(b) if so, as per present status, in how many projects cost has escalated due to \ndelay in timely completion of project and amount of cost which has been escalated, \nproject-wise; and\n(c)\t whether\t any\t railway\t officer\t has\t been\t found\t responsible\t for\t above-mentioned\t\ndelay and the response of government in this regard?\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) to (c) At present, 505 Railway projects including 185 New lines, \n57 gauge Conversion and 263 doubling Projects are in different stages of planning/\nsanction/execution. The completion of railway projects requires acquisition of land, \nshifting of utilities, various statutory clearances etc. leading to time and cost overrun. \nfurther, Railways cannot be considered responsible for delay and cost escalation \ndue to slow progress of projects, as many of these factors involve participation of \ndepartments/agencies other than Railways.\nDelay in operationalisation of Lucknow-Delhi Tejas Express\n740. sHRI sANJA y  seTH: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be pleased to    \nstate:\n(a) the reasons for non-operationalisation of the lucknow-delhi Tejas express \npromised by government in the Railway budget of 2016-17 till date;\n(b) the anticipated date by which the train would begin operations; and\n(c) the details of the fare of the Tejas express, as decided by the Railways?\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) and (b) during the Railway Budget 2016-17, the then Hon’ble Minister \nof Railways announced the introduction of four new type of train services which \ninter alia included Tejas express. subsequent to this announcement, it was decided to \nintroduce 12585/12586 lucknow Jn.-New delhi Tejas express. The operationalisation \nof 12585/12586 lucknow Jn.-New delhi Tejas express was pending due to non-\navailability of rake. As of now, the train is ready for introduction and earnest efforts \nare being made to introduce the train at the earliest.\n(c) The basic fare of AC Chair car and executive class of Tejas express is \n1.20 times of the basic fare of respective classes of shatabdi express trains.\n\n652 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\nVacancies in Railways\n741. CH. suKHRAM sINgH yAdAv : \n  sHRI vIsHAMBHAR PRAsAd NIsHAd:  \n  sHRIMATI CHHA yA veRMA:\nw ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be pleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that the number of new recruitments have decreased as \ncompared to retirements in Railways, following which posts in the Railways have \nbeen continuously falling vacant;\n(b) the details of group d, C and other employees who have retired from the \nRailways\t during\t the\t last\t five\t years,\t group-wise;\n(c) the details of employees appointed on various posts during the period, group-\nwise; and\n(d) the number of sanctioned posts which are vacant at present and the reasons \ntherefor?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJeN \ngo HAIN): (a) to (c) The number of employees retired from Railways and the number \nof candidates empanelled on Railways for group ‘C’ and level-1 posts (erstwhile \nGroup\t ‘D’\t post)\t through\t open\t market\t during\t the\t last\t five\t years,\t group-wise,\t is\t as\t\nunder:–\nyear Number of employees retired Number of candidates empanelled\ngroup ‘C’ level-1 Total group ‘C’ level-1 Total\n2013-14 54,519 5,384 59,903 31,805 31,650 63,455\n2014-15 54,771 4,393 59,164 15,191 31,995 47,186\n2015-16 50,205 2,637 52,842 27,995 51,808 79,803\n2016-17 52,282 5,923 58,205 19,587 6,731 26,318\n2017-18 47,842 3,684 51,526 19,100 5,362 24,462\n(d) for group ‘C’ and level-1 posts, as on 01.12.2018, total number of  \nsanctioned posts over zonal Railways which are vacant is 2,74,254 (Provisional). \nOccurrence\t and\t filling\t up\t of\t vacancies\t is\t a\t continuous\t process\t and\t the\t same\t are\t\nfilled\t by\t timely\t placement\t of\t indents\t with\t recruitment\t agencies\t as\t per\t operational\t\nrequirements. Recruitment against 1,51,548 vacancies in various categories is in \nprocess as on date.\n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 653\nConcerns about Bullet Train Project\n742. sHRI KAPIl  sIBAl: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be pleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that Japan wants more bullet train routes across India, \nif so, the details thereof;\n(b) whether it is also a fact that sAIl  has been discarded for supplying the \nrail for the bullet train project, if so, the details thereof; and\n(c) whether the Japanese are concerned about the different work culture between \nthe two countries, if so, the details thereof and the planning of government to solve it?\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) No formal proposal from government of Japan for High speed \nCorridor except for the ongoing Mumbai-Ahmedabad High speed Rail (MAHsR) \nproject is received in this Ministry. \n(b) The rail needed for the Mumbai-Ahmedabad High speed Rail (MAHsR) \nproject is not being produced in India.\n(c) No, sir.\nRunning Allowance to Loco Staffs and Guards\n743. sHRI elAMARAM KARee M: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be pleased \nto state:\n(a) the running allowance given to loco staffs and guards before and after \nseventh Pay Commission;\n(b) the reasons why Railways is not increasing the running allowance of loco \nrunning staffs and guards even after repeated protests and representations by the \nemployees;\n(c) whether government is aware that the running staff retirees of Railways are \ndenied parity in pension; and\n(d) if so, the reasons therefor and whether government would take action to \nimplement pension parity?\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) At present loco staff and guards are being allowed Running Allowance \nat\t the\t rates\t fixed\t prior\t to\t notification\t of\t Seventh\t Central\t Pay\t Commission.\t After\t\nimplementation of seventh Central Pay Commission, revision of Running Allowance \nis under process.\n\n654 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\n(b) Kilometerage Allowance and other related allowances of the Running staff \nis to be decided through bilateral discussions with the federations and further with \nthe concurrence of the Ministry of finance. Accordingly, a proposal was approved by \nthe Competent Authority and has been sent to Ministry of finance for concurrence.\n(c) and (d) The pension of Running staff retirees has been revised as per the \ninstructions of department of Pension and Pensioners’ w elfare (doP&Pw), the nodal \ndepartment on pensionary matters. Ministry of Railways, being an administrative \nMinistry, follows the instructions issued by the nodal department, i.e. doP&Pw  on \nall the issues of pension.\nReduction in dynamic fares in Suvidha trains\n744. sHRI T. g. veNKATesH: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be pleased \nto state: \n(a) whether g overnment is aware that the dynamic fares introduced in the \nsuvidha trains are very high and so passengers are unable to bear such exorbitant \nrates;\n(b) whether government has received any representation and proposals to reduce \nthe dynamic fares, if so, the details thereof; and\n(c) the steps being taken by Railways to reduce the fares?\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) suvidha specials are introduced during peak demand period on variable \nfare structures for reserved class. The fare structures of suvidha trains are as under:–\n(i) unreserved second class: Normal superfast Mail/express fares.\n(ii) Reserved Class: Minimum fare is Tatkal fare for initial 20% of the berths \nand thereafter the fare increases for subsequent slabs of 20% of seats/berths \nbooked subject to maximum three times of the Tatkal fare.\nHowever, alternative trains on normal fare structures are already running on most \nof the routes of suvidha trains.\n(b) and (c) Representations/suggestions on review of certain policies are received \nregularly at various levels like Ministry of Railways, zonal Railways, division, \nstation level, online etc. The representations/suggestions are examined and action as \nfound\t feasible\t and\t justified\t is\t taken.\t At\t present,\t there\t is\t no\t proposal\t to\t reduce\t the\t\nfare structure of suvidha trains. \nHowever, refund provisions of suvidha trains have been rationalised and normal \nrefund rules have been made applicable for suvidha trains also.\n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 655\nWork on Rewa-Sidhi-Churhat Railway Project\n745. sHRI AJA y  PRATAP sINgH: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be pleased \nto state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the work on Rewa-sidhi-Churhat project in Madhya \nPradesh is in progress;\n(b)\t if\t so,\t the\t detailed\t information\t of\t the\t administrative\t and\t financial\t expenditure\t\nincurred on the project; and \n(c) the current status of the work completed on this project and by when it is \ntargeted to be completed? \nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) to (c) Construction of new line from Rewa-Churhat-sidhi (87 km.) \nsection has been taken up as a part of lalitpur-satna, Rewa-singrauli and Mahoba-\nKhajuraho (541 km.) new line project. An expenditure of ` 1603.89 crore has been \nincurred in the entire project upto March, 2018 and an outlay of ` 315 crore has \nbeen provided for the year 2018-19.\nearthwork, bridges work, ballast supply, construction of Road over Bridge/Road \nunder Bridge, station buiding etc. in Rewa-sidhi section are in different stages of \nexecution.\nCompletion of the project is dependent upon many factors such as land acquisition, \nshifting of services, cutting of trees etc. since many of these factors are beyond \nthe control of Ministry of Railways, timeline for completion of the project is not \nfeasible\t to\t be\t fixed.\nUnmanned Level Crossings\n746. dR. AsHoK BAJPAI: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be pleased to state:\n(a) whether as on date, government has completed the work of eliminating all \nthe unmanned level crossings;\n(b) if so, the details of unmanned level crossings eliminated during the last \nthree years, state-wise; and\n(c) if not, the number of unmanned level crossings still existing in the country, \nstate-wise?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJeN \ngoHAIN): (a) Railways have decided to eliminate unmanned level crossings (uMlCs) \nfrom Broad gauge (Bg) track across the country and accordingly work of eliminating \n\n656 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\nuMlCs on Bg has been completed. so far as elimination of uMlCs on Meter \ngauge (Mg) and Narrow gauge (Ng) is concerned, it has been planned to eliminate \nthem during gauge conversion.\n(b) state-wise details of elimination of uMlCs on Broad gauge during the last \nthree years and the current year (upto 31st January, 2019) is as follows:–\nsl. \nNo.\nstate 2015-16 2016-17 2017-18 2018-19\n(upto 31 Jan'19)\n1. Andhra Pradesh 70 119 103 171\n2. Assam 35 84 69 72\n3. Bihar 44 76 66 535\n4. Chhattisgarh 28 17 12 3\n5. delhi 0 0 0 1\n6. gujarat 67 80 154 635\n7. Haryana 43 68 44 48\n8. Himachal Pradesh 0 0 2 0\n9. Jammu and Kashmir 0 0 0 1\n10. Jharkhand 40 66 82 34\n11. Karnataka 88 46 75 179\n12. Kerala 2 1 7 0\n13. Madhya Pradesh 63 40 43 2\n14. Maharashtra 75 66 37 39\n15. odisha 134 151 95 125\n16. Puducherry 0 0 0 2\n17. Punjab 41 60 107 228\n18. Rajasthan 75 90 160 312\n19. Tamil Nadu 96 123 152 184\n20. Telangana 35 34 29 9\n21. Tripura 8 0 0 0\n22. uttar Pradesh 170 214 200 729\n23. uttarakhand 6 9 13 23\n24. w est Bengal 133 159 115 147\nToT al 1253 1503 1565 3479\n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 657\n(c) As on date, no uMlC on Broad gauge exists in the country. state-wise \nposition of uMlCs on Mg and Ng is not available. zonal Railway-wise position \nof uMlCs on Mg and Ng as on 31.01.2019 is as under:–\nsl.No. zonal Railway Mg Ng\n1. Central Railway 0 104\n2. eastern Railway 0 0\n3. east Central Railway 0 0\n4. east Coast Railway 0 0\n5. Northern Railway 0 7\n6. North Central Railway 0 195\n7. North eastern Railway 49 0\n8. North east frontier Railway 0 163\n9. North w estern Railway 100 0\n10. southern Railway 0 0\n11. south Central Railway 0 0\n12. south eastern Railway 0 0\n13. south east Central Railway  0 61\n14. south w estern Railway 0 0\n15. w estern Railway 194 169\n16. w est Central Railway 0 0\n ToT al 343 699\nBuilding wall to guard railway tracks\n747. sHRI Jose K. MANI: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be pleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that the government has planned to build 3000 km. wall \nto guard tracks and keep away people from railway lines in residential areas; and\n(b) if so, by when the project would start and by when it would be completed?\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) and (b) Ministry of Railways has planned to build about 3000 km. \nwall to guard tracks from cattle crossing and keep away people from railway line \nin residential area. The works have already started; completion of the same depends \nupon availability of funds in coming years.\n\n658 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\nNew rail lines in Haryana and Punjab\n748. dR. KANw AR deeP sINgH: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be pleased \nto state:\n(a) the total number of railway projects announced for Haryana and Punjab for \nlaying new rail lines during the last three years and the current year and the details \nthereof;\n(b) whether the work has been initiated on these projects;\n(c) if so, the details thereof; and\n(d) if not, the reasons therefor?\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) Railway projects are not sanctioned state-wise. However, Panipat-Meerut \n(104 km.) and Rajpura-Mohali (24 km.), 2 new line projects have been announced \nin\t the\t Budget\t in\t the\t last\t 3\t financial\t years\t including\t current\t financial\t year\t 2018-19,\t\nwhich are falling fully/partly in the states of Haryana and Punjab.\n(b) to (d) detailed Project Report for Panipat-Meerut new line project has been \ntaken up. government of Punjab has been requested for cost sharing of Rajpura- \nMohali new line project. Consent is awaited.\nTechnology to prevent rail accidents\n749. dR. sAsIKAl A PusHPA RAMAsw AMy: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays  \nbe pleased to state:\n(a) whether government has put in place various technologies to avert rail \naccidents caused due to derailment, collision, sAdP etc.;\n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether government has made any assessment regarding the effectiveness \nof a particular technology for using it in Indian Railways (IR) on pan-India basis \nto prevent accidents;\n(d) if so, the details thereof; and\n(e) if not, the reasons therefor?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJeN \ngoHAIN): (a) and (b) yes, sir. safety is accorded the highest priority by Indian \nRailways and all possible steps are undertaken on a continual basis to prevent \n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 659\naccidents and to enhance safety of passengers. following improvement in techniques/\ntechnology have been adopted to prevent accidents:–\n1. All electric locomotives are equipped with v igilance Control device \n(vCd) to ensure alertness of loco Pilots.\n2.\t Provision\t of\t Retro-reflective\t sigma\t boards\t on\t the\t mast\t which\t is\t located\t\ntwo\t OHE\t masts\t prior\t to\t the\t signals\t in\t electrified\t territories\t to\t warn\t the\t\ncrew about signal ahead when visibility is low due to foggy weather. \n3. Provision of gPs based fog Pass device to loco pilots in fog affected \nareas which enables loco pilots to know the exact distance of the \napproaching landmarks like signals, level crossing gates etc. \n4. Train Protection and w arning system (TPws) based on european \nTechnology eTCs level-1, a proven Automatic Train Protection (ATP) \nsystem to avoid train accident/collision on account of human error of \nsignal Passing at danger (sPAd) or over speeding, has been provided \non Noapara-Kavi subhash section of Kolkata Metro (28 RKMs), Chennai-\ngumidipundi suburban section of southern Railway (50 RKMs), Basin \nBridge - Arrakkonam section of southern Railway (67 RKMs) and Hazrat \nNizamuddin-Agra section of Northern/North Central Railway (200 RKMs)\n5. Auxiliary w arning system (A ws) is presently functional on 413 RKMs \nin the Mumbai suburban section of Central Railway (289 RKMs) and \nw estern Railway (124 RKMs)\n6. Train Collision Avoidance system (TCAs): It is an Automatic Train \nProtection (ATP) system being developed in association with 3 Indian \nmanufacturers. The system has been installed on lingampalli-v ikarabad-\nw adi, v ikarabad-Bidar section (250 RKMs) on south Central Railway. \n7. Mobile Train Radio Communication, a secure means of mobile train \ncommunication, has been deployed over 2541 RKMs.\n8. Total 85,577 nos. of w alkie-Talkie sets have been provided to Train \ndriver and guard for safe train operation, total 72,740 nos. of w alkie- \nTalkie sets have been provided to Maintenance staff and Total 8616 nos. \nof 25 watt w alkie-Talkie sets have been provided to station Masters for \ncommunication with driver and guard. \n9. emergency phone/sockets have been provided along railway track at a \nspan of one Km each for emergency communication by driver with \nRailway control, in case of any emergency. \n\n660 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\n10. Indian Railways has already adopted the technological upgradation in \nsafety\t aspects\t of\t coaches\t and\t wagons\t by\t way\t of\t introducing\t Modified\t\nCentre Buffer couplers, Bogie Mounted Air Brake system (BMB s), \nimproved\t suspension\t design\t and\t provision\t of\t Automatic\t fire\t and\t smoke\t\ndetection system in coaches.\n11. use of long Rail panels on track to minimize welded joints.\n12.\t Ultrasonic\t Flaw\t Detection\t (USFD)\t testing\t of\t rails\t to\t detect\t flaws\t and\t\ntimely removal of defective rails.\n13. Procurement of Thick w eb switches and w eldable CMs Crossing for \nuse on track. \n14. Mechanization of track maintenance to reduce human errors. \n(c) to (e) yes, sir. All the safety equipments are provided based on the trial \nreports and requirement depending upon the geography of the area.\nEnhancing average speed of trains\n750. sHRI NARA yAN lAl  PANCHARIy A: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays \nbe pleased to state:\n(a) whether government has taken any step to increase the average speed of \ntrains;\n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;\n(c) whether government has taken any step to upgrade railway tracks to enable \nrunning of trains at high speed;\n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and\n(e) the details regarding cost implications involved in increasing average speed \nof trains?\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) and (b) yes, sir. w ith a view to increasing speed of trains in Indian \nRailways, ‘Mission Raftaar’ has been announced in the Railway Budget 2016-17. \nThe mission envisages a target of doubling of average speed of freight trains \nand increasing the average speed of all non-suburban passenger trains by 25 kmph \nin next 5 years. \nAction Plan for improving mobility and increasing average speed includes \nremoval of speed restrictions, construction of road over bridges (RoBs) and road \n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 661\nunder bridges (RuBs), right powering of trains, introduction of twin-pipe in wagons \nand replacement of conventional trains by Main line electric Multiple unit (MeMu) \ntrains and diesel electric Multiple unit (deMu) trains.\n(c)\t and\t (d)\t Running\t of\t high\t speed\t trains\t is\t decided\t on\t specific\t routes\t of\t Indian\t\nRailways,\t after\t conducting\t necessary\t trials.\t Required\t improvements\t in\t the\t field\t of\t\ntrack, signal and Traction are carried out as per trial reports.\n(e) since Mission Raftaar is a ‘mission’ and not a ‘project’, precise capital \nexpenditure for development of the railway infrastructure and capacity expansion \nwhich would lead to attainment of the goals and objectives of Mission Raftaar has \nnot\t been\t quantified.\nEstablishment of Primary Health Centres at Railway Stations\n751. sHRI d. KuPe NdRA Reddy : w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be \npleased to state:\n(a) whether the Railways had proposed to set up Primary Health Centres at \nabout 7000 railway stations in the country;\n(b) if so, the details thereof and the time by which these Primary Health Centres \nwould be established at the railway stations; and\n(c) the names of railway stations wherein the said centres have been proposed \nto be set up?\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) No, sir.\n(b) and (c) do not arise.\nIntroduction of new categories of trains\n752. dR. l. HANuMANTHAIAH: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be pleased \nto state:\n(a) whether the Central government has announced four new categories of trains \nto facilitate rail passengers;\n(b) if so, the details thereof;\n(c) whether all the said categories of trains have started as per the schedule \nprepared by government;\n(d) if so, the details thereof; and\n\n662 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\n(e) if not, the reasons therefor and the steps being taken to expedite the \nintroduction of the said trains to facilitate passengers?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI \nRAJeN goHAIN): (a) and (b) yes, sir. during the Railway Budget 2016-17, the \nthen Hon’ble Minister of Railways announced introduction of four new categories of \ntrains services to facilitate passengers:- Antyodaya express, Humsafar express, Tejas \nexpress and udA y  express.\n(c) to (e) All the 4 types of services have been operationalized. At present, 11 \npairs of Antyodaya express, 29 pairs of Humsafar express, 1 pair of Tejas express \nand 1 pair of udA y  express are plying over Indian Railways. Besides, introduction \nof such type of train services is an ongoing process on Indian Railways subject \nto availability of resources, availability of necessary sanction of Commissioner of \nRailway safety, etc.\nRobbery in Jammu-Delhi Duronto Express\n753. sHRIMATI wANsuK syIeM: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be pleased \nto state:\n(a) whether in a shocking incident, robbers struck at dawn, looting train passengers \nof delhi-bound Jammu duronto express at knife-point near samaypur Badli in the \nNational Capital Region;\n(b) whether the loot occurred along the track running through JJ Colonies known \nfor the acts of vandalism and theft in the running trains; and\n(c) whether the gangs place coins on the track between two signal system, to \ndisrupt insulation that causes short circuit, making signal systems go into default \nmode (red) thereby forcing the train to stop?\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) yes, sir. \n(b) The railway track, where the said incident of robbery reported, is surrounded \nby JJ colonies on both sides. \n(c) The signal system was thoroughly checked and it was found functioning \nproperly. data logger report showed that one track circuit bobbed from 3:23:59 to \n3:24:01 and again from 3:24:25 to 3:34:06. It is suspected that it is a case of outside \ninterference with Track Circuit. \n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 663\nClosure of printing presses under Railways\n754. sHRI BINoy  vI sw AM: w ill the Minister of RAI lw Ays be pleased to state:\n(a) whether government has closed any printing press under Railways in the \npast;\n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor;\n(c) the total number of printing presses presently functioning under Railways; \nand\n(d) the details of wages and number of casual and permanent workers who are \npresently employed in the printing presses under Railways?\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) yes, sir.\n(b) 9 printing presses located at fairly Place/Kolkata/eastern Railway, lucknow/\nNorthern Railway, gorakhpur/North eastern Railway, garden Reach and Kharagpur/\nsouth eastern Railway (02 printing presses), Ajmer/North w estern Railway, \nThiruchirapalli/southern Railway, Kurseong/North east frontier Railway and Mahalaxmi/\nw estern Railway have been closed by 31st July, 2018.\nThese 9 printing presses were old printing presses engaged in printing of card \ntickets and general documents using the old technology. due to introduction of \nunreserved Ticketing system, Automatic Ticket vending Machines and digitization \nof\t various\t documents,\t running\t of\t these\t printing\t presses\t was\t not\t justified.\n(c) 5 printing presses are presently functioning under Railways.\n(d) The wages paid to the workers who are presently employed in the 05 printing \npresses are ` 7,04,10,137 per month (as for the month of december, 2018) Number \nof casual workers presently employed in the printing presses under Railways is: \nNil Number of permanent workers presently employed in the printing presses under \nRailways is: 1196 \nSpecial train from Ayodhya to Sitamarhi\n755. sHRI PRABHAT JHA: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Ministry has received any proposal to run a special train from \nAyodhya to sitamarhi; \n(b) if so, the details thereof;\n\n664 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\n(c) whether a decision on the said proposal has been taken/would be taken soon; \nand \n(d) if so, the details thereof?\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) to (d) Request from the Hon’ble Member of Parliament has been \nreceived for introduction of trains between Ayodhya to sitamarhi. The same has \nbeen examined but not found operationally feasible, at present. However, presently \n14017/14018 sadbhavna express is available for the passengers desirous of travelling \nbetween Ayodhya and sitamarhi. \nProblems faced by passengers stranded in Delhi due to \nnon-availability of seats in trains\n756.\t SHRI\t AHMAD\t ASHFAQUE\t KARIM:\t Will\t the\t Minister\t of\t RAILW AYS\t\nbe pleased to state:\n(a) whether government has any scheme to ease the condition of Indian citizens \nwho are compelled to stay in delhi for several days due to non-availability of \nreservation in trains, thereby spending more money than their paying capacity; and\n(b) if so, the detailed information thereof? \nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) and (b) The demand for reserved accommodation on Indian Railways \nis not uniform throughout the year and varies from time to time as well as train to \ntrain. during peak rush period, the demand outstrips the availability whereas during \nthe lean period the accommodation is generally available in most of the t rains. \nIn Air Conditioned class, since only limited number of berths/seats are available, \nduring peak rush periods, such as summer/winter vacations, festival season etc., the \naccommodation in these classes gets exhausted within a short time. However, during \nthe lean period accommodation remains available for much longer period in all \nclasses including Air-Conditioned classes. Trains running on popular and convenient \ntimings with lesser stoppages and running time are generally well patronized and also \nexperience waiting list almost throughout the year. However, other trains not having \nthese features are chosen by passengers for travel only as a second alternative and \nare, therefore, not fully patronized. \nHowever, with a view to meet the additional demand, Railways take various \nmeasures from time to time some of which are listed below:–\n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 665\n(i) w aiting list position of trains is monitored on regular basis and wherever \nfeasible as well as required, load of the trains is augmented, special trains are \nrun, new trains are introduced subject to operational feasibility, commercial \njustification,\t etc.\n(ii) To facilitate those passengers who have to undertake journey at short notice \nand to save such passengers from the clutches of unscrupulous elements, \nTatkal scheme of reservation is available where the accommodation becomes \navailable for booking on the previous day of journey from train originating \nstation. \n(iii) Further,\t with\t a\t view\t to\t provide\t confirmed\t accommodation\t to\t waiting\t list\t\npassengers and to ensure optimal utilisation of available accommodation, \na scheme known as Alternate Train Accommodation (ATAs) known as \n“vIKAlP” has been introduced. for this facility, waiting list passenger has \nto give an option at the time of booking of ticket and passengers with \nwaiting\t list\t status\t at\t the\t time\t of\t preparation\t of\t first\t reservation\t charts\t are\t\nshifted to other trains, subject to availability of vacant accommodation.\nTaking\t such\t measures\t to\t facilitate\t passengers\t in\t getting\t confirmed\t reserved\t\naccommodation is a continuous and ongoing measure. \nSteps to maintain punctuality of trains in \nSouthern Railway\n757. sHRI v. MuRAleedHARAN: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be pleased \nto state:\n(a) the situation regarding maintaining punctuality by the trains especially in \nsouthern Railway; \n(b) whether government has received complaints of late running of trains in \nKerala; and\n(c)\t if\t so,\t the\t reasons\t for\t that\t and\t the\t steps\t that\t have\t been\t initiated\t to\t find\t a\t\nsolution to this issue?\nTHe  MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): (a) The improved punctuality (%) performance of southern Railway for  \nthe last three months and comparison with corresponding period of last year is as \nunder:–\n\n666 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\nMonth 2017-18 2018-19\nNov. 75.62 82.80\ndec. 75.09 80.68\nJan. 74.63 82.82\n(b) and (c) yes, sir. Complaints of late running of trains in Kerala are received \nat various levels i.e. station, division, zonal Railway and Railway Board. In order \nto improve punctuality on southern Railway, numerous steps have been taken:–\n(i) Timings of certain Mail/express trains have been rearranged and \nrationalized, which has improved punctuality of trains to some extent. \n(ii) Twenty pairs of express trains in Thiruvananthapurm division are provided \nwith w AP7 locomotives which have better acceleration/deceleration \ncharacteristics. \n(iii) shadow corridor blocks are being used to grant maximum corridor blocks \nto avoid repeated blocks. \n(iv) Continuous efforts are being made to improve asset reliability to reduce \nfailures to bare minimum level, so that detentions to trains are minimized.\nRailway projects in Madhya Pradesh\n758. dR. sATy ANARAyAN JATIy A: w ill the Minister of RAIlw Ays be \npleased to state details of expansion of new passenger train services and rail tracks, \nelectrification\twork\t and\t details\t of\t significant\tpassenger\t services\t introduced\t in\t Madhya\t\nPradesh during last four years as well as details of forthcoming special railway projects?\nTHe MINIsT e R of sTAT e  IN THe MINIsTR y  of RAIlw Ays (sHRI RAJe N \ngoHAIN): Indian Railways is administratively divided into zonal Railways whose \njurisdiction straddles across state boundaries. However, details of train services \nintroduced\t serving\t station\t located\t in\t Madhya\t Pradesh,\t railway\t electrification\t projects\t\nand new lines falling partially/fully in the state of Madhya Pradesh, during last four \nyears (i.e. 2014-15 to 2017-18) and in the current year (i.e.2018-19), are as under:– \n(i) New services:–\nyear Number of trains (in single)\n2014-15 18\n2015-16 03\n2016-17 24\n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 667\nyear Number of trains (in single)\n2017-18 12\n2018-19* 30\n *As on 05.02.2019\n(ii)\t Railway\t electrification\t projects:–\t\nyear Name of the section Route Kilometer\n2014-15 Nil NIl\n2015-16 singrauli-Katni 260\nRatlam-Nimach-Chanderia-Kota 348\nIndore-Mhow 21\nJhansi-Manikpur Incl. Khairar-Bhimsen 409\n2016-17 gondia-Nainpur-Jabalpur 229\nv ijaipur-Maksi 188\nChhindwara-Nainpur-Mandala fort 183\n2017-18 Birlanagar-etawah 115\nguna-gwalior 227\nRatlam-fatehabad-laxmibainagar 115\n2018-19 Mahoba-udaipura 196\nTumsar Road-Tirodi 20\nBalaghat-Katangi 47\nTirodi-Katangi 15\nMhow-Khandwa 138\n(iii) New lines:–\nsl. \nNo.\nNew line Project year of \ninclusion \nin Budget\nlatest \nCost \nexpend as \non March \n2018\nTotal \noutlay \n2018-19 \nstatus of Project\n1. Indore-Budhni \n(Jabalpur) (342 Km)\n2016-17 3261.82 0.15 0.2 Indore-Budhni new line \nhas been sanctioned in \nseptember, 2018 at a \ncost of ` 3261.82 crore\n2. Indore-Manmad via \nMalegaon (358 Km)\n2016-17 8857.98 - 101 Project has been taken \nup under special \nPurpose v ehicle (sPv).\n3. Nimach-Badi sadri \n(48 Km)\n2017-18 490.44 0.05 5 Project sanctioned in \noctober, 2018.\n\n668 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\nEstablishment of High Courts or their Benches in State Capitals\n759. sHRI v. MuRAleedHARAN: w ill the Minister of lA w  ANd JusTICe \nbe pleased to state:\n(a) the states which do not have a High Court in the state Capital; and\n(b) whether there is a proposal before government to establish High Courts or \ntheir Benches in state capitals where there is none?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of lA w  ANd JusTICe \n(sHRI P.P. CHAudHAR y): (a) The states of Kerala, gujarat, uttarakhand, odisha, \nChhattisgarh, Assam and Madhya Pradesh do not have a Principal seat or Bench of \nHigh Court in the respective state capitals. \n(b) No sir. There is no such proposal.\nUnified\t Model\t Recruitment\t Rules\t for\t Statistical\t Cadre\n760. sHRI sANTIuse KuJuR: w ill the Minister of sTATIsTICs ANd  \nPRogRAMMe IMPleMeNTATIoN be pleased to state:\n(a) the progress of 7 th Central Pay Commission recommendation with regard to \nstreamlining the \"statistical Cadre\"' existing in various departments of the Central \nGovernment\t under\t Unified\t Model\t Recruitment\t Rules,\t laying\t down\t the\t educational\t\nqualification,\t job\t responsibilities\t and\t pay\t structure\t of\t the\t Statistical\t Cadre\t Posts,\t as\t\nper para 75 of Chapter 7.7 of CPC report;\n(b) whether such type of recommendation has been made, if so, the details of \nthe current status thereof; and\n(c) if not, the reasons therefor?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of sTATIsTICs ANd  \nPRogRAMMe IMPleMeNTATIoN (sHRI vIJA y  goel): (a) and (b) 7th Central \nPay Commission in its report, vide  para 7.7.75, inter alia recommended that\" there \nis a need to streamline the statistical Cadres existing in various departments under \nthe\t Central\t Government\t under\t unified\t Model\t Recruitment\t Rules\t laying\t down\t the\t\neducational\t qualifications,\t job,\t responsibilities\t and\t pay\t structure\t of\t the\t Statistical\t\nCadre posts. This will ensure uniformity and remove avoidable anomalies in pay \nstructure amongst similar/identical statistical Cadre posts in various departments. \nThe Commission accordingly recommends that the government may look into this \naspect in its entirety.\"\n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 669\nsubordinate statistical service (sss) is a group 'B' organised service of statistical \nofficers\t under\t the\t cadre\t control\t of\t this\t Ministry.\tAs\t a\t part\t of\t Cadre\t Review\t process\t\nof sss, this Ministry has already requested all the Ministries/departments/organisations \nin the government of India to convey the requirements of statistical function posts \nfor consideration of possible en-cadrement in sss. Thus, the government in Ministry \nof statistics and Programme Implementation is looking holistically into the aspect \nof streamlining the \"statistical Cadre\" existing in various departments of the Central \ngovernment.\n(c) does not arise.\nIncrease in GDP of the country\n761. sHRIMATI KANTA KARdAM: w ill the Minister of sTATIsTICs ANd \nPRogRAMMe IMPleMeNTATIoN be pleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that India ranks seventh among countries having highest \ngross domestic Product (gdP) and if so, the details thereof;\n(b) whether any modalities have been drawn to further strengthen country’s \nposition;\n(c) whether government plans to work towards further increasing the gdP of \nthe country; and\n(d) if so, the steps taken so far in this regard?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of sTATIsTICs ANd  \nPRogRAMMe IMPleMeNTATIoN (sHRI vIJA y  goel): (a) Comparative position \nof India with respect to other countries is not maintained in the Ministry. As per the \nInternational Monetary fund’s (IMf’s) w orld economic outlook Report (october-2018), \nIndia is the world’s seventh-largest economy in us $ among 193 countries considered \nin the report. This ranking is based on projections made by International Monetary \nfund (IMf), for gdP (Nominal) Ranking for the year 2018.\n(b) Reforms in the Indian economy is a continuing process and various Ministries \nand departments are implementing government’s strategic programmes and policies \nto enhance the economic growth. NITI Aayog has specially been constituted for \nproviding both directional and policy inputs and to enhance cooperative federalism \nby fostering the involvement of state governments in the economic policy-making \nprocess using a bottom-up approach. Its initiatives include \"15-year road map\",  \n\"7 year vision, strategy and action plan\".\n\n670 Written Ans. to Std. and... [RAJyA sABHA] ...the 8 February, 2019\n(c) and (d) Promoting growth of the economy has always been the utmost priority \nof the government. The government is continuously taking steps to facilitate production \nand gdP growth of the economy. Key initiatives taken, inter alia, include ‘Make in \nIndia’\t initiative\t under\t which\t thrust\t sectors\t have\t been\t identified\t to\t provide\t a\t push\t to\t\nmanufacturing in India, ‘startup India’ initiative and ‘ease of doing Business’. The \ngovernment aims at creating a conducive environment by streamlining the existing \nregulations and processes and eliminating unnecessary requirements and procedures. \nFurther,\t Foreign\t Direct\t Investment\t (FDI)\t policy\t and\t procedures\t have\t been\t simplified\t\nand liberalized progressively. other measures include special package for textile industry, \nconcrete measures for transport sector including measures for regional connectivity \nand power sector via schemes like ujjwal dIsCoM Assurance yojana (udA y). \nThe\t introduction\t of\t the\t Goods\t and\t Services\t Tax\t (GST)\t has\t provided\t a\t significant\t\nopportunity to improve growth momentum by reducing barriers to trade, business \nand\t related\t economic\t activities.\t The\t Union\t Cabinet\t approved\t a\t significant\t increase\t\nin the Minimum support Prices (MsPs) for all Kharif and Rabi crops for 2018-19 \nseason, in order to provide major boost to farmer’s income. The government has \nlaunched a phased programme for bank recapitalization entailing infusion of capital \nto the public sector banks, to the tune of about `\t 2.11\t lakh\t crore\t over\t two\t financial\t\nyears, which is expected to encourage banks to enhance lending.\nBudget 2018-19 included various measures to provide a push to the economy, \nwhich among others, include major push to infrastructure via higher allocation to \nrail and road sector, reduced corporate tax rate for companies with turnover up to \n` 250 crore to help MsMes, etc. The government launched a support and outreach \nprogramme for helping the growth, expansion and facilitation Micro, small and \nMedium enterprises (MsMe) sector. several initiatives have been undertaken to boost \nthe MsMe sector including improving access to credit, wider access to markets, \ntechnological upgradation of MsMe through hub and spoke model etc.\nIn\t addition,\t efficient\t financial\t intermediation,\t macroeconomic\t stability\t through\t\nprudent\t fiscal\t and\t monetary\t policies\t are\t other\t efforts\t initiated\t by\t the\t Government\t\nto increase the gdP of the country.\nCost overrun of Infrastructure Projects\n762. sHRIMATI AMBIKA soNI: \n  dR. T. suBBARAMI Reddy :\nw ill the Minister of sTATIsTICs ANd PRogRAMMe IMPleMeNTATIoN \nbe pleased to state:\n(a) the number of infrastructure projects, each worth ` 150 crore or above, \nwhere cost overruns took place, as on January, 2019;\n\nWritten Ans. to Std. and... [11 february, 2019]   ...the 8 February, 2019 671\n(b) the reasons for the cost overruns.\n(c) the anticipated completion cost, as against the original cost, both in terms \nof\t figure\t and\t percentage;\n(d) the number of projects where time escalation is noticed and their period of \ntargeted completion; and\n(e) the steps taken by government to remove the bottlenecks and putting these \ninfrastructure projects on the fast track?\nTHe MINIsTeR of sTATe IN THe MINIsTR y  of sTATIsTICs ANd  \nPRogRAMMe IMPleMeNTATIoN (sHRI vIJA y  goel): (a) The Ministry \nof statistics and Programme Implementation monitors on-going Central sector \nInfrastructure Projects costing ` 150 crore and above on time and cost overruns, \non the basis of information provided by the project implementing agencies. As on \n01.12.2018, a total of 1443 projects were on the monitor of this Ministry. of these, \n347 projects are showing cost overruns.\n(b)\t The\t causes\t for\t cost\t overruns\t are\t project-specific,\t depend\t on\t a\t variety\t of\t\ntechnical,\t financial\t and\t administrative\t factors,\t and\t differ\t from\t project\t to\t project.\t\nHowever, as reported by the project implementing agencies on this Ministry’s oCMs, \nthe main reasons for cost overrun of the Projects are: law and order problems, delay \nin land acquisition, delay in environment and forest clearances, funding constraints, \nrehabilitation and resettlement issues, local body/municipal permissions, utility shifting, \ncontractual issues, etc.\n(c)\t The\t details\t of\t such\t projects\t are\t available\t in\t the\t flash\t report\t at\t http://www.\ncspm.gov.in/english/publication.html.\n(d) As on 01.12.2018, out of total 1443 projects, 360 are running behind their \nschedule\t of\t completion.\t The\t details\t of\t such\t delay\t projects\t are\t available\t in\t the\t flash\t\nreport at http://www.cspm.gov.in/english/publication.html.\n(e) The major steps undertaken to ensure completion of Central sector Infrastructure \nProjects without time and cost overruns include; Periodic review of projects under \nPRAgATI through video conferencing; rigorous project appraisal; on-line Computerized \nMonitoring system (oCMs) for better monitoring; setting up of Revised Cost \nCommittees\t in\t the\t Ministries\t for\t fixation\tof\t responsibility\t for\t time\t and\t cost\t overruns;\t\nregular review of infrastructure projects by the concerned administrative Ministries; \nand setting up of Central sector Projects Coordination Committees (CsPCCs) in the \nstates under the Chief secretaries for removal of bottlenecks and for facilitating the \nspeedy implementation of major projects.\n————\n\n672 Announcement by [RAJyA sABHA] the Chair\nANNOUNCEMENT BY THE CHAIR\nsHRI deReK o’BRIeN (w est Bengal): sir,…\nMR. dePuTy  CHAIRMAN: first, an important information.\nHon. Members, I have to inform the House that a function to unveil the portrait \nof Bharat Ratna shri Atal Bihari vajpayee, the former Prime Minister of India, will \nbe held tomorrow at 10.00 a.m. in the Central Hall, Parliament House. The hon. \nPresident of India would be unveiling the portrait and addressing the distinguished \ngathering. A formal invite for hon. Members has also been extended through Bulletin \nPart II. Members are requested to attend the function and be seated in Central Hall \nby 9:45 a.m. Now, Motion of Thanks on the President’s Address. \nsHRI ANANd sHARMA (Himachal Pradesh): sir,…(Interruptions)..\nsHRI deReK o’BRIeN: sir,…(Interruptions)..\nsHRI suKHeNdu seKHAR RA y  (w est Bengal): sir,…(Interruptions)..\nMR. dePuTy  CHAIRMAN: further discussion on the following Motion moved \nby shri Bhupender yadav on 6th february, 2019:–\n ‘‘That an Address be presented to the President in the following terms:–\n ‘That the Members of the Rajya sabha assembled in this session are \ndeeply grateful to the President for the Address which he has been \npleased to deliver to both Houses of Parliament assembled together on \nJanuary 31, 2019.’’ \n…(Interruptions)...\n Hon. Members, we will now take up further discussion on the Motion \nof Thanks on the President's Address which was moved on 6th february, 2019. \n...(Interruptions)… As you are aware, the Business Advisory Committee has allotted 10 \nhours’ time for this discussion, and it was to be taken up on 4th and 5th february, \n2019. …(Interruptions).. unfortunately, no Business could be taken up in the House \nduring the entire last week. …(Interruptions)...\nsHRI ANANd sHARMA: sir, I have a point of order. …(Interruptions)...\nsHRI Nee RAJ sHe KHAR ( uttar Pradesh): sir, I have a point of order. \n...(Interruptions)...\nsHRI suKHe Ndu seKHAR RA y: sir, I have a point of order. …(Interruptions)... \n\nAnnouncement by [11 february, 2019]  the Chair 673\nMR. dePuTy  CHAIRMAN: w e are now left with only two-and-a-half days \nduring which we have to not only discuss the Motion of Thanks but also have to \ndo\t financial\t Business\t which\t includes\t discussions\t on\t Interim\t Budget,\t Appropriation\t\nBill and finance Bill, apart from consideration and passing of some government \nBills to which there was a consensus in the last meeting of the Business Advisory \nCommittee. ...(Interruptions)...\nsHRI deReK o’BRIeN: sir, ...(Interruptions)...\nsHRI suKHeNdu seKHAR RA y : sir, ...(Interruptions)...\nMR. dePuTy  CHAIRMAN: so, I propose that we resume the discussion now. \n...(Interruptions)...\nsHRI suKHeNdu seKHAR RA y : what is this? …(Interruptions)..\nMR. dePuTy  CHAIRMAN: when the House adjourned on the 7th\n february, \n2019, shri Bhupender yadav who had moved the Motion of Thanks had not concluded \nhis speech. …(Interruptions)... I now, therefore, call upon shri Bhupender yadav to \ncontinue his speech. …(Interruptions)... Please. …(Interruptions)...\nsHRI ANANd sHARMA: sir, I have a point of order. …(Interruptions)...\nsHRI BHuPeNdeR yAdAv  (Rajasthan): Hon’ble deputy Chairman sir, on the \nAddress of the Honourable President, I …(Interruptions)... \nMR dePuTy  CHAIRMAN: Hon’ble Members, I would like to make an appeal \nto you once again that let’s to discuss only those subjects which have been decided \nby\t the\t Business\t Advisory\t Committee\t and\t have\t already\t figured\t in\t today’s\t business.\t\nlet’s discuss on the Address by the Hon’ble President. …(Interruptions)... Nothing \nelse will go on record. …(Interruptions)... you may express your views and raise \nyour questions within this It is our constitutional responsibility and an essential part \nof glorious parliamentary tradition. …(Interruptions)... first let the House be in order. \n...(Interruptions)... It is our duty to uphold this tradition. Therefore, I appeal to you \nto go back to your seats and let the House function smoothly. …(Interruptions)... \nThe House is not in order. …(Interruptions)... Please go back to your seats. …\n(Interruptions)... The Hon’ble Chairman has made it clear in the morning that he \nhas disallowed the Notices he got on certain subjects, therefore the Chair cannot \nallow discussion on those issues. At the same time, there are some issues being \nraised about which no notices had been given, nor any prior information nor the \npermission of the Hon’ble Chairman. Therefore, we cannot allow these issues to be \nraised in the House.\n\nThe Chair has been repeatedly appealing to you to go back to your seats and \ncreate a conducive atmosphere for discussion on the President’s Address and it does \nso even now, but going by the way you enter the well of the House and create \nrules, it appears that you do not want to discuss the subjects decided by the Business \nAdvisory Committee. This is very unfortunate.\nI once again appeal to you to go back to your seats and allow the discussion \non the President’s Address to continue consistent with the constitutional sanctity and \nlet the Motion of Thanks be passed. Please go back to your seats. …(Interruptions)... \nlet the House be in order. …(Interruptions)... Anandji, I can hear only when …\n(Interruptions)... The House is adjourned till 11.00 hrs on Tuesday, the 12th february, \n2019. \nThe House then adjourned at four minutes past \ntwo of the clock till eleven of the clock on \nTuesday, the 12th February, 2019.\n674 Announcement by [RAJyA sABHA] the Chair\n[Mr deputy Chairman]\n\nPublished  under  r ule  260 of  r ules  of  Procedure  and  c onduct  of  b usiness  in \nthe  c ouncil  of  sta tes (r ajy a sabha ) and  Printed  by  DRV Grafix  Print\n41, institutional  a rea , d -b lock , janak Puri , n ew  d elhi -110058"
